window.BEYNGLAB_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"/","title":"How do worlds open?","type":"Home","description":"BeyngLab maps the ways a world becomes perceptible through language, place, technics, art, ritual, and practice.","keywords":["world disclosure","D0","Ereignis","Heidegger","regional dasein"],"text":"A studio for thinking with Heidegger and beyond How do worlds open? BeyngLab maps the ways a world becomes perceptible through language, place, technics, art, ritual, and practice. Follow Heidegger’s turns, explore regional grammars of disclosure, and try exercises that begin with attunement. Enter the Atlas ↗ Trace the Turns ↗ the opening language place technics practice FIG. 01 / WORLD DISCLOSURE D0 / THE INTERVAL Pause before the map. What is already acting through this situation? This is an invitation to notice, not a scored exercise. Begin anywhere. Seven entrances / one field of questions Choose an opening. Each entrance makes a different cut through the same unsettled terrain. 01 / FIELD ◌ Atlas of Regional Daseins Six revisable grammars of disclosure, each with its affordance and danger. ↗ 02 / TIME ↝ Turns Labyrinth Early, middle, and late Heidegger as recurring questions with crossings. ↗ 03 / CONCEPT ✳ Topoi Topiary Care, world, thing, and technics as connected problem spaces, not dictionary entries. ↗ 04 / MAKE ⌗ Sixfold Make a pop fourfold, then follow a thing through the worlds it gathers. ↗ 05 / VOICES ✦ Hot House Living readers, makers, and critics who keep the argument open. ↗ 06 / ARCHIVE ⌁ Pete’s Corner A passage to Pete Ferreira’s Ereignis archive and its community memory. ↗ 07 / CARE Breakdown When a world stops holding: crisis, therapeutic encounters, and making cures together. Arguments you can look through Three plates to begin with. Each diagram has a complete text reading and a visible risk. PLATE 01 Three Heideggers Helyx Coils, not stages ↗ PLATE 02 Regional Daseins Atlas Overlapping grammars ↗ PLATE 03 Sufi Heidegger after Corbin A contested reception field ↗ The first pathway Follow an image into a question. Corbin’s imaginal opens a route through hermeneutics, Islamicate reception, film, and art. The links are carefully named: historical reception, scholarly comparison, or a BeyngLab resonance. Walk the Sufi Heidegger path ↗ WORKSHOP / 30 MINUTES Ta’wil Portal Bring an image, object, or place. Describe what is visible, what world it suggests, and what remains withheld before proposing an intervention. Try the exercise ↗ A NOTE ON METHOD “Regional” names a grammar of disclosure, not a bounded people, nation, or cultural essence. Several grammars may meet in one situation; those involved can contest and revise our terms. Heidegger is a point of departure here, never the measure of every world."},{"url":"/atlas/","title":"Atlas of Regional Daseins","type":"Atlas","description":"Six overlapping grammars of disclosure, each shown with a possibility, a danger, and a way to correct the reading.","keywords":["Corbin","Tanabe","Escobar","Stiegler","Hui","Jullien","imaginal","metanoetic","pluriversal","mythic","pharmacological","cosmotechnical"],"text":"01 / FIELD Atlas of Regional Daseins Ways a world takes shape. A region here is an operation of disclosure. It can travel across places, coexist with other operations, and change when people challenge our description. These six cards are working lenses, not identities to assign to cultures. READING RULE / 01 Read across each card: an operation, what it lets us notice, and how that operation can be captured. Use more than one card for a situation; ask the people involved where this atlas misses them. Six working grammars The field, with its risks. A1 / CORBIN Imaginal Operation: Corbin names a mode of perception and an order of reality; BeyngLab asks how an image mediates disclosure in a particular encounter. Possibility Read the depth and mediation of an image. Danger Angelic abstraction can erase history and material conditions. Read the full grammar ↗ A2 / TANABE Metanoetic Operation: failure, repentance, and other-power interrupt self-sufficient mastery. Possibility Make room for a turn after impasse. Danger Moral absolutism can convert reflection into guilt capture. Working card · detail in development A3 / ESCOBAR Pluriversal Operation: territory, relation, and autonomy foreground many ways of worlding and their design consequences. Possibility Let situated partners shape the question. Danger Romantic localism can picture “the local” as innocent or static. Working card · detail in development A4 / CONTESTED FIELD Mythic–Traditionalist Operation: myth, initiation, gods, and origin diagnose a hunger for belonging and depth. Possibility Examine how myth gives form to experience. Danger Mythopolitical hierarchy, ethnos, and enemy-making. This is a diagnosis, not an endorsed doctrine. Working card · detail in development A5 / STIEGLER Pharmacological Operation: technics and memory shape attention, care, and individuation. Possibility Critique media and AI through their effects on attention. Danger Therapeutic technocracy can manage care as a system. Read the full grammar ↗ A6 / HUI · JULLIEN Cosmotechnical Operation: technodiversity and detour unsettle the idea that technics has one universal path. Possibility Ask how different worlds shape technical choices. Danger Comparative essentialism can freeze traditions into opposites. Working card · detail in development D0 / BEFORE INTERVENTION The interval before the design. Stop long enough to notice what already matters. “Non-thought” and “non-action” are working names for refusing an automatic solution; they do not forbid thinking or action. “Perfumance” and “infinihil” are the report’s experimental language, held here as prompts rather than settled concepts. Pause Attune World Dwell D0 is a method across the site, not a seventh region, therapeutic score, or required gateway. Provenance Experimental These cards synthesize the AIR V report into editable design lenses. The named thinkers orient research; this short atlas does not stand in for their texts. For Heidegger and technology, read The Question Concerning Technology at Ereignis."},{"url":"/atlas/#metanoetic","title":"Metanoetic / Tanabe","type":"Atlas","description":"Failure, repentance, and other-power interrupt mastery; test the risk of moral absolutism.","keywords":["Tanabe","metanoia","Kyoto"],"text":"Failure, repentance, and other-power interrupt mastery; test the risk of moral absolutism."},{"url":"/atlas/#pluriversal","title":"Pluriversal / Escobar","type":"Atlas","description":"Territory, relation, and autonomy invite situated worlding; test the risk of romantic localism.","keywords":["Escobar","pluriverse","design"],"text":"Territory, relation, and autonomy invite situated worlding; test the risk of romantic localism."},{"url":"/atlas/#mythic-traditionalist","title":"Mythic–Traditionalist / contested","type":"Atlas","description":"A diagnostic lens for myth, origin, and belonging, with hierarchy and enemy-making kept in view.","keywords":["mythopolitics","traditionalism","danger"],"text":"A diagnostic lens for myth, origin, and belonging, with hierarchy and enemy-making kept in view."},{"url":"/atlas/#pharmacological","title":"Pharmacological / Stiegler","type":"Atlas","description":"Technics and memory shape attention and care; test the risk of therapeutic technocracy.","keywords":["Stiegler","technology","AI","care"],"text":"Technics and memory shape attention and care; test the risk of therapeutic technocracy."},{"url":"/atlas/#cosmotechnical","title":"Cosmotechnical / Hui and Jullien","type":"Atlas","description":"Technodiversity and detour unsettle one universal path for technics; test comparative essentialism.","keywords":["Yuk Hui","François Jullien","technodiversity"],"text":"Technodiversity and detour unsettle one universal path for technics; test comparative essentialism."},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/","title":"Imaginal","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Corbin’s imaginal as a situated disclosure grammar, tested through Bayzāʾī’s Travelers and a Kiarostami film resonance, with sources and counter-readings.","keywords":["Henry Corbin","mundus imaginalis","ta'wil","Suhrawardi","cognitive imagination","Bayzai","Travelers","mirror","Mansoor Behnam","Kiarostami","The Wind Will Carry Us","offscreen","regional disclosure"],"text":"A1 / ATLAS GRAMMAR A revisable operation of disclosure Imaginal When does an image disclose a world—and who gets to say so? Henry Corbin used “imaginal” for an order of reality with its own mode of perception in the traditions he studied. BeyngLab borrows a question about mediation. This grammar cannot identify a people, certify an experience, or turn every striking image into an instance of the imaginal. Read the grammar ↘ ON THIS PAGE 01 / Operation 02 / Historical hinge 03 / Worked encounter 04 / Kiarostami 05 / Capture and correction 06 / Try and revise 07 / Source ledger 01 / Operation What this lens lets us ask. The imaginal is Corbin’s claim, with its own religious and philosophical stakes. These three moves form a BeyngLab reading protocol. They do not establish that any particular artwork belongs to an imaginal realm. [1] [4] 01 Attend to form Describe the image, its material, setting, maker, and the way it reaches you before declaring what it means. 02 Ask how it appears What kind of attention or interpretive practice lets this image matter? Corbin’s cognitive imagination is a specific philosophical claim, not a synonym for creativity. [1] 03 Return to a world Name the people, histories, institutions, and disagreements that the reading depends on. Test whether the image opens more than your first account can hold. 02 / Historical hinge Keep the routes distinct. Reception Corbin encountered Heidegger’s hermeneutics and completed a French translation of What Is Metaphysics? in the 1930s. His sustained work on Suhrawardī followed a distinct intellectual and textual path. A reception history traces these contacts without treating one tradition as the hidden content of another. [2] Corbin’s claim In “Mundus Imaginalis,” Corbin distinguishes the imaginal from the merely unreal or fanciful. He writes of a mode of perception corresponding to an order of reality, drawing on the “eighth clime” and visionary accounts. That ontological claim belongs to Corbin and the traditions as he interprets them. [1] BeyngLab’s move Here the transferable question is narrower: what does an image make perceptible in this encounter, by what mediation, and at what cost? This is an editorial comparison. It is not a practice of ta’wil, a theological proof, or a definition of Islamicate thought. [4] Boundary. “Regional” identifies this lens’s way of disclosing, not a fixed trait of Persia, Islam, or anyone living there. Corbin’s own broad geography is a historical interpretation to examine, never a map of cultural essence. [2] 03 / Worked encounter · plot details The mirror in Travelers Bahrām Bayzāʾī, Musāfirān (Travelers), 1991 · reading with Mansoor Behnam An heirloom mirror is carried toward a wedding; after a fatal accident, the mirror is missing. The grandmother holds to the promise of its arrival. Mansoor Behnam reads the film’s mirror and its return through hope, absence, presence, and imaginal mediation. [3] What the film gives A particular mirror, a disrupted journey, a family preparing a wedding and then a funeral, and a staging of reflected light. Begin with these choices of object, performance, and film form. What the lens proposes The mirror can be read as a threshold at which the absent remain active in the family’s world. This is Behnam’s scholarly interpretation and a BeyngLab comparison; neither establishes that Bayzāʾī adopted Corbin’s philosophy. What pushes back The accident, women’s actions, mourning practices, the material heirloom, and the film’s relation to taʿzīyah resist being dissolved into a free-floating spiritual symbol. Revised claim In this film, the mirror’s movement changes how kinship, death, and hope can appear together. Return to a specific sequence and let another reading alter that sentence. Interpretive Read the film and Behnam’s argument together. This example tests the lens; it does not confer a “Sufi” identity on Bayzāʾī or his film. 04 / Film resonance · plot details Kiarostami, at the edge of the frame Abbas Kiarostami, The Wind Will Carry Us, 1999 · BeyngLab film resonance A television crew travels from Tehran to a Kurdish village to film an anticipated funeral. The elderly woman remains alive and unseen. The crew waits; the film lets other lives and voices continue beyond its frame. [5] What the film gives A woman never shown, crew members heard but not seen, an offscreen digger, and a milkmaid at work in a dark barn. Delay frustrates the visitor’s timetable. Attend to framing, voices, labor, and the crew’s stated purpose before assigning a philosophical meaning. What this lens asks How does a life outside the frame organize what appears within it? This question about cinematic withholding is a BeyngLab experiment. It does not make an offscreen figure an instance of Corbin’s imaginal order. What pushes back Kaveh Akbar reads the audience’s imagining of unseen lives as part of the film’s ethical problem: the crew wants images of death, and viewers can presume to know people they cannot see. Withholding may mark a limit on the camera’s claim, rather than a hidden spiritual world. Revised claim The film’s unseen figures interrupt the visitor’s planned image of the village. Looking and listening become answerable to living people and their work, which do not fit his schedule. Test this claim against a particular sequence. Experimental Akbar’s film reading sharpens the objection [6] ; the comparison with Corbin is BeyngLab’s. Neither a documented influence nor a “Sufi filmmaker” identity is claimed. Compare a Pharmacological reading of the same film ↗ 05 / Capture and correction Where this reading can go wrong. Capture risk What happens Counter-practice Angelic abstraction A luminous reading erases bodies, labor, loss, or political conditions. Keep the material sequence and its social setting beside the symbolic reading. Fixed cultural essence “Iranian” or “Sufi” becomes one timeless way of seeing, assigned to every work or person. Name a text, maker, scene, and interpreter; compare disagreements within the field. Attribution drift A resonant comparison becomes a claim of documented influence or religious identity. Label each relation as reception, scholarly interpretation, or BeyngLab experiment. 06 / Try and revise Take one image through four moves. A ten-minute comparative exercise, not a reproduction of taʾwīl in a living tradition. Choose Bring an image or scene whose maker and context you can identify. Describe Record its form and conditions before proposing what world it opens. Counter-read Ask what it withholds and who would challenge your description. Revise Keep the first claim, the objection, and the changed claim together. Run the 30-minute Ta’wil Portal ↗ Revision protocol / v0.1 · 1 October 2026. A correction should travel with the claim it changes: record the source, the objection, and the revised reading. One situation may call for another Atlas grammar as well. No person or place is assigned to this one. [4] 07 / Source ledger What supports which claim. Primary Henry Corbin, “Mundus Imaginalis, or the Imaginary and the Imaginal,” in Swedenborg and Esoteric Islam, trans. Leonard Fox (West Chester, PA: Swedenborg Foundation, 1995), online excerpt and continuations. Corbin’s distinction between imaginal and imaginary, and his account of cognitive imagination. Historical Daryush Shayegan, “Corbin, Henry,” Encyclopædia Iranica 6, no. 3 (1993): 268–72, updated February 2, 2015. Biographical and intellectual history of Corbin’s Heidegger encounter and Suhrawardī studies. Interpretive Mansoor Behnam, “Travelers (1991),” Cinema Iranica, May 21, 2025. A film-specific reading of the mirror, mourning, women’s roles, and imaginal mediation; it is not evidence of direct Corbin influence. Experimental BeyngLab, AIR V Regional Daseins / Sufi Heidegger report, working site architecture. The grammar, worked comparison, capture risks, and revision protocol are BeyngLab’s editable synthesis. Primary Janus Films, The Wind Will Carry Us, film record (Abbas Kiarostami, Iran, 1999). Distributor record for the crew’s purpose, the woman’s continued life, and several unseen figures. The film itself remains the primary work for close reading. Interpretive Kaveh Akbar, “The Wind Will Carry Us: Dust to Dust,” The Criterion Collection, May 13, 2025. A film-specific account of offscreen lives, labor, and the ethical risk in the documentarian’s and viewer’s gaze. The evidence labels distinguish Corbin’s text, historical account, film"},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/#operation","title":"Imaginal / operation","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Three reading moves: attend to form, ask how an image appears, return to its material and social world.","keywords":["image","cognitive imagination","method"],"text":"Three reading moves: attend to form, ask how an image appears, return to its material and social world."},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/#hinge","title":"Corbin / historical hinge","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Heideggerian hermeneutics, Corbin’s Suhrawardī studies, and BeyngLab’s narrower comparison remain distinct.","keywords":["Heidegger","Suhrawardi","ta'wil"],"text":"Heideggerian hermeneutics, Corbin’s Suhrawardī studies, and BeyngLab’s narrower comparison remain distinct."},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/#encounter","title":"Travelers / mirror reading","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"A worked film encounter with Bayzāʾī’s mirror, Behnam’s interpretation, and a counter-reading.","keywords":["Bayzai","Mansoor Behnam","film","mirror"],"text":"A worked film encounter with Bayzāʾī’s mirror, Behnam’s interpretation, and a counter-reading."},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/#kiarostami","title":"Kiarostami / at the edge of the frame","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"The Wind Will Carry Us: unseen lives, the visiting crew’s gaze, and a BeyngLab imaginal resonance with an ethical counter-reading.","keywords":["Abbas Kiarostami","The Wind Will Carry Us","film","offscreen","Kaveh Akbar"],"text":"The Wind Will Carry Us: unseen lives, the visiting crew’s gaze, and a BeyngLab imaginal resonance with an ethical counter-reading."},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/#capture","title":"Imaginal / capture risks","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Angelic abstraction, fixed cultural essence, and attribution drift with counter-practices.","keywords":["risk","regional Daseins","essentialism"],"text":"Angelic abstraction, fixed cultural essence, and attribution drift with counter-practices."},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/#practice","title":"Imaginal / try and revise","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"A ten-minute image reading exercise with a correction carried forward; links to the full Ta’wil Portal.","keywords":["workshop","revision","objection"],"text":"A ten-minute image reading exercise with a correction carried forward; links to the full Ta’wil Portal."},{"url":"/atlas/imaginal/#sources","title":"Imaginal / source ledger","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Corbin and Shayegan, film records and readings of Bayzāʾī and Kiarostami, and BeyngLab’s experiment separated by evidence status.","keywords":["bibliography","edition","translation"],"text":"Corbin and Shayegan, film records and readings of Bayzāʾī and Kiarostami, and BeyngLab’s experiment separated by evidence status."},{"url":"/atlas/pharmacological/","title":"Pharmacological","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"A sourced Stiegler grammar of technics, memory, attention, and care, tested through Kiarostami’s The Wind Will Carry Us with a counter-reading.","keywords":["Bernard Stiegler","pharmakon","technics","attention","care","external memory","Heidegger","enframing","Kiarostami","The Wind Will Carry Us","mobile phone","cinematic time"],"text":"A5 / ATLAS GRAMMAR A revisable operation of disclosure Pharmacological What does a technique enable, and what care does it put at risk? For Bernard Stiegler, technical supports help form memory, attention, and shared life. Their effects call for a pharmacological critique: the same arrangement may support a capacity and undermine it. BeyngLab uses this lens on one film situation, not as a diagnosis of a people, a device, or an entire culture. Read the grammar ↘ ON THIS PAGE 01 / Operation 02 / Philosophical hinge 03 / Film encounter 04 / Capture and correction 05 / Try and revise 06 / Source ledger 01 / Operation Follow the support and its effects. A pharmakon can carry beneficial and harmful possibilities. Stiegler’s analysis of digital relational technologies puts attention and care at stake; these steps are a BeyngLab protocol for one situated encounter. [1] [7] 01 Locate the support Name the tools, recorded traces, skills, institutions, and people that make this situation possible. Ask who makes and maintains the support. 02 Trace the double effect Which forms of memory, attention, or coordination become possible? Which are shortened, captured, or displaced? The two effects need not balance neatly. [1] 03 Try a counter-practice Ask what participants could change in the use or governance of the support, and who should judge whether that change improves care. [1] 02 / Philosophical hinge Keep the questions distinct. Heidegger In “The Question Concerning Technology,” enframing names a way in which beings are revealed as orderable standing-reserve. This is a question about revealing, not a claim that a phone or camera is itself enframing. [2] Stiegler In “Relational Ecology and the Digital Pharmakon,” Stiegler describes attention as formed through social life and material techniques. He asks how externalized memory and digital relational technologies might capture attention or sustain critical participation. [1] Cinema and this site Stiegler makes cinematic time a subject of a separate book. The present film exercise extends questions from his essay to an analogue camera and a mobile phone in Kiarostami’s fiction. It is BeyngLab’s comparison, not a reading Stiegler made of this film. [3] Boundary. This lens travels across situations. It cannot identify a “technological culture” or rank places by their media. The film pairing is a test of what the questions disclose and conceal, not a history of influence. [7] 03 / Worked encounter · plot details A phone call above the village Abbas Kiarostami, The Wind Will Carry Us, 1999 · hilltop return A crew has come to a Kurdish village to film a funeral rite while an elderly woman remains alive. The visiting filmmaker repeatedly goes uphill for mobile reception, near a worker digging out of sight. Choose one such return for a close reading; this page gives no edition-dependent timecode. [4] [6] What is recorded The phone directs the visitor uphill and connects him to people elsewhere. The planned camera work gives the crew a reason to wait for a death. The digger’s work and voice persist outside the image. Begin with the particular shot and sound you can verify. What the support affords A call can coordinate work at a distance; a camera can preserve and circulate an event. The film itself uses cinematic means to let viewers notice the crew’s impatience. These are possible capacities, not a verdict on every use. What the arrangement costs A production schedule turns the woman’s continued life into a delay for the visitor. The calls repeatedly reorient him toward demands elsewhere. Akbar reads the audience’s wish to picture unseen lives as part of this ethical problem. What resists the diagnosis Brody also reads the film’s means of communication and cinema as possible openings for contact and freedom. The digger’s work, the woman’s life, and local relationships exceed the crew’s plan. Do not make the phone the sole cause of the harm. Revised claim This scene lets several times meet uneasily: a remote deadline, a worksite, a film crew’s anticipation, and a woman’s living time. Test how the phone and camera mediate those relations without turning technics into the whole explanation. Experimental The film analysis draws on Akbar’s and Brody’s differing readings [5] [6] . Stiegler did not make this reading of Kiarostami. Compare it with the Imaginal lens ↗ 04 / Capture and correction Where this reading can go wrong. Capture risk What happens Counter-practice Device scapegoat The phone becomes the villain; the crew’s purpose, employer, and relation to villagers disappear. Describe the entire arrangement and ask which decision or demand directs the device. Managed care A proposed remedy turns attention into a score assigned by an expert. Let the people affected contest both the diagnosis and the proposed change. False equivalence Heidegger’s enframing, Stiegler’s pharmacology, and Kiarostami’s film become one doctrine. Keep each claim beside its source and label the film comparison Experimental. 05 / Try and revise Read a technique with its people. Try these four moves with any well-described situation. For the film, use the guided two-lens exercise. Support List the device, records, institutions, labor, and dependencies in one encounter. Double effect Write one affordance and one cost, with a specific observation for each. Objection Find a use the diagnosis misses, or a person whose account is absent. Revision Change the claim and propose a counter-practice someone could actually contest. Run the Film Lab ↗ Revision protocol / v0.1 · 1 October 2026. Keep the observation, the counter-reading, and the changed claim together. Ask whose labor or care the technical diagnosis overlooked. [7] 06 / Source ledger What supports which claim. Primary Bernard Stiegler, “Relational Ecology and the Digital Pharmakon,” trans. Patrick Crogan, Culture Machine 13 (2012). Stiegler on attention, externalized memory, the pharmakon, and critical uses of digital relational technologies. The essay does not analyze Kiarostami. Primary Martin Heidegger, “The Question Concerning Technology,” in Basic Writings, Ereignis text archive. Enframing and standing-reserve as a mode of revealing. The film comparison does not attribute this vocabulary to Kiarostami. Historical Bernard Stiegler, Technics and Time, 3: Cinematic Time and the Question of Malaise, trans. Stephen Barker (Stanford University Press, 2010), publisher record. Bibliographic context for Stiegler’s separate work on cinematic time; the linked record is not a substitute for a passage from the book. Primary Janus Films, The Wind Will Carry Us, film record (Abbas Kiarostami, Iran, 1999). Distributor record for the crew, village, elderly woman, and unseen figures; close readings should return to the film. Interpretive Kaveh Akbar, “The Wind Will Carry Us: Dust to Dust,” The Criterion Collection, May 13, 2025. Film-specific reading of offscreen lives, labor, and the ethical risk in the visitor’s and viewer’s gaze. Interpretive Richard Brody, “DVD of the Week: The Wind Will Carry Us,” The New Yorker, February 22, 2010. Identifies the hilltop phone calls and unseen digger; offers a different emphasis on the possibilities of cinema and communication. Experimental BeyngLab, AIR V Regional Daseins / Sufi Heidegger report and this Atlas comparison. The three-move operation, film pairing, capture risks, and revision exercise are BeyngLab proposals. The primary texts, distributor record, critics’ readings, and BeyngLab experiment carry different kinds of authority. The film itself remains the work to inspect."},{"url":"/atlas/pharmacological/#operation","title":"Pharmacological / operation","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Locate the technical support, trace its double effect, and propose a counter-practice.","keywords":["Stiegler","pharmakon","attention","technics"],"text":"Locate the technical support, trace its double effect, and propose a counter-practice."},{"url":"/atlas/pharmacological/#hinge","title":"Heidegger and Stiegler / distinct questions","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Enframing as revealing, technical memory and care, and BeyngLab’s film comparison kept distinct.","keywords":["Question Concerning Technology","externalized memory","cinema"],"text":"Enframing as revealing, technical memory and care, and BeyngLab’s film comparison kept distinct."},{"url":"/atlas/pharmacological/#encounter","title":"The Wind Will Carry Us / phone and camera","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"A worked reading of the hilltop call, unseen digger, funeral crew, and competing times.","keywords":["Kiarostami","film","phone","digger"],"text":"A worked reading of the hilltop call, unseen digger, funeral crew, and competing times."},{"url":"/atlas/pharmacological/#capture","title":"Pharmacological / capture risks","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Device scapegoating, managed care, and false philosophical equivalence, each with a correction.","keywords":["risk","therapeutic technocracy","counter-practice"],"text":"Device scapegoating, managed care, and false philosophical equivalence, each with a correction."},{"url":"/atlas/pharmacological/#practice","title":"Pharmacological / try and revise","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Four moves for a technical encounter, with a direct route to the Film Lab.","keywords":["workshop","revision","care"],"text":"Four moves for a technical encounter, with a direct route to the Film Lab."},{"url":"/atlas/pharmacological/#sources","title":"Pharmacological / source ledger","type":"Atlas grammar","description":"Stiegler and Heidegger texts, distributor film record, film critics, and BeyngLab’s experiment identified separately.","keywords":["bibliography","Stiegler","Corbin"],"text":"Stiegler and Heidegger texts, distributor film record, film critics, and BeyngLab’s experiment identified separately."},{"url":"/turns/","title":"Turns Labyrinth","type":"Turns","description":"Three recurring coils in Heidegger's thought: care, the history of Being, and dwelling, each with a danger and an archival entry.","keywords":["Being and Time","Ereignis","Contributions","Building Dwelling Thinking","care","fourfold"],"text":"02 / TIME Turns Labyrinth Three turns, no final stage. These are reading orientations, not a ladder of improvement. Questions about care, history, and dwelling return in altered forms; a later text does not simply erase an earlier one. 01 1927–33 Early / Dasein and care How is a life already involved in a world? In Being and Time , Dasein is being-in-the-world rather than an isolated observer. Care, thrownness, and being-with let us ask how significance is already there before a detached description. Care Worldhood Being-with Watch for A heroic picture of authenticity that forgets dependence and shared worlds. Being and Time (GA 2) ↗ 02 1933–45 Middle / history of Being What if an epoch shapes what can appear? The question turns toward the history of Being, the event of appropriation (Ereignis), and ways an age orders beings. This turn must be read alongside Heidegger’s political entanglement, not insulated from it. Ereignis History Machination Watch for Mythopolitical capture: an epochal story can authorize hierarchy and exclusion. Contributions to Philosophy ↗ 03 1945–76 Late / thing and fourfold How might a thing gather a place? Dwelling, the fourfold, and the thing shift attention toward gathering and letting-be. A built place can be read for how it draws together earth, sky, mortals, and divinities without treating these terms as a design recipe. Thing Fourfold Dwelling Watch for Poetic quietism that turns dwelling into withdrawal from material conflict. Building Dwelling Thinking ↗ A WAY THROUGH Follow Care , World , and Thing across the turns. Gestell and standing-reserve cross the later thinking of technics with those questions; the Three Heideggers plate traces the coils. Reading status Interpretive Periods and danger labels are an editorial map. Primary The three work links lead to texts or tables of contents in Pete Ferreira’s Ereignis archive. Dates mark broad reading arcs, not hard borders."},{"url":"/turns/#early","title":"Early Heidegger / care","type":"Turns","description":"Being and Time: being-in-the-world, care, thrownness, and being-with, with heroic authenticity as a danger.","keywords":["GA 2","worldhood","1927"],"text":"Being and Time: being-in-the-world, care, thrownness, and being-with, with heroic authenticity as a danger."},{"url":"/turns/#middle","title":"Middle Heidegger / Ereignis","type":"Turns","description":"History of Being, event, and machination, read alongside political entanglement and mythopolitical risk.","keywords":["Contributions to Philosophy","1933","1945"],"text":"History of Being, event, and machination, read alongside political entanglement and mythopolitical risk."},{"url":"/turns/#late","title":"Late Heidegger / dwelling","type":"Turns","description":"Thing, fourfold, and dwelling: what a place gathers and what poetic quietism leaves out.","keywords":["Building Dwelling Thinking","earth","sky","mortals","divinities"],"text":"Thing, fourfold, and dwelling: what a place gathers and what poetic quietism leaves out."},{"url":"/topoi/","title":"Topoi Topiary","type":"Topoi","description":"Sixteen connected problem spaces with primary archive passages, interpretive readers, disagreements, and reading practices.","keywords":["care","worldhood","thing","fourfold","dwelling","topos","Gestell","Bestand","standing-reserve","release","releasement","Gelassenheit","nihilism","nothingness","das Nichts","mindfulness","Besinnung"],"text":"03 / CONCEPT Topoi Topiary Concepts grow in relation. A topos is a cultivated problem space. Begin with a question, return to a text, register disagreement, and test what the concept does in practice. Sixteen worked entries connect the early question of care with later questions of technics, art, and event. Performance opens an explicitly marked path for thinking with Heidegger. T1 / EARLY CROSSING Care How are we already involved in a world and open to possibilities? Text. Being and Time articulates Dasein’s being as care (Sorge), distinguished from concern (Besorgen) and solicitude (Fürsorge). Disagreement. Does an account centered on Dasein sufficiently describe dependency and shared forms of care? Danger. The call to own one’s life can be read as heroic self-sufficiency. Counter-practice. Name the people, infrastructures, and histories making a situation possible. StudioLab form. In D0, list three claims the situation makes on you before proposing a design. Cross to Gestell : whose care becomes availability on a schedule? Read the GA 2 contents ↗ Read the full Care Topos T2 / CROSS-TURN World How does a background of significance let anything show up? Text. Worldhood in Being and Time begins from involvement, not a container of objects. Disagreement. What changes when plural ways of worlding are described by their participants, rather than inferred through one philosophical vocabulary? Danger. “A world” can become a sealed cultural unit. Counter-practice. Follow exchanges, translations, technical mediations, and internal disagreements. StudioLab form. Apply two Atlas grammars to one encounter; invite a correction from someone within it. Cross to Gestell : when does orderability become a background of significance? Return to Being and Time ↗ Read the full World Topos T3 / LATE CROSSING Thing / Fourfold What does a thing gather, and who is left outside its gathering? Text. Building Dwelling Thinking asks how building belongs to dwelling. Disagreement. Can the fourfold disclose a place while still accounting for its labor, property, and conflict? Danger. Poetic quietism can beautify a place while ignoring its conditions. Counter-practice. Read the object’s materials, maintenance, access, and contested uses. StudioLab form. Bring one ordinary thing to the Sixfold studio and test its gathering across different worlds. Cross to standing-reserve : what changes when a thing appears chiefly as on-call stock? Read Building Dwelling Thinking ↗ Read the full Thing / Fourfold Topos T4 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Gestell / Standing-Reserve How does what exists come to appear as orderable and on call? Text. GA 7 distinguishes the ordering mode ( Ge-stell ) from what appears as available ( Bestand ); GA 79 reopens the question of positionality and replaceability. Disagreement. What changes across the Bremen lecture, the later essay, and their English translations? Danger. A sweeping diagnosis may erase differences in place, labor, and power. Counter-practice. Keep a primary passage, a reader’s interpretation, and an objection on the same page. BeyngLab form. A four-stop Ereignis trail with Babich, Mitchell, Kisiel, and Wise as distinct interlocutors. Read the full T4 Topos ↗ Read Heidegger at Ereignis ↗ T13 / LETTING AND THINKING Release / Gelassenheit How can thinking let go of mastery without abandoning responsibility? Text. Read the Memorial Address alongside Country Path Conversations, with their different dates, versions, and translations. Disagreement. What can a freer relation to technology change, and what material conditions does it leave unresolved? Danger. Release becomes resignation or another productivity technique. Counter-practice. Keep a concrete obligation and another person’s objection beside the proposal to let go. Readers. Davis, Dreyfus, and Glazebrook, with a further route through Schürmann. Read the full Release Topos T16 / REFLECTION AND QUESTIONING Mindfulness / Besinnung What changes when thinking questions its own assumptions? Text. Follow meditation in GA 65, Besinnung in GA 66, and reflection on science in GA 7. Disagreement. What lets reflective thinking recognize its own blindness? Danger. A critique of other methods exempts itself from correction. Counter-practice. Put one working measure and your own interpretation under question. Readers. Babich, Berger, and Vallega-Neu at Ereignis. Read the full Mindfulness Topos T14 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Nihilism Does making new values overcome nihilism—or continue it? Read devaluation and forgottenness through GA 5, with Pippin, Mitchell, and Fried. Keep the diagnosis situated and its political stakes open to criticism. Read the full Nihilism Topos T15 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Nothingness / das Nichts How can the nothing disclose beings without becoming another being? Read anxiety, worldhood, and What Is Metaphysics? with Withy, Wise, and Carman. Keep the nothing distinct from nihilism and clinical anxiety. Read the full Nothingness / das Nichts Topos T5 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Call / Ruf How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say? Begin with the call of conscience in Being and Time. Follow its unsettling relation to selfhood and care, then ask what kind of response it makes possible. Keep this analysis distinct from later accounts of the call of being. Read the full Call / Ruf Topos T6 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world? Read authenticity through possibility, finitude, and the retrieval of a past. This path asks how a way of existing changes; it offers no personality test and no ranking of people or cultures. Read the full Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit Topos T8 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Dwelling / Wohnen What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain? Follow Building Dwelling Thinking from sparing to gathering and lived space. Keep the poetic account in conversation with questions of access, maintenance, and displacement. Those questions belong to our testing of the text, not to a ready-made housing policy in Heidegger. Read the full Dwelling / Wohnen Topos T9 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Event / Ereignis How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing? Read Ereignis across Contributions to Philosophy and Time and Being. Preserve the difference between the 1930s manuscripts and the 1962 lecture, and keep translation choices visible. “Event” is an entry into a problem, not a synonym for any dramatic occurrence. Read the full Event / Ereignis Topos T7 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Difference Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference? Begin with Identity and Difference: Heidegger’s thinking of belonging-together and his encounter with Hegel. Follow distinct crossings into Derrida’s différance and Deleuze’s difference in itself, while keeping the earlier ontological question and other sources of twentieth-century difference in view. Read the full Difference Topos T12 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Technē What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making? Follow Heidegger’s interpretation of technē through knowing, bringing-forth, and the question of modern technology. Read the Greek inheritance as an argument with a history, not as an uncontested dictionary definition. Read the full Technē Topos T11 / ARCHIVE CROSSING Poiesis How does bringing something forth change what can appear? Read bringing-forth alongside the work of art’s opening of truth. Keep poiēsis, poetry, manufacture, and creative novelty distinct. A new product does not automatically disclose a new world. Read the full Poiesis Topos T10 / THINKING WITH Performance How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts? Read practical involvement with Heidegger, labor and the performance principle with Marcuse, and performativity with Lyotard. The shared question is how ways of doing become demands that organize existence. Their concepts differ; this Topos makes the connections available for testing. Read the full Performance Topos SIXTEEN WORKING ENTRIES / OPEN TO REVISION The initial Topoi garden now has a reading path for every entry. This is a working inventory, not a canon: follow the sources, compare the readers, and keep the experiments open to correction. Reading status Primary The linked Ereignis texts are starting points. Interpretive The disagreements and dangers are editorial questions. Experimenta"},{"url":"/topoi/#call","title":"Call / Ruf / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/#authenticity","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/#dwelling","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/#event","title":"Event / Ereignis / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/#difference","title":"Difference / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/#techne","title":"Technē / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/#poiesis","title":"Poiesis / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/#performance","title":"Performance / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/topoi/#care","title":"Care / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"What already claims us? Read care with dependency, infrastructure, and shared forms of life in view.","keywords":["Sorge","Dasein","D0"],"text":"What already claims us? Read care with dependency, infrastructure, and shared forms of life in view."},{"url":"/topoi/#world","title":"World / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"How a background of significance lets things appear; resist treating a world as a sealed cultural unit.","keywords":["worldhood","disclosure","plural worlds"],"text":"How a background of significance lets things appear; resist treating a world as a sealed cultural unit."},{"url":"/topoi/#nihilism","title":"Nihilism / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"Devaluation, revaluation, and forgottenness of being, with Pippin, Mitchell, and Fried at Ereignis.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Devaluation, revaluation, and forgottenness of being, with Pippin, Mitchell, and Fried at Ereignis."},{"url":"/topoi/#nothingness","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"Anxiety, worldhood, and the nothing, with Withy, Doug Wise, and Carman at Ereignis.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Anxiety, worldhood, and the nothing, with Withy, Doug Wise, and Carman at Ereignis."},{"url":"/topoi/#mindfulness","title":"Mindfulness / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"Besinnung as reflection, meditation, and questioning, with a critical account of blindness.","keywords":["GA 66","attention","science"],"text":"Besinnung as reflection, meditation, and questioning, with a critical account of blindness."},{"url":"/topoi/#release","title":"Release / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"Gelassenheit: releasement, non-willing, and a freer relation to technology.","keywords":["letting","GA 16","GA 77"],"text":"Gelassenheit: releasement, non-willing, and a freer relation to technology."},{"url":"/topoi/#thing","title":"Thing and fourfold / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"What does a thing gather? Read the fourfold with labor, property, maintenance, and contested access.","keywords":["Geviert","dwelling","sixfold"],"text":"What does a thing gather? Read the fourfold with labor, property, maintenance, and contested access."},{"url":"/topoi/#standing-reserve","title":"Gestell and standing-reserve / Topoi","type":"Topoi","description":"An Ereignis trail through GA 7, GA 79, translation choices, readers, disagreement, and counter-practice.","keywords":["Bestand","enframing","positionality","technics"],"text":"An Ereignis trail through GA 7, GA 79, translation choices, readers, disagreement, and counter-practice."},{"url":"/topoi/care/","title":"Care / Sorge","type":"Topos","description":"Care, concern, and solicitude in Being and Time, with primary passages and readings by Sheehan, Babich, and Volpi at Ereignis.","keywords":["care","Sorge","Besorgen","Fürsorge","Fursorge","solicitude","concern","GA 2","Being and Time","Sheehan","Babich","Volpi"],"text":"T1 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path / Being and Time Already involved. Open to possibility. What does Heidegger mean when he calls Dasein’s being “care”? Read Sorge through its relation to world, possibility, and being with others. Follow the primary passages at beyng.com, then ask how their formal account meets bodily need, dependence, and assistance. Begin with the passages FOLLOW CARE 01 / Archive trail 02 / Three related terms 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Primary passages Read the structure and its differences. The page references below distinguish the German standard pagination (SZ), the collected edition (GA 2), and each English edition. Matching page numbers across editions do not necessarily name the same passage. 01 / §41 · GA 2: 255 · SZ 192 Care as a whole Dasein projects possibilities from a situation it already inhabits while engaging with what it encounters. The care structure gathers these dimensions; it does not name one feeling or activity alongside others. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 236 Continue on p. 237 German SZ 192 02 / §26 · SZ 121–122 Concern and solicitude Heidegger distinguishes dealings with things from being with others. Solicitude can take over another’s concern or help the other recover their own possibilities. The passage also names indifferent and deficient ways of being with others. M&R p. 157 / translation note p. 158 / leaping in p. 159 / leaping ahead 03 / §42 · SZ 199–200 Care and ordinary cares The ontological claim concerns the constitution that makes particular cares and forms of dedication possible. It does not generalize from the observation that people often worry. This distinction also limits what one can infer about an ethics of assistance. Stambaugh–Schmidt, p. 192 02 / Translation matters Sorge, Besorgen, Fürsorge. German M&R rendering Reading distinction Sorge Care The unified structure of Dasein’s being. Kindness and anxiety do not exhaust its meaning. Besorgen Concern Ways of dealing with what is encountered within the world. Fürsorge Solicitude Ways of being with others, including taking over, enabling, and indifference. Macquarrie and Robinson flag the loss of the German words’ visible kinship in English at p. 157. Read their note before treating these as three unrelated categories. 03 / Distinct readings at Ereignis Meaning, embodiment, praxis. MEANING Thomas Sheehan In “Heidegger’s Doctrine of Meaning,” the section on Sorge argues for reading it as the existential structure of engagement in meaning. His strong objection to psychological translations gives the familiar English word “care” a problem to answer. Read Sheehan at Ereignis BODY AND NEED Babette Babich “Da-Sein’s Body” brings Günther Anders’ criticism of Heidegger into view: how much does an ostensibly concrete analytic disclose about bodily need and dependence? This challenges the account’s reach without turning Sorge into a synonym for nursing. Read Babich at Ereignis ARISTOTLE AND PRAXIS Franco Volpi “Dasein as Praxis” reads care through Heidegger’s transformation of Aristotelian practical philosophy. Ask what this genealogy changes in an account centered on meaning or selfhood. Read Volpi at Ereignis 04 / Interpretive problem What does the formal account leave open? Sheehan’s reading guards against collapsing an existential structure into a psychological state. Babich’s encounter with Anders presses a different question: can the analytic adequately account for embodied and dependent lives? These emphases form a BeyngLab comparison, not a documented exchange between the authors. Return to §26 before deciding that the text simply neglects others. Return to bodily need before deciding that an ontological distinction settles their claims. Neither the care structure nor the contrast between leaping in and leaping ahead supplies a complete rule for every act of assistance. Risk Correction Care means kindness Distinguish Sorge from particular caring actions, and read the indifferent modes of Fürsorge. Authenticity means self-sufficiency Keep being-with, thrownness, and dependence in the reading. The helper already knows Ask whether assistance displaces another’s possibilities and who can contest the helper’s description. 05 / Reading move Keep three claims apart. 01 Mark the primary claim. Read §41 beside §26. Record what each says about care, concern, and solicitude, with the edition and page. 02 Choose an interpretation. Read Sheehan, Babich, or Volpi. State one argument of the author’s and the passage it changes for you. 03 Form your own question. Ask how assistance can support or displace another’s possibilities. Mark that extension as yours, and keep an objection beside it. 06 / Crossings Care opens onto a world. CONNECTED READING Performance / thinking with Follow the archive trail and test the neighboring question. CONNECTED READING Call / Ruf Follow the question of taking up a possibility. T16 / MINDFULNESS Question the frame. Examine the assumptions that shape what counts and what matters. T2 / WORLD What already matters? Care’s possibilities arise within a meaningful world. T4 / GESTELL When does involvement become availability? A proposed crossing between early and later questions; Sorge and Bestand remain distinct. TURNS / EARLY Return to being-in-the-world. Read care within the early analytic of Dasein. HOT HOUSE / READER Follow Babich’s wider work. Carry the question of embodiment into another reading. Experimental “How does care become organized as availability?” is BeyngLab’s cross-Topos question. It proposes a comparison; it does not describe a demonstrated historical derivation of Gestell from Sorge. 07 / Sources and editions Return to the archive. Primary Martin Heidegger, Sein und Zeit (1927), GA 2 §26, SZ 121–122; §41, SZ 192 (GA 2: 255); §42, SZ 199–200. SZ denotes the standard German pagination, not GA pagination. Translation Being and Time, trans. John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (1962) pp. 157–159 and 236–237. Being and Time, trans. Joan Stambaugh, rev. Dennis J. Schmidt (2010), p. 192 , supplies the third stop. These editions use different English pagination. Interpretive Sheehan, “Heidegger’s Doctrine of Meaning: His Bedeutungslehre,” §3 ; Babich, “Da-Sein’s Body: Between Heidegger on Being and Anders on Having” ; Volpi, “Dasein as Praxis” Readings hosted at Ereignis, attributed here to their respective authors. Lawrence Berger, “Attention as the Way to Being,” also supplies the GA 2: 255 / SZ 192 concordance. Experimental The sequence, comparison, risks, and cross-Topos question are BeyngLab’s editorial work. Their adequacy remains open to the passages and to further readers."},{"url":"/topoi/care/#archive","title":"Care / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"Being and Time §§26, 41, 42 with explicit edition and page references at Ereignis.","keywords":["GA 2","SZ 192","solicitude"],"text":"Being and Time §§26, 41, 42 with explicit edition and page references at Ereignis."},{"url":"/topoi/care/#terms","title":"Sorge, Besorgen, Fürsorge","type":"Topos","description":"Care, concern, and solicitude distinguished; the English translation loses their visible German kinship.","keywords":["Fursorge","translation","Macquarrie","Robinson"],"text":"Care, concern, and solicitude distinguished; the English translation loses their visible German kinship."},{"url":"/topoi/care/#readers","title":"Care / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Sheehan on meaning, Babich on bodily need, and Volpi on Aristotelian praxis.","keywords":["Sheehan","Babich","Volpi","Anders"],"text":"Sheehan on meaning, Babich on bodily need, and Volpi on Aristotelian praxis."},{"url":"/topoi/care/#disagreement","title":"Care / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"How can a formal account of existence meet bodily need and assistance?","keywords":["embodiment","dependency","ethics"],"text":"How can a formal account of existence meet bodily need and assistance?"},{"url":"/topoi/care/#practice","title":"Care / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"Keep a primary claim, an interpretation, and your own question distinct.","keywords":["archive","reading","revision"],"text":"Keep a primary claim, an interpretation, and your own question distinct."},{"url":"/topoi/care/#relations","title":"Care / World and Gestell","type":"Topos","description":"A proposed crossing from meaningful involvement to organized availability.","keywords":["worldhood","Bestand","Gestell"],"text":"A proposed crossing from meaningful involvement to organized availability."},{"url":"/topoi/care/#sources","title":"Care / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"GA 2 and SZ pagination, Macquarrie–Robinson and Stambaugh–Schmidt editions, and interpretive sources.","keywords":["bibliography","translation","Ereignis"],"text":"GA 2 and SZ pagination, Macquarrie–Robinson and Stambaugh–Schmidt editions, and interpretive sources."},{"url":"/topoi/world/","title":"World / Worldhood","type":"Topos","description":"Worldhood, involvement, significance, and being-with: primary archive passages with Sheehan and McNeill at Ereignis.","keywords":["world","worldhood","Weltlichkeit","Bewandtnis","Bedeutsamkeit","significance","GA 2","Sheehan","McNeill"],"text":"T2 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path / Being and Time Things make sense. A world is already open. How does a context of significance let something show up as something? Follow worldhood through involvement, significance, and being-with. Read the passages at beyng.com, then ask what their account of meaningful encounter leaves open. Begin with the passages FOLLOW WORLD 01 / Archive trail 02 / Translation matters 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Primary passages From things to their relations. These stops follow Being and Time, collected in GA 2. English page numbers belong to the named translation; they are not GA page numbers. The trail begins with two translations of §14 before continuing in Macquarrie–Robinson. 01 / §14 · The question of world What an inventory cannot explain An account of the things encountered, even an ontology of nature, does not yet clarify the phenomenon of world. Ask what must already be open for an encounter to be possible. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 91 Stambaugh–Schmidt, p. 63 02 / §18 · Involvement and significance Follow what refers to what Things become intelligible through relations of use, purpose, and possibility. Familiarity with these relations precedes an explicit theory of them. Heidegger calls their connected structure significance. M&R p. 119 / familiarity p. 120 / significance 03 / §26 · Being-with Others already belong to the encounter Others show themselves through the shared world of concern. Worldhood is not adequately described by adding isolated subjects to an inventory of things. M&R p. 160 / others in the world 02 / Translation matters Keep the relations visible. Term English rendering Reading distinction Weltlichkeit Worldhood / worldliness M&R uses “worldhood”; Stambaugh–Schmidt uses “worldliness” in §14. Here the question concerns the structure of world. Bewandtnis Involvement (M&R) Read a thing through the relations in which it has a role, rather than through isolated properties alone. Bedeutsamkeit Significance (M&R) The connected relations through which things can be encountered meaningfully; more than one item’s importance. The translation notes on M&R p. 120 distinguish significance from signification. Sheehan’s “meaningfulness” is an interpretive rendering to examine alongside the text. 03 / Distinct readings at Ereignis Meaning and what exceeds it. MEANINGFULNESS Thomas Sheehan “What, after all, was Heidegger about?” reads being-in-the-world as engagement with meaningfulness. Things already meet us through concerns and possibilities; meaning is not a decoration added to a bare object afterward. Read Sheehan at Ereignis INDEPENDENCE AND WITHDRAWAL William McNeill “Saying the Unsayable” follows the problem of beings in themselves through world-entry and letting-be. Beings can exceed the field in which they appear, even while their independence becomes manifest through disclosure. Read McNeill at Ereignis 04 / Interpretive problem Does appearing exhaust what appears? Sheehan emphasizes intelligibility; McNeill emphasizes the independence and withdrawal of beings within an account of disclosure. BeyngLab places these emphases beside one another as a reading problem, without claiming these essays constitute a direct exchange. Read M&R p. 120 closely: the account of significance also speaks of encountering beings as they are in themselves. What work does that claim do? Record where each interpretation clarifies the passage and where a question remains. Risk Counter-practice World becomes a container Trace relations of involvement and possibility before compiling a list of objects. Meaning becomes personal invention Return to familiarity and being-with. Ask how a practice is inherited, shared, or contested. A world becomes a cultural essence Describe a situated practice and its disputes. Follow translation, exchange, and change; invite correction from its participants. Experimental In BeyngLab’s Atlas, regional disclosure grammars name revisable ways of attending and interpreting. They do not assign an unchanging worldview to a people or place. This editorial extension must be argued and tested; §18 alone does not establish it. 05 / Reading move Trace one involvement. Keep an objection. 01 Anchor the claim. Read M&R pp. 119–120. In your own words, state how involvement connects to significance. Record the edition and page. 02 Describe an ordinary encounter. Choose a key, a cup, or a shared workbench. Trace what it is for, the other things and people involved, and the possibilities at stake. Mark this example as your own application. 03 Let the description be corrected. Read one of the two essays. Ask what your account leaves out: another person’s use, a contested purpose, or something that resists the role assigned to it. Revise one sentence and retain the objection. 06 / Crossings One question, several neighboring Topoi. CONNECTED READING Difference Follow the archive trail and test the neighboring question. CONNECTED READING Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit Follow the question of taking up a possibility. NOTHINGNESS When significance recedes. Follow the archive passages and distinct readers. T1 / CARE Why does anything matter? Return to involvement, possibility, and being-with. T3 / THING AND FOURFOLD What does a thing gather? Carry the question toward later dwelling; keep the change of vocabulary in view. T4 / GESTELL When does availability organize the encounter? Compare significance with ordering without equating them. ATLAS / EDITORIAL EXTENSION How can a grammar be revised? Compare situated disclosure practices and preserve their internal differences. Experimental These crossings propose questions across periods and projects. Early worldhood, later worlding, and Atlas grammars remain distinct terms requiring their own evidence. 07 / Sources and editions Return to the archive. Primary Martin Heidegger, Sein und Zeit (1927), GA 2 §§14, 18, 26. The trail uses English edition pagination, with no assumed equivalence to GA 2 pages. Translation Being and Time, trans. John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (1962) pp. 91, 119–120, 160. Compare Joan Stambaugh, revised by Dennis J. Schmidt (2010), p. 63 , for §14. Interpretive Thomas Sheehan, “What, after all, was Heidegger about?” (2015) Read alongside William McNeill, “Saying the Unsayable: Heidegger on the Being-in-Itself of Beings,” Gatherings 13 (2023) . Both essays are hosted at Ereignis. Experimental The comparison, reading exercise, risks, and Atlas extension are BeyngLab’s editorial proposals. They remain open to correction by the texts and their readers."},{"url":"/topoi/world/#archive","title":"World / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"Being and Time §§14, 18, 26 with English edition page references at Ereignis.","keywords":["GA 2","being-with","worldhood"],"text":"Being and Time §§14, 18, 26 with English edition page references at Ereignis."},{"url":"/topoi/world/#terms","title":"Worldhood, involvement, significance","type":"Topos","description":"Weltlichkeit, Bewandtnis, Bedeutsamkeit and their English renderings.","keywords":["worldliness","translation","meaningfulness"],"text":"Weltlichkeit, Bewandtnis, Bedeutsamkeit and their English renderings."},{"url":"/topoi/world/#readers","title":"World / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Sheehan on meaningfulness and McNeill on independence and withdrawal.","keywords":["Sheehan","McNeill","world-entry"],"text":"Sheehan on meaningfulness and McNeill on independence and withdrawal."},{"url":"/topoi/world/#disagreement","title":"World / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"Does appearing exhaust what appears? Regional disclosure grammars remain revisable practices, not cultural essences.","keywords":["essentialism","disclosure","independence"],"text":"Does appearing exhaust what appears? Regional disclosure grammars remain revisable practices, not cultural essences."},{"url":"/topoi/world/#practice","title":"World / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"Trace one involvement, compare a reader, and preserve an objection.","keywords":["significance","reading","revision"],"text":"Trace one involvement, compare a reader, and preserve an objection."},{"url":"/topoi/world/#relations","title":"World / Care, Thing, Gestell","type":"Topos","description":"Cross from meaningful involvement toward gathering and organized availability.","keywords":["fourfold","care","Bestand"],"text":"Cross from meaningful involvement toward gathering and organized availability."},{"url":"/topoi/world/#sources","title":"World / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"Being and Time translations and essays by Sheehan and McNeill at beyng.com.","keywords":["bibliography","Ereignis","GA 2"],"text":"Being and Time translations and essays by Sheehan and McNeill at beyng.com."},{"url":"/topoi/thing/","title":"Thing / Fourfold","type":"Topos","description":"Thing, fourfold, and gathering in GA 7 and GA 79, with Mitchell, Vallega-Neu, and Han at Ereignis.","keywords":["thing","Ding","fourfold","Geviert","earth","sky","mortals","divinities","GA 7","GA 79","Mitchell","Vallega-Neu","Han"],"text":"T3 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path / GA 7 and GA 79 A thing gathers. What comes near? What does a thing gather, and what escapes our account of its gathering? Read earth, sky, divinities, and mortals through Heidegger’s later thinking of things and places. Then test the reading against a particular thing, its relations, and the lives that sustain it. Begin with the passages FOLLOW THING 01 / Archive trail 02 / Fourfold terms 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Primary passages Gathering, mortality, place, world. The Bremen “Thing” lecture belongs to GA 79; the later version belongs to GA 7. Keep the versions and translations distinct. The Poetry, Language, Thought pages below carry different edition labels at Ereignis; use each linked page’s label when citing. 01 / GA 79: 17 · Bremen lecture Nearness through thinging Heidegger describes a thing’s gathering of the four as a happening of nearness. The four belong together in that gathering; they do not occupy independent compartments. Bremen and Freiburg Lectures, p. 16 Open the Bremen “Thing” lecture 02 / GA 7 · The Thing Read the four together Earth, sky, divinities, and mortals each call for thinking the others with them. Mortality receives a demanding definition through the capacity for death as death. Keep that claim visible, including its exclusion of animals from dying in this sense. Poetry, Language, Thought, p. 176 / 2001 label 03 / GA 7: 156 · Building Dwelling Thinking A bridge opens a location The bridge helps constitute a location and makes room for ways and places. Heidegger’s claim concerns how a place comes to presence, beyond the placement of a structure at coordinates. Poetry, Language, Thought, p. 154 / 1971 label 04 / GA 79 · The Thing The fourfold and world The interplay of the four is named world. Compare this later account with early worldhood: what changes when the question turns from significance and involvement toward gathering? Bremen and Freiburg Lectures, p. 18 02 / Fourfold terms Four in their belonging together. The table separates the terms for reading. Heidegger’s claim concerns their mutual belonging. These brief glosses follow the linked passages, especially “The Thing,” p. 176. Term Starting point in the text Question to retain Earth / Erde Bearing and nourishing; water, stone, plants, animals. What happens when earth appears chiefly as material to extract? Sky / Himmel Celestial courses, seasons, weather, light, and darkness. How does this gathering belong to rhythms it cannot command? Divinities / die Göttlichen The concealed and beckoning dimension of godhead. What gets lost if we translate this immediately into personal values? Mortals / die Sterblichen Humans understood through their capacity for death as death. What does the distinction disclose, and whom does it exclude? Geviert names the fourfold. The questions in the last column are BeyngLab’s prompts, not definitions supplied by Heidegger. The Sixfold studio’s “Divinities / Other(s)” wording likewise marks an experimental adaptation. 03 / Distinct readings at Ereignis Relation, particularity, emptiness. RELATIONAL THING Andrew J. Mitchell The Fourfold reads the jug and bridge through relations that extend beyond an enclosed identity. Gathering brings a thing into its surroundings; the jug’s giving and the bridge’s crossing help develop that account. Read The Fourfold, p. 13 PARTICULAR THING Daniela Vallega-Neu “Attunements, Truth, and Errancy in Heidegger’s Thinking,” note 18, questions whether Heidegger thinks concrete, singular things in their particular happening. Does the jug disclose this thing, or chiefly express a general account of thinghood? Read Vallega-Neu at Ereignis COMPARATIVE CHALLENGE Byung-Chul Han “Emptiness” argues that Heidegger’s gathering still preserves an isolating essence. Han contrasts this with his account of Zen emptiness and relations among things. Read that challenge beside Mitchell’s relational interpretation; their descriptions pull in different directions. Read Han at Ereignis 04 / Interpretive problem Can the fourfold meet this particular thing? Mitchell’s account emphasizes openness through relation. Vallega-Neu presses the question of singularity; Han challenges the persistence of an enclosing essence. This juxtaposition is BeyngLab’s editorial comparison, not a claim that all three texts form a direct debate. Return to the bridge passage. What does it let you notice about a crossing? What would you still need to learn about a particular bridge’s construction, maintenance, ownership, and accessibility? A philosophical reading can open those questions without supplying their answers. Risk Counter-practice Fourfold as a checklist Describe relations among the terms. Retain a detail that resists fitting into your scheme. Poetic quietism Name the work, exclusions, and conflicts that sustain or interrupt the gathering. Ask who can contest your account. Fixed cultural essences Treat Han’s comparison as one situated interpretation. Do not assign “relationality” or “emptiness” to whole peoples or regions. Sixfold mistaken for Heidegger’s doctrine Identify Thing and World as BeyngLab’s explicit prompts across the four, rather than two additional Heideggerian members. 05 / Reading move From an archive passage to one thing. 01 State the textual claim. Choose the bridge or fourfold passage. Record the work, translation, edition label, and page. Write one sentence explaining what gathering means there. 02 Attend to a particular thing. Choose a cup, a bench, or a crossing you can examine. Describe a relation you can point to, then name a detail the philosophical account might miss. Distinguish observation from interpretation. 03 Bring a reader and an objection. Use Mitchell, Vallega-Neu, or Han to challenge your description. Ask someone who uses or maintains the thing what needs correction. Keep their disagreement visible. 04 Try the Sixfold extension. Carry your notes into the studio. Make Thing and World explicit, then revise the cosmogram. The exercise tests a reading; it does not prove that everything fits the fourfold. Open the Sixfold studio 06 / Crossings Gathering and its neighbors. CONNECTED READING Poiesis Follow the archive trail and test the neighboring question. CONNECTED READING Dwelling / Wohnen Return to primary passages and distinct readers at beyng.com. T13 / RELEASE Letting go of mastery. Read Gelassenheit with technological dependence and responsibility in view. T2 / WORLD From significance to gathering. Compare early worldhood with later worlding while retaining the difference. T4 / GESTELL When does a thing become on-call stock? Read gathering beside the ordering question in the Bremen lectures. SIXFOLD / EXPERIMENT Make Thing and World explicit. A BeyngLab design exercise with its own purposes and limits. THINKING WITH / HARMAN Follow another transformation. Harman’s quadruple object develops a different construction from Heidegger’s fourfold. 07 / Sources and editions Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, “The Thing,” Bremen lecture (1949), GA 79 Bremen and Freiburg Lectures, trans. Andrew J. Mitchell (2012), pp. 16, 18. The GA 79: 17 / English p. 16 concordance also appears in Mitchell, The Fourfold, p. 297 . Primary Heidegger, “The Thing,” GA 7, Poetry, Language, Thought, p. 176 Trans. Albert Hofstadter; Ereignis labels this page 2001. The archive marks the transition to GA 7: 180 within the page. Primary Heidegger, “Building Dwelling Thinking,” GA 7: 156 Poetry, Language, Thought, trans. Albert Hofstadter, p. 154; Ereignis labels this page 1971. Keep this pagination separate from other printings. Interpretive Mitchell, The Fourfold: Reading the Late Heidegger (2015), p. 13 ; Vallega-Neu, “Attunements, Truth, and Errancy in Heidegger’s Thinking” (2017), note 18 ; Han, “Emptiness,” excerpt hosted at Ereignis These readings support the attributed accounts above; the comparison and exercise belong to BeyngLab. Experimental The risks, practice prompts, and Sixfold extension invite revision. Ulmer’s EPS, Heidegger’s fourfold, and BeyngLab’s Sixfold retain distinct sources and aims."},{"url":"/topoi/thing/#archive","title":"Thing / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"The Thing and Building Dwelling Thinking with translation, edition, and page distinctions.","keywords":["GA 7","GA 79","bridge","nearness"],"text":"The Thing and Building Dwelling Thinking with translation, edition, and page distinctions."},{"url":"/topoi/thing/#terms","title":"Earth, sky, divinities, mortals","type":"Topos","description":"Fourfold terms read together, with a distinction between Heidegger and BeyngLab prompts.","keywords":["Geviert","Erde","Himmel","mortality"],"text":"Fourfold terms read together, with a distinction between Heidegger and BeyngLab prompts."},{"url":"/topoi/thing/#readers","title":"Thing / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Mitchell on relation, Vallega-Neu on particularity, and Han on emptiness.","keywords":["Mitchell","Vallega-Neu","Han"],"text":"Mitchell on relation, Vallega-Neu on particularity, and Han on emptiness."},{"url":"/topoi/thing/#disagreement","title":"Thing / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"Can the fourfold meet particular things without losing labor, conflict, and difference?","keywords":["quietism","essentialism","singularity"],"text":"Can the fourfold meet particular things without losing labor, conflict, and difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/thing/#practice","title":"Thing / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"Read a passage, examine one thing, retain an objection, and test the Sixfold extension.","keywords":["cosmogram","reading","revision"],"text":"Read a passage, examine one thing, retain an objection, and test the Sixfold extension."},{"url":"/topoi/thing/#relations","title":"Thing / World, Gestell, Sixfold","type":"Topos","description":"Connected questions of significance, gathering, ordering, and experimental design.","keywords":["Harman","EPS","standing-reserve"],"text":"Connected questions of significance, gathering, ordering, and experimental design."},{"url":"/topoi/thing/#sources","title":"Thing / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"GA 7 and GA 79, Mitchell and Hofstadter translations, and interpretive archive sources.","keywords":["bibliography","Ereignis","edition"],"text":"GA 7 and GA 79, Mitchell and Hofstadter translations, and interpretive archive sources."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/","title":"Nihilism","type":"Topos","description":"Devaluation, revaluation, and forgottenness of being, with Pippin, Mitchell, and Fried at Ereignis.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"T14 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path What still binds? When values lose their force. Does making new values overcome nihilism—or continue it? Follow Heidegger’s reading of Nietzsche from devaluation toward the forgottenness of being. Keep Nietzsche’s claims, Heidegger’s interpretation, and our own diagnoses of the present distinct. Begin with the passages FOLLOW NIHILISM 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail Devaluation and forgottenness. Both passages come from Heidegger’s essay on Nietzsche’s “God is dead,” collected in GA 5, but use different English translations. Their page numbers are not interchangeable. These are Heidegger’s interpretations of Nietzsche, not direct excerpts from Nietzsche’s published books. 01 / GA 5 · Nietzsche’s Word “God Is Dead” The ambiguity of nihilism Heidegger reads Nietzsche’s devaluation alongside a revaluation that establishes new values. The world remains even when its previous highest values lose their authority. This complicates a simple story of decline. Off the Beaten Track, p. 167 02 / GA 5 · The Word of Nietzsche: “God Is Dead” What happens to being? Heidegger shifts the question from the standing of values to being’s withdrawal and its remaining unthought. Metaphysics itself enters the diagnosis. Read this shift carefully before adopting “nihilism” as a label for something you dislike. The Question Concerning Technology and Other Essays, p. 110 02 / Terms and distinctions Three claims to keep apart. Claim Question Devaluation How do formerly binding values lose their force? Trace a process rather than assuming a sudden absence of belief. Revaluation What changes when new values take the place of old ones? Does value-setting itself remain unquestioned? Forgottenness of being Heidegger changes the register of the diagnosis. What gets overlooked when attention fixes exclusively on beings? Nihilismus names the diagnosis explored here. Das Nichts, the nothing, poses a related but distinct question: its disclosure does not amount to declaring everything worthless. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Mattering, transition, political stakes. WHAT MATTERS Robert B. Pippin “Heidegger on Nietzsche on Nihilism” asks how meaning can lose its binding force without simply being argued away. His account of mattering complicates the idea that people can restore significance merely by choosing new beliefs. His notes also flag Heidegger’s heavy reliance on Nietzsche’s posthumous materials. Read Pippin at Ereignis A CHANGING ACCOUNT Andrew J. Mitchell “Nihilism in Transition” traces changes in Heidegger’s diagnosis, including a later distinction between the destruction of beings and the withdrawal of being. This reading makes it difficult to treat “Heidegger on nihilism” as a single unchanging position. Read Mitchell at Ereignis HISTORICAL RESPONSIBILITY Gregory Fried Fried’s account of Introduction to Metaphysics connects its critique of nihilism with Heidegger’s political commitments and asks whether the Western tradition holds resources exceeding his confrontation with it. Read Fried at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk How far does the diagnosis reach? Pippin emphasizes mattering, Mitchell follows textual transformations, and Fried presses political stakes. BeyngLab juxtaposes these readings; they do not form a single direct debate. Ask what evidence would support an epochal diagnosis, and which experiences it might erase. Risk Counter-practice Nihilism becomes an insult Identify a specific claim, practice, or historical change and explain why the term helps. One history explains everyone Keep the diagnosis situated. Regional disclosure grammars do not name fixed cultural essences or stages on one universal ladder. A promised renewal excuses domination Ask who authorizes the new values and who bears their cost. Philosophical ambition does not settle political responsibility. 05 / Reading move Test a diagnosis before prescribing renewal. Experimental A BeyngLab exercise, open to correction. 01 Read the change of register. Compare the two primary passages. State the difference between devaluation and forgottenness of being, with edition and page references. 02 Choose one contested purpose. Consider an institution or practice whose declared purpose no longer seems to guide its activity. Record both its declaration and one concrete example. Do not presume that everyone experiences the same loss. 03 Preserve the counterexample. Find someone for whom that practice still matters. Ask whether a change of values would answer the problem—or merely rename it. Keep the objection beside your diagnosis. 06 / Crossings Value, ordering, and the nothing. CONNECTED READING Call / Ruf Follow the question of taking up a possibility. T15 / NOTHINGNESS Keep the nothing distinct. A disclosure of beings is not a doctrine that nothing matters. T4 / GESTELL When does optimization become the purpose? A proposed comparison with orderability and availability. T13 / RELEASE Question the demand to overcome. Consider whether renewed mastery repeats the problem. T2 / WORLD Return to what matters. Read significance alongside a loss of binding force. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, “Nietzsche’s Word ‘God Is Dead’,” GA 5, in Off the Beaten Track, trans. Julian Young and Kenneth Haynes (2002), p. 167. Translation The same essay under the title “The Word of Nietzsche: ‘God Is Dead’,” in The Question Concerning Technology and Other Essays, trans. William Lovitt (1977), p. 110. The two stops select different parts of the essay, not matching passages. Interpretive Pippin, “Heidegger on Nietzsche on Nihilism”; Mitchell, “Nihilism in Transition”; Fried, “Introduction to Metaphysics.” The reader cards link directly to these texts at Ereignis. Experimental The comparison, risk questions, exercise, and crossings belong to BeyngLab. Nietzsche’s published works and posthumous notes require their own editorial distinctions before extending this trail."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/#archive","title":"Nihilism / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"Primary passages with edition and translation distinctions.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Primary passages with edition and translation distinctions."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/#terms","title":"Nihilism / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"Keep nihilism, the nothing, and neighboring terms distinct.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Keep nihilism, the nothing, and neighboring terms distinct."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/#readers","title":"Nihilism / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Devaluation, revaluation, and forgottenness of being, with Pippin, Mitchell, and Fried at Ereignis.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Devaluation, revaluation, and forgottenness of being, with Pippin, Mitchell, and Fried at Ereignis."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/#disagreement","title":"Nihilism / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"Test the interpretation and preserve an objection.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Test the interpretation and preserve an objection."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/#practice","title":"Nihilism / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"Compare passages and distinguish a primary claim from an extension.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Compare passages and distinguish a primary claim from an extension."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/#relations","title":"Nihilism / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"Follow World, Nihilism, Nothingness, and neighboring concepts.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Follow World, Nihilism, Nothingness, and neighboring concepts."},{"url":"/topoi/nihilism/#sources","title":"Nihilism / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"Primary texts and interpretive readings at beyng.com.","keywords":["nihilism","Nihilismus","Nietzsche","values","Pippin","Mitchell","Fried"],"text":"Primary texts and interpretive readings at beyng.com."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts","type":"Topos","description":"Anxiety, worldhood, and the nothing, with Withy, Doug Wise, and Carman at Ereignis.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"T15 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Nothing to point to. Yet something is disclosed. How can the nothing disclose beings without becoming another being? Read das Nichts through anxiety, worldhood, and “What Is Metaphysics?” Keep the texts’ philosophical claims distinct from nihilism and from clinical descriptions of anxiety. Begin with the passages FOLLOW NOTHINGNESS 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail Worldhood, the nothing, and a methodological question. The 1927 analysis and the 1929 lecture offer connected but distinct arguments. The Pathmarks page also records later marginal annotations: their edition dates matter. 01 / Being and Time §40 · SZ 186–187 When ordinary significance recedes Anxiety has no particular threatening entity as its object. The collapse of familiar involvements lets worldhood come into view; it does not make the world disappear. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 231 Continue on p. 232 02 / What Is Metaphysics? · GA 9: 115–116 The nothing is no object The lecture relates the manifestness of the nothing to our encounter with beings. It explicitly denies that the nothing is an entity beside other entities. The page then raises the difficulty: must existence remain anxious constantly? Pathmarks, p. 91 / text and marginal notes 03 / Being and Time §40 A rare experience, a methodological role Heidegger argues that anxiety’s rarity does not disqualify it from an ontological analysis. Ask how that experience warrants the wider claim made for it. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 235 02 / Terms and distinctions Nothing, not, and nihilism. Term Distinction Das Nichts “The nothing.” “Nothingness” is this Topos’s heading; the linked lecture denies that the nothing names an entity. Nicht “Not.” A grammatical negation should not silently substitute for Heidegger’s question of the nothing. Angst / Furcht Anxiety and fear. The reading distinguishes an indefinite disruption of familiarity from fear directed toward something threatening. Nihilismus A neighboring historical and philosophical diagnosis. The nothing’s disclosure does not establish that nothing matters. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Method, extension, and the dispute over metaphysics. PHILOSOPHICAL METHOD Katherine Withy “The Methodological Role of Angst in Being and Time” asks how beings directed toward entities can grasp their own being as a whole. Withy interprets angst as answering that problem in Division I, extending the discussion beyond its usual association with authenticity. Read Withy at Ereignis WISEACRE CROSSING Doug Wise “Angst and the thick sublime” takes up Withy’s methodological account and explores anxiety alongside the sublime. Follow the extension as Wise’s inquiry, then ask what it changes in the primary passage. Read Wise’s essay at Ereignis WHAT COUNTS AS METAPHYSICS? Taylor Carman “Heidegger’s Disavowal of Metaphysics” places Heidegger’s changing use of “metaphysics” beside Carnap’s critique. This offers a route into the dispute over philosophical language; it does not settle that dispute simply by naming the opposing positions. Read Carman at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk What warrants the passage from experience to ontology? Withy clarifies the methodological claim, Wise explores an extension, and Carman reopens the setting of a dispute about metaphysics. Their juxtaposition is BeyngLab’s comparison. Return to the lecture’s own questions on p. 91 before treating its conclusion as self-evident. Risk Counter-practice The nothing becomes a hidden object Read the explicit denial that it is a being. Explain what the term does in the argument. Distress becomes a badge of insight Keep the philosophical analysis distinct from clinical categories and from claims about anyone’s suffering. This reading exercise asks for no induced anxiety. Every tradition means the same by emptiness Compare particular sources before asserting equivalence. “Nothing,” “emptiness,” and “void” require distinct textual histories. 05 / Reading move Read an interruption without romanticizing it. Experimental A textual exercise; no special state needs to be produced. 01 Mark what recedes and what appears. Read Being and Time pp. 231–232. Make two short notes: what loses its ordinary significance, and what the passage claims becomes manifest. 02 Track the change of argument. Read Pathmarks p. 91. Identify one claim that goes beyond the earlier passage and one marginal note whose date changes how you read it. 03 Keep the objection. Read Withy or Carman. State what would count as support for the philosophical claim and where its language still troubles you. If you pursue Wise’s sublime comparison, label that extension separately. 06 / Crossings World, nihilism, reflection, and Wiseacre. CONNECTED READING Call / Ruf Follow the question of taking up a possibility. T2 / WORLD When familiarity recedes. Return to significance and the disclosure of worldhood. T14 / NIHILISM A related but distinct diagnosis. Compare the question of the nothing with devaluation and forgottenness. T16 / MINDFULNESS Question the warrant. Keep reflection open to its own blind spots. WISEACRE Continue thinking with Doug Wise. Find his archive links and the essays responding to his work. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, Being and Time, §40, trans. John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (1962), pp. 231–232, 235. SZ denotes standard German pagination, not GA 2 pagination. Primary Heidegger, “What Is Metaphysics?” (1929), GA 9: 115–116, trans. David Farrell Krell, in Pathmarks, ed. William McNeill (1998), p. 91. The linked page distinguishes first-edition and fifth-edition marginal annotations. Interpretive Withy, “The Methodological Role of Angst in Being and Time” (2012); Wise, “Angst and the thick sublime”; Carman, “Heidegger’s Disavowal of Metaphysics” (2019). Direct links appear in the reader cards. Experimental The comparisons, risks, and exercise belong to BeyngLab. They neither diagnose a person nor equate distinct philosophical traditions."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/#archive","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"Primary passages with edition and translation distinctions.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Primary passages with edition and translation distinctions."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/#terms","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"Keep nihilism, the nothing, and neighboring terms distinct.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Keep nihilism, the nothing, and neighboring terms distinct."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/#readers","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Anxiety, worldhood, and the nothing, with Withy, Doug Wise, and Carman at Ereignis.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Anxiety, worldhood, and the nothing, with Withy, Doug Wise, and Carman at Ereignis."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/#disagreement","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"Test the interpretation and preserve an objection.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Test the interpretation and preserve an objection."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/#practice","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"Compare passages and distinguish a primary claim from an extension.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Compare passages and distinguish a primary claim from an extension."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/#relations","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"Follow World, Nihilism, Nothingness, and neighboring concepts.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Follow World, Nihilism, Nothingness, and neighboring concepts."},{"url":"/topoi/nothingness/#sources","title":"Nothingness / das Nichts / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"Primary texts and interpretive readings at beyng.com.","keywords":["nothingness","das Nichts","Angst","anxiety","Withy","Wise","Carman"],"text":"Primary texts and interpretive readings at beyng.com."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/","title":"Mindfulness / Besinnung","type":"Topos","description":"Mindfulness, meditation, and reflection through GA 65, GA 66, and GA 7, with Babich, Berger, and Vallega-Neu at Ereignis.","keywords":["mindfulness","Besinnung","reflection","meditation","attention","GA 66","GA 65","GA 7","Babich","Berger","Vallega-Neu"],"text":"T16 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path / GA 65, GA 66, and GA 7 Attend to the question. Question the frame. What changes when thinking examines the meaning and assumptions of its own activity? Read Besinnung through its English renderings: mindfulness, meditation, and reflection. Follow the question toward science, history, and the possibility that thinking remains blind precisely where it feels most certain. Begin with the passages FOLLOW MINDFULNESS 01 / Archive trail 02 / Translation matters 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Primary passages Meaning, historical belonging, and science. These texts belong to different moments and projects. Read each in its own vocabulary before comparing them. GA numbers identify the German collected edition; English page numbers refer to the named translation. 01 / GA 65: 43–44 Meditation as questioning Contributions describes Besinnung as inquiry into meaning and the truth of beyng. The question of who we are opens onto our historical belonging. Heidegger also claims a sovereign role for philosophy here: keep that claim available for criticism. Contributions to Philosophy (of the Event), p. 36 Compare German GA 65: 44 02 / GA 66: 308 · Besinnung Thinking and the event This German passage puts thinking in relation to Ereignis and challenges the priority of representational calculation. Its demanding language of decision offers a point of comparison with the later vocabulary of releasement. Read German GA 66: 308 The archive supplies the English concordance: Mindfulness, p. 274. Its editorial introduction to GA 66 dates composition to 1938–39 and first publication to 1997. 03 / GA 7 · Science and Reflection How does the real become an object? The essay asks what modern theory means and how things appear through it as objects. Its account of scientific observation challenges the assumption that theory simply receives an already neutral reality. Read this as a philosophical claim to examine, including against scientific practice. The Question Concerning Technology and Other Essays, p. 163 Continue on p. 167 / observation 02 / Translation matters One German term, several English routes. Rendering Text or reading Distinction to retain Mindfulness Besinnung, GA 66; English title. Here the title names a work of being-historical thinking. The English word alone does not establish an equivalence with contemporary meditation practices. Meditation Contributions to Philosophy (of the Event), p. 36. The passage directs questioning toward meaning, beyng, and historical belonging. Reflection Lovitt’s “Science and Reflection.” Examine how a field discloses its objects and what its methods presuppose. Attention / Achtsamkeit Berger’s “Attention as the Way to Being.” A related vocabulary to compare; do not silently replace Besinnung with it. Gelassenheit names the neighboring question of releasement. Translation creates connections worth pursuing; each connection still needs a textual argument. 03 / Distinct readings at Ereignis Science, attention, and blindness. REFLECTION ON SCIENCE Babette Babich “The Problem of Science” in Nietzsche and Heidegger attends to Besinnung and to Wissenschaft’s collective, institutional character. Her reading invites inquiry into what scientific activity presupposes and how philosophy approaches that inquiry. Read Babich at Ereignis ATTENTIVENESS Lawrence Berger “Attention as the Way to Being” develops attention as a route through Heidegger’s thinking, including attentiveness to technology. Use this account to test how attending changes an encounter, while keeping its terms distinct from Besinnung. Read Berger at Ereignis DISPOSITIONAL BLINDNESS Daniela Vallega-Neu “Attunements, Truth, and Errancy” asks how attunements both disclose and blind. Her reading of Contributions, Mindfulness, and the Black Notebooks challenges the assumption that awareness of errancy protects thinking from its own distortions. Read Vallega-Neu at Ereignis 04 / Interpretive problem Who questions the questioner? Reflection can expose a method’s assumptions. It can also grant the thinker an unwarranted sense of authority over other forms of knowledge. Read the claim for philosophy in Contributions beside Vallega-Neu’s criticism of dispositional blindness. Neither depth of conviction nor a vocabulary of disclosure settles whether a judgment deserves trust. This comparison belongs to BeyngLab; the three essays do not constitute a single debate. The practical question is how an inquiry allows others to correct its account, especially when they inhabit the situation it claims to explain. Risk Counter-practice Mindfulness becomes self-optimization Ask whether the purpose and measure of success themselves need revision. Philosophy exempts itself from correction Name a claim that evidence or another person’s experience could require you to change. Critique of science dismisses research Specify the method, its achievement, and the assumption under examination. Keep conceptual criticism and empirical claims distinct. Comparison fixes cultural identities Compare named texts and situated practices. Regional disclosure grammars remain revisable descriptions, not essences of peoples. 05 / Reading move Put one measure under question. Experimental This is BeyngLab’s reading exercise, not a method prescribed by Heidegger. 01 Record the passage. Choose Contributions p. 36 or “Science and Reflection” pp. 163 and 167. State its claim and record the work, translator, and page. 02 Examine one working measure. Choose a grade, a response-time target, or a project metric. What does it reveal? What must it leave aside to do its work? 03 Question your own account. Read Vallega-Neu. Identify where your certainty might hide a limitation. Ask someone affected by the measure to contest your description. 04 Revise one claim. Retain the useful work the measure performs, record what it misses, and explain whether its purpose or use should change. Keep an unresolved objection alongside the revision. 06 / Crossings Reflection among neighboring questions. CONNECTED READING Event / Ereignis Return to primary passages and distinct readers at beyng.com. NIHILISM Question a diagnosis of the age. Follow the archive passages and distinct readers. T13 / RELEASE Question the will to secure the answer. Compare reflective inquiry with releasement while preserving their different histories. T4 / GESTELL What does a measure make available? Follow objectification and ordering into the technological question. T1 / CARE Who and what already matter? A proposed crossing from reflection to situated involvement and responsibility. T2 / WORLD What lets the question make sense? Consider the significance a practice takes for granted. 07 / Sources and editions Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, Contributions to Philosophy (of the Event), GA 65: 43–44 Trans. Richard Rojcewicz and Daniela Vallega-Neu (2012), p. 36. Besinnung appears here as “meditation.” Primary Heidegger, Besinnung, GA 66: 308 German passage; archive concordance to Mindfulness, p. 274. The GA 66 introduction supplies editorial publication context and should be distinguished from Heidegger’s text. Primary Heidegger, “Science and Reflection,” GA 7 The Question Concerning Technology and Other Essays, trans. William Lovitt (1977), pp. 163 and 167. These English page numbers are not GA pagination. Interpretive Babich, “The Problem of Science” in Nietzsche and Heidegger ; Berger, “Attention as the Way to Being,” Gatherings (2020) ; Vallega-Neu, “Attunements, Truth, and Errancy in Heidegger’s Thinking,” Gatherings (2017) The comparison, risks, and exercise are BeyngLab’s proposals and remain open to correction."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/#archive","title":"Mindfulness / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"Besinnung through Contributions, Mindfulness, and Science and Reflection.","keywords":["GA 65","GA 66","GA 7"],"text":"Besinnung through Contributions, Mindfulness, and Science and Reflection."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/#terms","title":"Mindfulness, meditation, reflection","type":"Topos","description":"English renderings of Besinnung; attention and releasement as related but distinct terms.","keywords":["Achtsamkeit","Gelassenheit","translation"],"text":"English renderings of Besinnung; attention and releasement as related but distinct terms."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/#readers","title":"Mindfulness / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Babich on science, Berger on attention, and Vallega-Neu on dispositional blindness.","keywords":["Babich","Berger","Vallega-Neu"],"text":"Babich on science, Berger on attention, and Vallega-Neu on dispositional blindness."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/#disagreement","title":"Mindfulness / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"Who questions the questioner? Reflection and its own possibilities of distortion.","keywords":["errancy","authority","essentialism"],"text":"Who questions the questioner? Reflection and its own possibilities of distortion."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/#practice","title":"Mindfulness / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"Examine a measure, invite correction, and revise a claim.","keywords":["metric","reflection","revision"],"text":"Examine a measure, invite correction, and revise a claim."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/#relations","title":"Mindfulness / Release, Care, Gestell","type":"Topos","description":"Crossings among reflection, releasement, involvement, and ordering.","keywords":["World","significance","technology"],"text":"Crossings among reflection, releasement, involvement, and ordering."},{"url":"/topoi/mindfulness/#sources","title":"Mindfulness / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"Primary texts and distinct interpretive readings at beyng.com, with edition and translation notes.","keywords":["bibliography","Ereignis","Besinnung"],"text":"Primary texts and distinct interpretive readings at beyng.com, with edition and translation notes."},{"url":"/topoi/release/","title":"Release / Gelassenheit","type":"Topos","description":"Releasement, waiting, and the technological relation, with GA 16 and GA 77 passages and Davis, Dreyfus, and Glazebrook at Ereignis.","keywords":["release","releasement","Gelassenheit","Gegnet","non-willing","waiting","GA 16","GA 77","Davis","Dreyfus","Glazebrook","Schürmann"],"text":"T13 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path / GA 16 and GA 77 Release the grip. Keep the question open. How can thinking let go of mastery without abandoning responsibility? Read Gelassenheit through the relation to technical things, waiting, and the open-region. Then ask what releasement makes possible—and what a change of attitude cannot accomplish on its own. Begin with the passages FOLLOW RELEASE 01 / Archive trail 02 / Translation matters 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Primary passages Technical things, waiting, and a revised dialogue. The 1955 address and the conversations composed in 1944–45 are distinct texts. A revised excerpt of the first conversation appeared with the address in Gelassenheit (1959). The complete conversations later appeared in GA 77. English page numbers below belong to the named translation. 01 / GA 16 · Memorial Address (1955) A yes and a no to technical things The address proposes using technical devices while refusing to let them determine everything. Releasement toward things belongs with openness to mystery: a relation to what shows itself while remaining concealed. Open the Memorial Address / Discourse on Thinking Reading target: Discourse on Thinking, pp. 54–55. Dreyfus reproduces and discusses these passages ; the archive entrance links the primary text. 02 / GA 77: 122–124 · A Triadic Conversation Waiting and the open-region The interlocutors place thinking in a relation to the open-region that exceeds thinking’s own determination. Their discussion of waiting unsettles the assumption that thinking must first represent and secure its object. Country Path Conversations, p. 80 03 / GA 77: 151–152 · A Triadic Conversation Read the revision as evidence The conversation speaks of releasement to the worlding of world. The translator’s note records a change to the open-region in the 1959 excerpt. Ask what shifts with that wording before treating the two versions as interchangeable. Country Path Conversations, p. 99 / note 58 02 / Translation matters Release, releasement, letting. Term Reading distinction Question to carry Gelassenheit “Release” names this Topos; “releasement” preserves the wording used by the English readings linked here. What loosens when things cease to appear only as material for our plans? Gegnet Davis renders this “open-region” in Country Path Conversations. How does thinking belong to an openness it cannot manufacture? Non-willing In Davis’s reading, the question reaches beyond the opposition between activity and passivity. Could an effort to force ourselves into release repeat the demand for mastery? Besinnung Mindfulness names a neighboring Topos. Keep its vocabulary and sources distinct from Gelassenheit. How might reflection change what merits our attention? The final column contains BeyngLab prompts. No single English word settles the philosophical relation among releasement, reflection, and letting-be. 03 / Distinct readings at Ereignis Non-willing, shared meaning, material dependence. BEYOND WILLING Bret W. Davis “Another Chorology” reads the conversations as a turn toward releasement. Non-willing exceeds the choice between imposing a result and passively accepting one; the dialogue attempts another relation to the openness in which things appear. Read Davis at Ereignis A FREE RELATION Hubert Dreyfus “Heidegger on Gaining a Free Relation to Technology” asks what follows once efficiency loses its claim to govern every purpose. Dreyfus also argues that releasement alone does not provide the shared practices and meaningful differences a community needs. Read Dreyfus at Ereignis LABOR AND CAPITAL Patricia Glazebrook “Heidegger and Economics” brings technological dependence into the relations of capital and labor. Devices can redirect work toward their purchase, operation, and maintenance. Her account puts economic conditions beside the promise of a freer relation to technology. Read Glazebrook at Ereignis Another route opens through Reiner Schürmann’s “Heidegger and the Mystical Tradition” . Follow its treatment of releasement and thinking as a situated interpretation; comparative inquiry requires more than identifying a shared vocabulary. 04 / Interpretive problem What can a freer relation change? Davis clarifies the challenge to willing; Dreyfus asks what can sustain shared meaning; Glazebrook brings material dependence into the picture. BeyngLab juxtaposes these arguments as a problem for reading and practice, without claiming a direct three-way debate. A worker required to remain available, a caregiver using essential equipment, and a person choosing a leisure device face different possibilities of refusal. Ask which conditions permit a yes and a no, and which must change before that choice becomes meaningful. These are BeyngLab’s extensions of the reading. Risk Counter-practice Release becomes resignation Name the obligation that remains and the concrete harm that calls for action. Releasement becomes a productivity technique Ask whether openness can unsettle the goal itself, rather than merely improve its execution. Withdrawal looks equally available to everyone Identify who can refuse, who bears the cost, and whose labor makes the pause possible. Comparison turns into cultural essence Distinguish particular texts and practices. Do not assign serenity, surrender, or mastery to entire traditions or regions. 05 / Reading move A yes, a no, and an unanswered question. Experimental This exercise tests an interpretation of the address. It does not claim to produce Gelassenheit on demand. 01 Anchor the distinction. Read the address beside Dreyfus’s discussion. Record what receives a yes, what receives a no, and how openness enters the argument. 02 Examine one arrangement. Choose a notification system, a scheduling practice, or a familiar device. Describe what it enables and what it asks you and others to make continuously available. 03 Keep responsibility in the account. Identify one demand you could question, one obligation you must sustain, and a person whose experience might change your description. Compare your proposal with Glazebrook’s account of dependence. 04 Retain what remains unresolved. Return to the dialogue’s waiting. Write the question that your proposed change cannot settle. Leave space for another answer and revise after listening. 06 / Crossings Letting, gathering, and care. CONNECTED READING Dwelling / Wohnen Return to primary passages and distinct readers at beyng.com. T4 / GESTELL Question the demand for availability. Read releasement beside ordering and the technological relation. T3 / THING AND FOURFOLD Attend to what gathers. What happens when a thing exceeds the use prescribed for it? T1 / CARE Keep another’s possibilities open. A proposed comparison between letting and solicitude across different periods. T16 / MINDFULNESS Distinguish releasement and reflection. Read Besinnung through its translations and the question of reflective blindness. 07 / Sources and editions Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, “Memorial Address” (1955), GA 16 Discourse on Thinking, trans. John M. Anderson and E. Hans Freund (1966), reading target pp. 54–55. The excerpts discussed here also appear in Dreyfus’s essay. Primary Heidegger, Country Path Conversations, GA 77 Composed 1944–45; trans. Bret W. Davis (2010), p. 80 (GA 77: 122–124) and p. 99 (GA 77: 151–152) . Notes 48 and 58 help distinguish the fuller dialogue from the 1959 excerpt. Interpretive Davis, “Another Chorology: Reading Heidegger’s Plato Books” ; Dreyfus, “Heidegger on Gaining a Free Relation to Technology” ; Glazebrook, “Heidegger and Economics” The readings are attributed to their authors. Schürmann, “Heidegger and the Mystical Tradition,” offers a further route with explicit editorial notes. Experimental The comparison, responsibility question, practical exercise, and cross-Topos connections belong to BeyngLab. Their adequacy remains open to the archive and to the people affected by a proposed practice."},{"url":"/topoi/release/#archive","title":"Release / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"Memorial Address and Country Path Conversations with edition and revision distinctions.","keywords":["GA 16","GA 77","1959"],"text":"Memorial Address and Country Path Conversations with edition and revision distinctions."},{"url":"/topoi/release/#terms","title":"Gelassenheit, Gegnet, non-willing","type":"Topos","description":"Releasement and the open-region, with Mindfulness as a distinct neighboring Topos.","keywords":["Besinnung","waiting","translation"],"text":"Releasement and the open-region, with Mindfulness as a distinct neighboring Topos."},{"url":"/topoi/release/#readers","title":"Release / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Davis on non-willing, Dreyfus on shared meaning, Glazebrook on labor and capital, and a Schürmann route.","keywords":["Davis","Dreyfus","Glazebrook","Schurmann"],"text":"Davis on non-willing, Dreyfus on shared meaning, Glazebrook on labor and capital, and a Schürmann route."},{"url":"/topoi/release/#disagreement","title":"Release / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"What can a freer relation change? Keep responsibility and unequal possibilities of refusal in view.","keywords":["resignation","labor","mastery"],"text":"What can a freer relation change? Keep responsibility and unequal possibilities of refusal in view."},{"url":"/topoi/release/#practice","title":"Release / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"A yes, a no, an obligation, and an unanswered question.","keywords":["technology","openness","revision"],"text":"A yes, a no, an obligation, and an unanswered question."},{"url":"/topoi/release/#relations","title":"Release / Gestell, Thing, Care","type":"Topos","description":"Crossings toward gathering, solicitude, and Mindfulness in cultivation.","keywords":["fourfold","Besinnung","availability"],"text":"Crossings toward gathering, solicitude, and Mindfulness in cultivation."},{"url":"/topoi/release/#sources","title":"Release / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"GA 16 and GA 77, Anderson–Freund and Davis translations, and distinct interpretive sources.","keywords":["bibliography","Ereignis","revisions"],"text":"GA 16 and GA 77, Anderson–Freund and Davis translations, and distinct interpretive sources."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/","title":"Gestell / Standing-Reserve","type":"Topos","description":"An archive-led reading of Heidegger’s Ge-stell and Bestand in GA 7 and GA 79, with Babich, Mitchell, Kisiel, and Wise at Ereignis.","keywords":["Ge-stell","Gestell","Bestand","standing-reserve","enframing","positionality","Heidegger","GA 7","GA 79","Babich","Mitchell","Kisiel","Doug Wise","Ereignis"],"text":"T4 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path / GA 7 and GA 79 When everything must stand ready. How does what exists come to appear as orderable, replaceable, and on call? Begin with Heidegger’s passages in Pete Ferreira’s Ereignis archive. Compare two English renderings of Ge-stell , then follow a disagreement about what the diagnosis shows and obscures. This Topos keeps its sources beside its questions. Open the archive trail ↘ FOLLOW THE TOPOS 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and translations 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement 05 / Capture risks 06 / Reading move 07 / Crossings 08 / Source ledger 01 / Primary passages at beyng.com Four stops through the text. Follow the passages in order, or open one that catches you. The first, second, and fourth belong to “The Question Concerning Technology” (GA 7); the third returns to the 1949 Bremen lecture “Das Ge-Stell” (GA 79). The English versions make different translation choices. [1] [2] 01 / GA 7: 18 Bestand: standing-reserve Heidegger asks how what is ordered comes to have its standing as reserve. The word reaches beyond a warehouse of objects to a way in which beings appear. German page ↗ English essay ↗ 02 / GA 7: 21 Ge-stell: the ordering claim He names the gathering that challenges people to reveal the real in the mode of ordering. The claim is about revealing; a machine or a rack does not itself constitute Ge-stell. German page ↗ English essay ↗ 03 / GA 79: 40; English p. 42 Positionality and replaceability The Bremen lecture presses the question of interchangeability. On the German page, Heidegger also raises an objection to making Ge-Stell universal: nature may set a limit to technical ordering. Andrew J. Mitchell’s English translation uses “positionality” for Ge-Stell . German page ↗ English page ↗ 04 / GA 7: 29; English p. 333 The danger to other revealing Heidegger’s danger is that ordering might bar access to a more originary revealing. Ask what this claim illuminates, and whether it leaves room for concrete differences among places and people. German page ↗ English p. 333 ↗ 02 / Distinctions Keep the terms in motion. GE-STELL Mode of revealing Lovitt renders the term “enframing” in Basic Writings ; Mitchell renders it “positionality” in the Bremen lectures. Read each translation with its passage and its date. Neither should be treated as a name for a gadget. BESTAND What stands on call “Standing-reserve” describes the way what is ordered shows up within this revealing. It is related to Ge-stell, not a second name for it. Test the difference in GA 7: 18–21 before extending the words to a current institution or interface. BeyngLab prompt. “Who gets positioned, by whom, and for what?” is our question for reading these pages. It neither settles a translation debate nor makes one epochal claim explain every situation. 03 / Essays hosted by Ereignis Four ways to reopen the question. QUESTIONING Babette Babich “Heidegger’s Questioning After Technology” returns to the essay’s opening claim that questioning builds a way. Her reading puts the form of the question and Heidegger’s account of causality back in view. Read at Ereignis ↗ TEXTUAL HISTORY Andrew J. Mitchell “The Question Concerning the Machine” traces notebooks leading toward the Bremen lectures and the later technology essay. Compare the earlier formulation with the familiar 1953 lecture. Read at Ereignis ↗ CONTEMPORARY READING Theodore Kisiel “Heidegger and Our Twenty-first Century Experience of Ge-Stell” extends the inquiry into contemporary experience. Ask where an extension gains precision and where it becomes too broad. Read at Ereignis ↗ WISEACRE Doug Wise “The Essence of Modern Technology and the Essence of Life:—Same” sets Heidegger’s language of positionality and standing reserve into a provocative encounter with life. Read Wise’s wager as his own argument. Read at Ereignis ↗ 04 / Interpretive problem How far does Gestell reach? The primary texts name a claim that draws the human into ordering as well as an ordering of beings. The Bremen passage on replaceability sharpens this account, then asks whether nature sets a limit to its universality. A diagnosis of an age may still smooth out differences in labor, vulnerability, place, and power. Keep both the text’s own hesitation and that further objection in view. [1] [2] Babich foregrounds the practice of questioning; Mitchell brings the drafts and sequence of lectures into view; Kisiel and Wise each risk an extension into new terrain. Their essays need not form a consensus. Compare what each adds to the passage at GA 7: 21, and keep the objection beside the reading. [3] 05 / Capture risks Where a Topos can harden. Risk What happens Correction Gadget shorthand Gestell becomes a synonym for a phone, camera, or computer. Return to GA 7: 21 and name the mode of revealing before naming an apparatus. One total age Every practice looks identical under a single account of orderability. Put a resistant passage or counterexample beside the claim; specify its limit. Cultural essence A people or place gets labeled “technological” or “authentic.” Describe a situated operation and invite those involved to contest the description. 06 / Reading move Make an archive comparison. A small research protocol for this Topos; keep the exact pages with the revised claim. 01 Read a pair. Put GA 7: 18 and 21 alongside the English version. Mark the difference between Ge-stell and Bestand. 02 Shift the translation. Open GA 79: 40 and Mitchell’s p. 42. What does “positionality” let you hear that “enframing” may not? 03 Bring one reader. Choose Babich, Mitchell, Kisiel, or Wise. Record an argument of theirs and the primary page it changes for you. 04 Keep an objection. Write where the account overreaches. Revise the question, and leave the objection visible for the next reader. 07 / Crossings within BeyngLab Return to another Topos. CONNECTED READING Technē Follow the archive trail and test the neighboring question. NIHILISM Does optimization answer the question of purpose? Follow the archive passages and distinct readers. T16 / MINDFULNESS Question the frame. Examine the assumptions that shape what counts and what matters. T13 / RELEASE Letting go of mastery. Read Gelassenheit with technological dependence and responsibility in view. T1 / CARE Who orders, and who is claimed? Read obligation and dependence beside availability. T2 / WORLD How does orderability become a world? Ask what else can show up in the same situation. T3 / THING Can a thing exceed inventory? Read the Bremen lectures’ Thing and Positionality together. TURNS / TIME Where does the question change? Compare middle machination with later positionality. WISEACRE / READER What does Wise do with Ge-Stell? Follow his original essays back to Ereignis. A further site crossing leads to the Pharmacological Atlas grammar , where Stiegler asks a different question about technical supports and care. The Topos above stands on its own archive trail. 08 / Source ledger Text, edition, and status. Primary Martin Heidegger, “The Question Concerning Technology,” trans. William Lovitt, in Basic Writings , rev. ed. David Farrell Krell (1993) German “Die Frage nach der Technik,” Vorträge und Aufsätze , GA 7: 18, 21, 29; English pp. 322–26, 333. The archive offers individual German pages and the English text. Primary Martin Heidegger, “Positionality,” trans. Andrew J. Mitchell, in Bremen and Freiburg Lectures (2012), p. 42 1949 Bremen lecture “Das Ge-Stell,” GA 79: 40; the English edition and German page are linked in stop 03. Translation vocabulary differs from Lovitt’s. Interpretive Babich, “Heidegger’s Questioning After Technology” ; Mitchell, “The Question Concerning the Machine” ; Kisiel, “Heidegger and Our Twenty-first Century Experience of Ge-Stell” ; Wise, “The Essence of Modern Technology and the Essence of Life:—Same” Four distinct readings hosted by Ereignis. Their arguments remain attributable to their authors. Experimental The archive sequence, questions, capture risks, and reading protocol are BeyngLab’s editorial arrangement. Readers can revise them against the linked texts and affected situations."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#archive","title":"Gestell / four-stop Ereignis trail","type":"Topos","description":"GA 7: 18, 21, 29 and GA 79: 40 with direct German and English archive links.","keywords":["Heidegger","Bestand","Ge-stell","beyng.com"],"text":"GA 7: 18, 21, 29 and GA 79: 40 with direct German and English archive links."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#terms","title":"Gestell and Bestand / terms","type":"Topos","description":"Enframing and positionality render Ge-stell in different English editions; standing-reserve names how beings appear under ordering.","keywords":["Ge-stell","Bestand","GA 7","GA 79"],"text":"Enframing and positionality render Ge-stell in different English editions; standing-reserve names how beings appear under ordering."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#readers","title":"Gestell / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Babich, Mitchell, Kisiel, and Wise reopen the textual history and contemporary reach of Ge-stell.","keywords":["Babich","Mitchell","Kisiel","Wiseacre"],"text":"Babich, Mitchell, Kisiel, and Wise reopen the textual history and contemporary reach of Ge-stell."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#disagreement","title":"Gestell / interpretive problem","type":"Topos","description":"How far does an epochal diagnosis reach, and what concrete differences does it risk obscuring?","keywords":["translation","ordering","power"],"text":"How far does an epochal diagnosis reach, and what concrete differences does it risk obscuring?"},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#capture","title":"Gestell / capture risks","type":"Topos","description":"Avoid gadget shorthand, a total diagnosis of an age, and fixed cultural labels by returning to the passages and objections.","keywords":["danger","counter-practice","essentialism"],"text":"Avoid gadget shorthand, a total diagnosis of an age, and fixed cultural labels by returning to the passages and objections."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#practice","title":"Gestell / archive comparison","type":"Topos","description":"Compare German and English passages, bring one interpretive reader, and preserve an objection.","keywords":["reading","positionality","revision"],"text":"Compare German and English passages, bring one interpretive reader, and preserve an objection."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#relations","title":"Gestell / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"Read Care, World, and Thing beside the ordering question.","keywords":["concept network","Topoi","technics"],"text":"Read Care, World, and Thing beside the ordering question."},{"url":"/topoi/standing-reserve/#sources","title":"Gestell / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"GA 7 and GA 79 texts, their English translations, and interpretive essays hosted at Ereignis.","keywords":["bibliography","translation","Ereignis"],"text":"GA 7 and GA 79 texts, their English translations, and interpretive essays hosted at Ereignis."},{"url":"/hot-house/","title":"Hot House","type":"People & works","description":"Ten situated entrances through books, essays, video, audio, and experiments that read with Heidegger and move beyond him.","keywords":["Iain Thomson","Babette Babich","Shaggy-Haired Angel","Simon Critchley","Lee Braver","Doug Wise","Avital Ronell","Peter Sloterdijk","Graham Harman","Ian Bogost","Wiseacre","Apply-Degger","Groundless Grounds","Tool-Being","Spheres","The Telephone Book","Alien Phenomenology","jurisprudence","video","art","poetry"],"text":"04 / VOICES Hot House Heidis Read with living voices. This reading room brings scholars, essayists, and makers into a shared conversation. Each entry starts with a particular work, then asks what its author does with a Heideggerian question. The connections differ; these people need not agree with Heidegger, one another, or BeyngLab. GO TO A READER Thomson Babich Shaggy-Haired Angel Critchley Braver Wise Ronell Sloterdijk Harman Bogost Opening shelf / six entrances Follow a work; bring back a question. 01 / ART & TECHNOLOGY · BOOK Iain Thomson Heidegger, Art, and Postmodernity (2011) asks how art can change a historical understanding of being. Bring the book into conversation with the middle Turn’s danger and the late Turn’s question of dwelling. Reading question When does a work open a possibility, and when do we project our desired history onto it? University of New Mexico profile & works ↗ 02 / POETRY & CRITIQUE · BOOK Babette Babich Words in Blood, Like Flowers (2007) moves among philosophy, poetry, music, Hölderlin, Nietzsche, and Heidegger. Her work gives the Topoi a way to test their own habits of philosophical reading. Reading question What changes when rhythm or material expression resists our neat concept map? Fordham bibliography ↗ Read Babich with the Care Topos 03 / PUBLIC PHILOSOPHY · VIDEO Shaggy-Haired Angel The channel’s Heidegger video essays include Should We CANCEL Martin Heidegger? and Horror Within Heidegger’s Philosophy . Their public-facing form puts difficult thought, attraction, and political judgment in the same viewing space. Reading question What can a video essay make newly thinkable, and what does its speed or drama leave out? Explore the YouTube channel ↗ Start with the Heidegger question ↗ 04 / EVERYDAYNESS · AUDIO & VIDEO Simon Critchley Apply-Degger takes a slow, episode-by-episode route through Being and Time , including world, being-with, care, death, and politics. His wider writing carries philosophical questions into public life. Reading question What happens when an analytic of everyday life becomes an invitation to apply a text to one’s own life? Official site ↗ Listen and watch Apply-Degger ↗ 05 / HEIDEGGER & WITTGENSTEIN · BOOK & VIDEO Lee Braver Groundless Grounds (2012) puts Wittgenstein and Heidegger into conversation; Heidegger: Thinking of Being (2014) offers another entrance. His Ergo video courses range more widely across philosophy, so the videos and Heidegger scholarship serve different reading tasks. Reading question Can the ordinary support thought without turning into a foundation that claims to settle it? USF profile and bibliography ↗ Videos: Descartes to Nietzsche ↗ Videos: Socrates to Sartre ↗ Read Braver at Ereignis ↗ 06 / HERMENEUTICS & LAW · WISEACRE Doug Wise / Wiseacre Wise’s essays at Pete Ferreira’s Ereignis move among Heidegger, affect, technics, biology, and legal interpretation. Two hermeneutical motives in Reading the Constitution makes jurisprudence a concrete site for asking how forms of interpretation order what can count. Reading question What has a legal frame already decided before a disputed case comes into view? Doug Wise’s essay index at Ereignis ↗ Read Wise on the Constitution ↗ Read Wise on Kainurgesic transcendence ↗ Read Wise with the Gestell Topos ↗ BEYNGLAB / READINGS WITH WISE The Wiseacre at Law A working paper reads Wise’s constitutional hermeneutics with Schürmann’s double bind and Lyotard’s judgment without final criteria. Its question is how law makes a common world while hearing a claim its categories cannot yet phrase. Read the essay ↗ Wiseacre at the Edge of Nothing A StudioLab/BeyngLab draft follows Wise’s comic and ontological experiments through D0, queer science fiction, deconstraint, and AI worlding. It asks what happens when a gap remains open to another world. Read the essay ↗ These are BeyngLab/StudioLab responses to Wise. They are working essays, distinct from his own writing in the Ereignis archive. Thinking with / moving elsewhere Four distinct departures. This is a reading path across works, not a school or a measure of Heideggerian fidelity. Each thinker changes a different part of the question; Bogost’s route is looser and often runs through object-oriented ontology and design. 07 / CALL · TEST Heidegger … Ronell Avital Ronell The Telephone Book (1989) reads Heidegger’s call through telephonic technology and drives the question into politics, mediation, interruption, and addiction. The Test Drive pursues her separate inquiry into the regimes that make truth answer to a test. Reading question Who or what calls us before we choose to answer, and what does a test demand before it tells us what is true? The Telephone Book · Nebraska Press ↗ The Test Drive · Illinois Press ↗ 08 / ATMOSPHERE · PRACTICE Heidegger … Sloterdijk Peter Sloterdijk Rules for the Human Zoo replies directly to Heidegger’s Letter on Humanism . The Spheres project pursues shared dwelling through interiors, air, envelopes, and immunitary space. That spatial and anthropotechnical project follows its own course beyond Heidegger scholarship. Reading question Who makes a world breathable, and who is kept outside its protective envelope? Rules for the Human Zoo · Suhrkamp ↗ Bubbles, Spheres I · MIT Press ↗ 09 / TOOL · WITHDRAWAL Heidegger … Harman Graham Harman Tool-Being (2002) makes Heidegger’s tool analysis the hinge of an object metaphysics: objects withdraw from practical use and theory, and from relations with other objects. This is a forceful revision, not an exposition. Harman’s later quadruple object is a different construction from Heidegger’s earth–sky–mortals–divinities. Reading question What becomes visible when withdrawal belongs to all objects, and what happens to world, history, and mortality in that move? Tool-Being · book record ↗ Test Thing and World in Sixfold ↗ 10 / GAME · CARPENTRY Heidegger … Bogost Ian Bogost Alien Phenomenology (2012) makes object-oriented thought operative through ontography, metaphorism, and carpentry; Bogost’s account of Cow Clicker directly applies enframing to social games. He works near Harman while defining his own methods. Heidegger is one interlocutor in a wider practice of games, software, media, and designed artifacts. Reading question What can a made artifact reveal about a system’s way of ordering things that a conceptual map might miss? Alien Phenomenology · author’s site ↗ Cow Clicker · author’s account ↗ Ontography and carpentry · author’s example ↗ EDITORIAL CHARTER Hot House entries remain invitations to read, watch, and argue. A linked work starts a particular encounter; it does not imply that its author endorses BeyngLab. The ellipsis marks a question carried between thinkers, not membership in a single Heideggerian school. Provenance Historical Work and series titles link to publisher pages, creators’ sites, universities, and the Ereignis archive. Sloterdijk’s Rules names Heidegger’s Letter as its addressee; Bogost names enframing in his Cow Clicker account. Interpretive The ellipses, reading questions, and relative strength of these connections are BeyngLab’s editorial judgments. Ronell’s Test Drive , Sloterdijk’s Spheres , Harman’s object metaphysics, and Bogost’s games each develop projects of their own."},{"url":"/hot-house/#iain-thomson","title":"Iain Thomson","type":"People & works","description":"Art and historical understanding of being in Heidegger, Art, and Postmodernity.","keywords":["art","technology"],"text":"Art and historical understanding of being in Heidegger, Art, and Postmodernity."},{"url":"/hot-house/#babette-babich","title":"Babette Babich","type":"People & works","description":"Philosophy, poetry, music, Nietzsche, and Heidegger in Words in Blood, Like Flowers.","keywords":["poetry","music"],"text":"Philosophy, poetry, music, Nietzsche, and Heidegger in Words in Blood, Like Flowers."},{"url":"/hot-house/#shaggy-haired-angel","title":"Shaggy-Haired Angel","type":"People & works","description":"Heidegger video essays on the channel, including a question about cancellation.","keywords":["video","YouTube"],"text":"Heidegger video essays on the channel, including a question about cancellation."},{"url":"/hot-house/#simon-critchley","title":"Simon Critchley","type":"People & works","description":"Apply-Degger: a slow audio and video reading of Being and Time.","keywords":["Apply-Degger","Onassis","podcast"],"text":"Apply-Degger: a slow audio and video reading of Being and Time."},{"url":"/hot-house/#lee-braver","title":"Lee Braver","type":"People & works","description":"Heidegger and Wittgenstein books, an Ereignis essay, and wider video courses on Ergo.","keywords":["Groundless Grounds","Heidegger","Ergo","video"],"text":"Heidegger and Wittgenstein books, an Ereignis essay, and wider video courses on Ergo."},{"url":"/hot-house/#doug-wise","title":"Doug Wise / Wiseacre","type":"People & works","description":"Wise’s writing at Ereignis and two BeyngLab responses: The Wiseacre at Law and Wiseacre at the Edge of Nothing.","keywords":["Wiseacre","jurisprudence","law","Ereignis","D0"],"text":"Wise’s writing at Ereignis and two BeyngLab responses: The Wiseacre at Law and Wiseacre at the Edge of Nothing."},{"url":"/hot-house/#avital-ronell","title":"Avital Ronell","type":"People & works","description":"The Telephone Book and The Test Drive: technological address, interruption, addiction, and testing.","keywords":["Telephone Book","Test Drive","technology","testology"],"text":"The Telephone Book and The Test Drive: technological address, interruption, addiction, and testing."},{"url":"/hot-house/#peter-sloterdijk","title":"Peter Sloterdijk","type":"People & works","description":"Rules for the Human Zoo responds to Heidegger; Spheres pursues shared dwelling through air and envelopes.","keywords":["Letter on Humanism","Spheres","Bubbles","anthropotechnics"],"text":"Rules for the Human Zoo responds to Heidegger; Spheres pursues shared dwelling through air and envelopes."},{"url":"/hot-house/#graham-harman","title":"Graham Harman","type":"People & works","description":"Tool-Being develops Heidegger’s tool analysis into object-oriented metaphysics of withdrawal.","keywords":["Tool-Being","OOO","withdrawal","quadruple object"],"text":"Tool-Being develops Heidegger’s tool analysis into object-oriented metaphysics of withdrawal."},{"url":"/hot-house/#ian-bogost","title":"Ian Bogost","type":"People & works","description":"Alien Phenomenology makes ontography, metaphorism, and carpentry; Cow Clicker reads social games through enframing.","keywords":["Alien Phenomenology","carpentry","Cow Clicker","enframing"],"text":"Alien Phenomenology makes ontography, metaphorism, and carpentry; Cow Clicker reads social games through enframing."},{"url":"/petes-corner/","title":"Pete’s Corner","type":"Archive","description":"A passage from BeyngLab's experimental studio to Pete Ferreira's Ereignis archive at beyng.com.","keywords":["Pete Ferreira","beyng.com","Ereignis","Heidegger archive","GA 2"],"text":"05 / ARCHIVE Pete’s Corner The archive keeps the door open. Pete Ferreira’s Ereignis at beyng.com gathers and preserves Heidegger texts and reading paths. BeyngLab stages diagrams and experiments with them. Follow a source out, and return with a better question. Open Ereignis archive ↗ Four archival entrances Begin with the text. GA 2 / EARLY Being and Time Worldhood, care, being-with ↗ MIDDLE Contributions to Philosophy History of Being and Ereignis ↗ LATE Building Dwelling Thinking Thing, fourfold, dwelling ↗ TECHNICS The Question Concerning Technology Enframing and the question of technology ↗ STUDIO ↔ ARCHIVE These links open the existing archive objects. The plates and workshops on this site are BeyngLab interpretations; they do not replace the source or claim to mirror the archive. Contributions to Pete’s Corner will carry their own attribution when developed with Pete."},{"url":"/paths/sufi-heidegger-after-corbin/","title":"Sufi Heidegger after Corbin","type":"Pathway","description":"A five-station path through Heidegger, Corbin's imaginal, disputed reception, film and art, and a workshop return.","keywords":["Sufi Heidegger","Corbin","ta'wil","imaginal","Suhrawardi","Fardid","Shayegan","Nasr","El-Bizri","Kiarostami","Bayzai","Farmanfarmaian","Neshat"],"text":"P1 / PATHWAY A guided reception field / five stations Sufi Heidegger after Corbin. “Sufi Heidegger” is BeyngLab’s working name for a contested route through texts and images. It is not the name of an established school, an identity for Islamicate philosophy, or a claim that every artist here read Heidegger. View the companion plate ↗ 01 Question 02 Hinge 03 Afterlives 04 Film & art 05 Return 01 HEIDEGGERIAN QUESTION What lets a world appear? Begin from worldhood and hermeneutics: understanding is always situated. The question of nothing, language, and mortality gives later readers several entrances; no single Heideggerian answer governs what follows. Primary Being and Time (GA 2) ↗ Trace the Turns ↗ Test the danger. A supposedly universal question may still carry the limits of its origin and vocabulary. 02 CORBIN AS HINGE What can an image disclose? Henry Corbin’s route from Heideggerian hermeneutics toward Suhrawardi, ta’wil, and the mundus imaginalis makes the image more than an illustration of a prior concept. Here, the imaginal proposes a mode of world disclosure. The passage is a historical and interpretive hinge, not a claim that Heidegger’s categories contain these traditions. Historical reception Read the Imaginal grammar ↗ Test the danger. Spiritual geography can become angelic abstraction, losing institutions, bodies, and history. 03 CONTESTED AFTERLIVES Follow branches, not disciples. These four names mark distinct conversations. Their placement together is an editorial comparison from the AIR V report; it does not make them a lineage or give them a shared doctrine. Fardid Iranian Heidegger reception and the language of Westoxification. Read with the danger of mythopolitical exclusion in view. Historical reception / contested Shayegan Early cultural dualisms and later self-critique complicate any simple East/West map. Historical & interpretive comparison Nasr Sacred art and traditionalist thought form an adjacent conversation; this is not simply a Heideggerian branch. Adjacent line / editorial comparison El-Bizri Work on Avicenna and phenomenology invites a wider Islamicate philosophical conversation beyond Corbin. Scholarly comparison Test the danger. A diagram can falsely make disagreement look like inheritance. Ask what each writer actually claims and who is missing. 04 FILM & ART Let the works interrupt the map. The following are BeyngLab resonances : proposals for close reading, not assertions of direct Heideggerian influence or religious identity. A future documented reception claim needs its own evidence. Kiarostami The Wind Will Carry Us : offscreen lives, delay, and the visiting camera’s power. Compare two lenses ↗ Bayzai Travelers and ritual threshold. Attend to the film’s own form and context. Farmanfarmaian Mirror geometry and divided perspective. Let material, craft, and bodily position matter. Neshat Veil, calligraphy, voice, and Logic of the Birds . Avoid collapsing distinct works into one symbol. Test the danger. Never call Kiarostami a “Sufi filmmaker” on the strength of this juxtaposition. Do not turn an artist’s work into a philosophical illustration. 05 RETURN TO PRACTICE Read, then revise. Choose one image, place, or object. Describe what it offers to sight; what world it opens; and what the reading leaves out. Name a danger before turning an interpretation into a design claim. If a participant contests a category, keep the objection with the diagram. Experimental Run the Ta’wil Portal ↗ Read the plate ↗ How to read the evidence Historical A documented reception must be checked in a named source. Interpretive A scholarly comparison reads works together without claiming direct influence. Experimental A BeyngLab resonance is a proposed encounter to test with the works. This pathway follows the AIR V report ; further bibliography is an editorial task before expanding the individual branches."},{"url":"/paths/sufi-heidegger-after-corbin/#question","title":"Heideggerian question / Sufi pathway","type":"Pathway","description":"Begin with situated worldhood and hermeneutics, while testing the limits of Heidegger’s vocabulary.","keywords":["Being and Time","world disclosure"],"text":"Begin with situated worldhood and hermeneutics, while testing the limits of Heidegger’s vocabulary."},{"url":"/paths/sufi-heidegger-after-corbin/#hinge","title":"Corbin and the imaginal / Sufi pathway","type":"Pathway","description":"Corbin’s interpretive hinge through Suhrawardi, ta’wil, and the mundus imaginalis.","keywords":["Suhrawardi","imaginal","ta'wil"],"text":"Corbin’s interpretive hinge through Suhrawardi, ta’wil, and the mundus imaginalis."},{"url":"/paths/sufi-heidegger-after-corbin/#afterlives","title":"Contested afterlives / Sufi pathway","type":"Pathway","description":"Distinct conversations with Fardid, Shayegan, Nasr, and El-Bizri; no single lineage is claimed.","keywords":["Fardid","Shayegan","Nasr","El-Bizri","Iran","Westoxification","Avicenna"],"text":"Distinct conversations with Fardid, Shayegan, Nasr, and El-Bizri; no single lineage is claimed."},{"url":"/paths/sufi-heidegger-after-corbin/#film-art","title":"Film and art / Sufi pathway","type":"Pathway","description":"BeyngLab resonances with Kiarostami, Bayzai, Farmanfarmaian, and Neshat, without claims of direct influence.","keywords":["Kiarostami","Bayzai","Farmanfarmaian","Neshat","cinema","mirror","Logic of the Birds"],"text":"BeyngLab resonances with Kiarostami, Bayzai, Farmanfarmaian, and Neshat, without claims of direct influence."},{"url":"/paths/sufi-heidegger-after-corbin/#return","title":"Return to practice / Sufi pathway","type":"Pathway","description":"Describe an image, name what it withholds, and keep a participant’s objection with the revised claim.","keywords":["workshop","Ta'wil Portal","risk"],"text":"Describe an image, name what it withholds, and keep a participant’s objection with the revised claim."},{"url":"/plates/three-heideggers-helyx/","title":"Three Heideggers Helyx","type":"Plate","description":"Three recurring coils of care, history of Being, and dwelling, with crossings and dangers.","keywords":["Helyx","early","middle","late","Dasein","Ereignis","fourfold"],"text":"01 / PLATE Visual argument / version 1.0 Three Heideggers Helyx. Care, the history of Being, and dwelling recur as interwoven questions. The plate proposes crossings among them rather than a line of intellectual progress. Plate 01 / Three reading coils. The crossings are editorial, not a chronology of supersession. Download SVG ↧ Read the plate The argument in words. Care / 1927–33. Begin with Dasein already involved in a world. The question is not the isolated subject’s control but its situated concern. The danger is heroic authenticity. History of Being / 1933–45. Ask how a historical clearing orders what can appear. The danger is mythopolitical capture; Heidegger’s political entanglement belongs to the reading. Thing and dwelling / 1945–76. Attend to gathering and place. The danger is poetic quietism that omits labor and conflict. Crossings. Read “world” through all three. A later coil reopens rather than resolves the earlier question. Use the Turns and Topoi to follow a crossing. LEGEND Large circles = recurrent reading orientations. Curved paths = conceptual crossings. Rust labels = capture risks, carried into any application. Sources & status Primary Being and Time (GA 2) · Contributions to Philosophy · Building Dwelling Thinking . Experimental Periodization, loop geometry, and dangers are BeyngLab editorial synthesis of the AIR V report. Revised 28 September 2026."},{"url":"/plates/regional-daseins-atlas/","title":"Regional Daseins Atlas","type":"Plate","description":"A visual map from D0 through attunement to six overlapping disclosure grammars, each paired with its danger.","keywords":["D0","imaginal","metanoetic","pluriversal","mythic","pharmacological","cosmotechnical"],"text":"02 / PLATE Visual argument / version 1.0 Regional Daseins Atlas. A pause precedes the categories. Six grammars are shown as overlapping ways of reading an encounter, each with a risk. No card stands for a nation, ethnicity, or sealed tradition. Plate 02 / A method and six lenses, not a map of peoples. Download SVG ↧ Read the plate The argument in words. D0. Pause and ask what already acts in the situation. This interval is optional and unscored. Attunement → worlding → dwelling. Notice felt orientation, trace how significance is made, then ask how a way of living holds or fails. Six lenses. Imaginal asks how an image appears and mediates a world; Metanoetic reads a turn after failure; Pluriversal reads relational territory; Mythic-Traditionalist diagnoses mythic hunger; Pharmacological reads technics and attention; Cosmotechnical reads plural technical worlds. Paired capture. Respectively: dehistoricization, guilt capture, romantic localism, mythopolitical hierarchy, therapeutic technocracy, comparative essentialism. Select multiple lenses; let partners revise or reject them. LEGEND Blue path = a proposed method. Six cards = overlapping operations. Rust line = danger, printed on the same card. Connections do not claim descent or a fixed geography. Sources & status Experimental The Atlas synthesizes the AIR V report into a revisable design instrument. Its named thinkers orient further reading; the labels are not their self-descriptions. Revised 28 September 2026."},{"url":"/plates/sufi-heidegger-after-corbin/","title":"Sufi Heidegger after Corbin","type":"Plate","description":"A branching map from Heidegger and Corbin to disputed afterlives, film and art resonances, and a workshop return.","keywords":["Sufi Heidegger","Corbin","Fardid","Shayegan","Nasr","El-Bizri","Kiarostami","Bayzai","Farmanfarmaian","Neshat","ta'wil"],"text":"03 / PLATE Visual argument / version 1.0 After Corbin: branches, not a lineage. This plate traces a question through Corbin’s imaginal and into different afterlives. The film and art row is a set of BeyngLab reading resonances, not proof of influence. Plate 03 / Reception, comparison, and resonance are visibly separated. Download SVG ↧ Read the plate The argument in words. Question and hinge. Heideggerian worldhood and hermeneutics meet Corbin’s turn to Suhrawardi, ta’wil, and the imaginal. The relation is a historical reception to research, not a merger of traditions. Disputed afterlives. Fardid raises mythopolitical danger; Shayegan’s self-critique complicates dualism; Nasr is an adjacent traditionalist line; El-Bizri invites comparison with Avicenna and phenomenology. These are distinct routes. Film and art. Kiarostami, Bayzai, Farmanfarmaian, and Neshat are proposed close-reading encounters. The plate marks them Experimental because direct influence is not asserted. Return. The Ta’wil Portal asks what an image discloses and what this reading obscures. Attach a risk and an objection before making a design claim. LEGEND Solid blue = historical hinge to investigate. Thin blue = editorial comparison. Dashed blue = BeyngLab resonance. Rust return = danger and revision. The arrows organize questions; they do not prove influence. Sources & status Historical Corbin’s reception is the report’s point of departure. Interpretive Intellectual branches need their own bibliography before individual articles. Experimental The film/art pairings and geometry are BeyngLab proposals. Read the AIR V report . Revised 28 September 2026."},{"url":"/workshops/","title":"Workshops & prototypes","type":"Workshop","description":"Two runnable exercises—the Ta’wil Portal and a two-lens Kiarostami Film Lab—plus three scoped prototypes.","keywords":["Ta'wil Portal","D0","workshop","cards","Mirror Garden","Kiarostami","Film Lab","Risk Ledger","prototype"],"text":"W1 / PRACTICE Workshops / prototype bench Try a reading. Keep the objection. Two exercises are ready for a small group or a solo session. Both move from description to interpretation and ask whose voice the reading omits. The remaining prototypes are sketches. PROTOTYPE 01 / RUNNABLE Ta’wil Portal 30 minutes · 1–6 people Bring an image, text, object, or place. Use paper or the worksheet below. A facilitator may read the prompts aloud. Scope This exercise borrows a question from the Imaginal grammar . It does not confer authority in a living religious practice or reproduce ta’wil as practiced in its own traditions. 03′ / D0 Pause at the threshold. Look without naming a solution. What has already arranged this encounter? Anyone may skip the pause. 05′ / FORM Describe what is here. Record visible or audible features without immediately explaining what they mean. Include material, setting, maker, and access where known. 07′ / WORLD Follow what it opens. What relations, histories, or ways of attending become possible through it? Offer more than one reading if the group differs. 07′ / WITHHELD Ask what is absent. Whose interpretation is missing? What labor, institution, or conflict would a beautiful reading make hard to see? 08′ / RETURN Revise the claim. State a provisional insight, a danger, and a counter-practice. Invite a partner’s correction; keep disagreement in the record. Optional local worksheet Keep a trace of your reading. Notes stay in this browser until you download or print them; this site has no account or submission endpoint. Object, image, text, or place Visible form and context Worlds it might open What is withheld or contested Revised claim, danger, and counter-practice Download notes ↧ Print this page ↗ W2 / RUNNABLE · 35 MINUTES Road, Voice, Death Film Lab Choose one hilltop passage in The Wind Will Carry Us . Test it through Imaginal and Pharmacological readings, exchange objections, and download a revised claim. Bring a film copy, or label a source-based rehearsal. Run the Film Lab ↗ Prototype bench / next iterations Three further moves. 03 / CARDS Regional Dasein Cards Use two or more grammars on one situation. Ask a participant to correct the fit. Risk: assigning a whole people to one card. 04 / SPACE Mirror Garden Studio Use fragment, geometry, and bodily position to map divided perspectives. Risk: reducing Farmanfarmaian’s work to a decorative pattern. 05 / REVISION Risk Ledger Add a danger, an objection, and a counter-practice to an Atlas or plate claim. Risk: treating the ledger as a box to check. ANOTHER RUNNABLE EXERCISE Sixfold your fourfold : make an EPS cosmogram from four scenes, choose one thing, and test what it gathers across plural worlds. What to publish after a workshop Keep the revised claim, its evidence, the named risk, and participant corrections with consent. Do not publish participants’ notes automatically. Experimental These exercises are BeyngLab prototypes grounded in the AIR V report, paired with the Sufi Heidegger plate and the Ereignis archive . BREAKDOWN / MAKING CURES What happens when a gathering no longer holds? Read Breakdown alongside Sixfold , then try the Making Cures workshop : map one situation, change an arrangement together, and return to its effects."},{"url":"/workshops/#tawil-portal","title":"Ta’wil Portal / 30-minute workshop","type":"Workshop","description":"A runnable sequence: pause, describe form, follow worlds, name what is withheld, and revise with objection.","keywords":["D0","ta'wil","worksheet","prototype"],"text":"A runnable sequence: pause, describe form, follow worlds, name what is withheld, and revise with objection."},{"url":"/workshops/#breakdown-crossing","title":"Sixfold / Making Cures","type":"Workshop","description":"Breakdown, collaborative making, and revisable shared worlds.","keywords":["breakdown","making cures"],"text":"Breakdown, collaborative making, and revisable shared worlds."},{"url":"/workshops/film-lab/","title":"Road, Voice, Death Film Lab","type":"Workshop","description":"A runnable 35-minute Kiarostami exercise: describe one hilltop scene, compare Imaginal and Pharmacological readings, keep objections, and export notes locally.","keywords":["Abbas Kiarostami","The Wind Will Carry Us","film","Imaginal","Pharmacological","Stiegler","Corbin","phone","offscreen","workshop","worksheet"],"text":"W2 / FILM LAB Road, Voice, Death / 35 minutes One film. Two readings. Choose one hilltop return in Abbas Kiarostami’s The Wind Will Carry Us (1999). A visiting filmmaker climbs for a mobile signal near a worker we hear but do not see. Describe that scene, let two Atlas grammars change the question, then keep the objections. Run the exercise ↘ The shared situation A call above the village. A crew has come from Tehran to a Kurdish village to document an anticipated funeral. The woman remains alive. The phone pulls the visitor uphill for reception; nearby, a digger works out of sight. The film’s camera and the crew’s proposed recording sit inside the story’s question about whose life becomes an image. [1] [2] Before reading: Watch one such return in a copy of the film you can access. Editions may differ, so record your own time mark. If you only have the published descriptions linked below, mark your notes “source-based rehearsal”; do not claim to have analyzed shots you have not seen. Two questions / one scene What changes with the lens? These are BeyngLab comparisons, not accounts of Kiarostami’s stated influences. A1 / IMAGINAL What does the unseen make active? The digger’s voice matters before his body appears. The woman’s life organizes the visitors’ waiting while remaining outside their frame. Ask what the film makes perceptible through hearing, delay, and withholding. Its objection An unseen person is not evidence of Corbin’s ontological imaginal. Akbar warns that our imagined access to others’ lives can repeat the crew’s presumption. [3] Read the Imaginal film resonance ↗ A5 / PHARMACOLOGICAL What does the support arrange? The phone connects the visitor to work elsewhere and sends him uphill. The planned camera work makes a death into a deadline. Ask how these supports coordinate attention, preserve something, and divert care. [4] Its objection The phone does not create the crew’s purpose. Brody also reads communication and cinema as possibilities for contact and freedom, complicating a simple account of technical harm. [2] Read the Pharmacological encounter ↗ Experimental A provisional joint claim: the film lets a remote deadline, local labor, and the woman’s living time interrupt one another. One lens attends to what remains beyond the image; the other to the tools and institutions directing attention. Test both claims against the scene and against someone else’s account. W2 / RUNNABLE EXERCISE Make the second reading answer the first. 35 minutes · 1–6 people Bring access to the film, or use the linked sources as a clearly labeled rehearsal. Use paper or the local worksheet. A facilitator can read the prompts aloud. Scope These are reading questions, not a claim that Kiarostami followed Corbin or Stiegler. A grammar belongs to a revisable interpretation; it cannot be assigned to a people or region. 05′ / WATCH Choose one return. Play a hilltop phone-call passage. Note the edition and time mark. If the film is unavailable, read the linked descriptions and label the exercise provisional. 05′ / DESCRIBE Keep form ahead of theory. Record the camera’s position, sound, visible work, phone movement, and what you can only infer. Separate observed detail from a critic’s account. 06′ / IMAGINAL Ask after the unseen. What does a voice, delay, or absence make active in the scene? Where would this reading pretend to know a person it cannot see? 06′ / PHARMACOLOGICAL Follow the supports. What does phone or camera enable, and what demand organizes its use? Include the digger’s labor and the crew’s assignment, then name one possible benefit the critique might miss. 06′ / OBJECT Swap counter-readings. Have someone challenge each lens. Which villager’s account is unavailable? Which part of the film refuses the opposition between good and bad technology? 07′ / REVISE Leave a trace of disagreement. Write one claim that uses both lenses, cite one scene detail, preserve both objections, and state what further viewing or testimony would change your mind. Optional local worksheet Keep both readings visible. Notes remain in this browser until you download or print them. BeyngLab receives no submission. Evidence basis Choose your basis I watched the film scene Source-based rehearsal; I did not view the scene Film copy and time mark What the shot and sound give Imaginal question and claim Pharmacological question and claim Two objections and absent accounts Revised claim and further evidence Download notes ↧ Print this page ↗ Evidence and status Read beside the film. Primary Janus Films, The Wind Will Carry Us (1999), distributor record Premise and unseen figures; the film itself is the primary work for scene observations. Interpretive Richard Brody, “DVD of the Week: The Wind Will Carry Us,” The New Yorker , 2010 Hilltop phone and digger, with an emphasis on the openings of communication and cinema. Interpretive Kaveh Akbar, “The Wind Will Carry Us: Dust to Dust,” The Criterion Collection , 2025 Offscreen lives, labor, and the ethics of the visitor’s and viewer’s gaze. Primary Bernard Stiegler, “Relational Ecology and the Digital Pharmakon,” trans. Patrick Crogan, Culture Machine 13 (2012) Technics, external memory, attention, and care. Applying this text to the film is BeyngLab’s experiment. Primary Henry Corbin, “Mundus Imaginalis,” in Swedenborg and Esoteric Islam , trans. Leonard Fox Corbin’s ontological imaginal cannot be inferred from an offscreen device in cinema. Experimental The two-lens pairing, timing, questions, and provisional joint claim belong to BeyngLab. They are open to correction."},{"url":"/workshops/film-lab/#scene","title":"Film Lab / shared hilltop scene","type":"Workshop","description":"A phone call and unseen digger in The Wind Will Carry Us provide one situation for two readings.","keywords":["Kiarostami","hilltop","phone","digger"],"text":"A phone call and unseen digger in The Wind Will Carry Us provide one situation for two readings."},{"url":"/workshops/film-lab/#two-readings","title":"Film Lab / Imaginal and Pharmacological","type":"Workshop","description":"Side-by-side questions and objections on one scene, with separate links to both Atlas grammars.","keywords":["Corbin","Stiegler","comparison","regional grammars"],"text":"Side-by-side questions and objections on one scene, with separate links to both Atlas grammars."},{"url":"/workshops/film-lab/#run-lab","title":"Film Lab / 35-minute exercise","type":"Workshop","description":"Watch, describe, read through two lenses, exchange objections, revise, and download local notes.","keywords":["workshop","worksheet","film","revision"],"text":"Watch, describe, read through two lenses, exchange objections, revise, and download local notes."},{"url":"/workshops/film-lab/#sources","title":"Film Lab / evidence and status","type":"Workshop","description":"Film record, critics, Stiegler and Corbin texts, and the experimental pairing remain distinct.","keywords":["sources","bibliography","provenance"],"text":"Film record, critics, Stiegler and Corbin texts, and the experimental pairing remain distinct."},{"url":"/cosmograms/","title":"Cosmograms: Pop Fourfolds & Sixfolding","type":"Studio exercise","description":"Make an existential positioning system from four scenes, then sixfold a concrete thing through earth, sky, mortals, divinities, thing, and world.","keywords":["cosmogram","pop fourfold","EPS","Existential Positioning System","Ulmer","popcycle","sixfold","earth","sky","mortals","divinities","thing","world","StudioLab"],"text":"C1 / MAKE Cosmograms / pop fourfolds / EPS Sixfold your fourfold. Make a small map of the scenes that orient you. Pick one real thing. Turn it through earth, sky, mortals, and divinities; then ask what the thing gathers and which worlds let that gathering happen. You can start with a single thing and return to your four scenes later. Start with one thing ↘ See an example ↘ Three related moves Begin in a life. Test it in a world. 01 / ULMER · EPS Pop fourfold Recall a scene from each of four fields: family; community/history; popular culture; school/career. An Existential Positioning System asks how institutions, memories, hopes, and attractions orient a life. Your fields can change: street, religion, sport, or another scene may matter more. 02 / HEIDEGGER · FOURFOLD Gathering Read a particular thing through earth, sky, mortals, and divinities. These are related dimensions of dwelling, not four cultural bins or four boxes that correspond to Ulmer’s fields. “Divinities” remains a question about what calls and withdraws; no belief is required. 03 / BEYNGLAB · SIXFOLD Thing ↔ World Make the thing and its world explicit. What gathers here? What wider histories, institutions, ecologies, and platforms make this gathering possible or impossible? Tilt the map with another person’s account or a material constraint, then revise it. Experimental This crossing is a BeyngLab exercise. Heidegger did not name a sixfold, and Ulmer’s EPS is not a translation of Heidegger’s fourfold. Read the Thing / Fourfold Topos and its archive passages. QUICK START / ONE ORDINARY THING A table can open the exercise. Start with an actual table you know. Give it a place and a history before you give it a theory. You can do this in ten minutes, with a pen or with the form below. Four scenes. A family meal; a community meeting; a table in a film; a desk at work. One scene or image from each field is enough. Fourfold. Earth: wood, food, hands, repair. Sky: light, seasons, time of day. Mortals: who eats, works, cleans, or is absent. Divinities / Other(s): hospitality, memory, faith, obligation, or a hope that exceeds the plan. Sixfold. Thing: what this table actually gathers and where it fails. World: housing, supply chains, institutions, customs, and other tables in other lives. Move a chair or invite someone who has not spoken. What changes? Your own / 10–20 minutes Make, turn, and test. No right answer and no score. Short phrases work. Your entries stay on this device until you download or print them; the site does not collect them. 01 Gather four scenes Use Ulmer’s popcycle as a starting grid. Recall one object, image, event, or phrase in each field. Leave a field blank if it does not fit; name a better one in your note. Family What was handed to you or held around you? Community / history What place or public event shaped your position? Popular culture What story, image, sound, or game drew you in? School / career Where did you learn what counts as knowledge or work? 02 Turn one thing through a fourfold Choose a concrete thing from one of those scenes, or one near you now. Walk around it; touch, listen, or trace its materials where possible. The prompts are lenses, not definitions. Your thing Earth Material, land, bodies, labor, wear, repair? Sky Light, weather, season, duration, possibility? Mortals Who uses, cares, makes, loses, or cannot enter? Divinities / Other(s) What calls, matters, exceeds control, or stays absent? 03 Sixfold and put it under pressure Thing and World make the gathering and its conditions visible. They work across the four terms; they are not two more slots of the same kind. Thing / the gathering What comes together in this particular thing, and what frays? World(s) / the field Which histories, institutions, platforms, ecologies, or other worlds pattern it? Stress / another reading Move it, change a condition, or ask another person. What does your map miss? YOUR REVISABLE COSMOGRAM A thing in a world This diagram repeats your notes. It is a draft for conversation, never an identity label. Family A scene can begin here. Community / history A scene can begin here. Popular culture A scene can begin here. School / career A scene can begin here. WORLD(S) / FIELD What worlds pattern the gathering? Earth Material and resistance Sky Time and conditions Mortals Finite lives and care Divinities / Other(s) Calls and absences THING / GATHERING What gathers and what frays? TURN / TEST / RETURN Ask another person or change a material condition, then revise. Download your notes ↧ Print the cosmogram ↗ Clear the form FROM PERSONAL TO SHARED Invite a partner to draw the same thing from a different position. Put the two cosmograms next to each other. Keep the mismatch visible: another world may gather a different thing here. Ask who can change the material conditions, who carries the cost, and what your first map made disappear. Sources & method Primary Heidegger’s “The Thing” (GA 7; Poetry, Language, Thought , p. 176) names earth, sky, divinities, and mortals. Ulmer His Konsult account of EPS and the popcycle names family, community/history, entertainment, and career/profession; see also his EPS/GPS distinction . StudioLab / BeyngLab The four-scene exercise follows the StudioLab workshop sheet and “Cosmography via LifeLines” . The Thing/World sixfold turn and stress test are a BeyngLab extension. No entry is saved or sent to BeyngLab. BREAKDOWN / MAKING CURES What happens when a gathering no longer holds? Read Breakdown alongside Sixfold , then try the Making Cures workshop : map one situation, change an arrangement together, and return to its effects."},{"url":"/cosmograms/#three-moves","title":"Pop fourfold and Sixfold / method","type":"Studio exercise","description":"Ulmer’s four scenes, Heidegger’s fourfold, and BeyngLab’s Thing/World extension remain distinct moves.","keywords":["EPS","popcycle","Geviert","method"],"text":"Ulmer’s four scenes, Heidegger’s fourfold, and BeyngLab’s Thing/World extension remain distinct moves."},{"url":"/cosmograms/#quick-start","title":"Sixfold example / a table","type":"Studio exercise","description":"A worked ordinary thing: family and public scenes, earth, sky, mortals, divinities, thing, and world.","keywords":["example","table","cosmogram"],"text":"A worked ordinary thing: family and public scenes, earth, sky, mortals, divinities, thing, and world."},{"url":"/cosmograms/#make-cosmogram","title":"Make your own cosmogram","type":"Studio exercise","description":"Fill a local worksheet, watch a live Sixfold diagram, then download or print your provisional reading.","keywords":["sixfold","worksheet","download","print"],"text":"Fill a local worksheet, watch a live Sixfold diagram, then download or print your provisional reading."},{"url":"/cosmograms/#breakdown-crossing","title":"Sixfold / Making Cures","type":"Studio exercise","description":"Breakdown, collaborative making, and revisable shared worlds.","keywords":["breakdown","making cures"],"text":"Breakdown, collaborative making, and revisable shared worlds."},{"url":"/essays/wiseacre-at-law/","title":"The Wiseacre at Law","type":"Wiseacre essay","description":"A reading of Doug Wise’s constitutional hermeneutics through Schürmann’s double bind and Lyotard’s judgment without final criteria.","keywords":["Doug Wise","jurisprudence","textualism","Schürmann","Lyotard","Wiseacre","anarchy"],"text":"W1 / WISEACRE Hot House / Wiseacre essay The Wiseacre at Law Anarchy, Just Gaming, and the Performance of Jurisprudence BeyngLab working paper · September 2026 · Text edition from the working manuscript Return to Doug Wise and the Wiseacre ↗ IN THIS ESSAY The Jurisprudential Wiseacre From Automaticity and Plasticity to the Double Bind Lyotard and the Play of Justice Wisdom Wisedumb and the Wiseacre Law Performs a World Objections and Limits Conclusion This is a text edition of a working draft. Paragraphs and page notes follow the source manuscript. Figures are omitted here. MANUSCRIPT / 01 Abstract Doug Wise’s essay “Two Hermeneutical Motives in Reading the Constitution” translates the American dispute between textualism and pragmatism into a conflict between automaticity and plasticity. Read by itself, this translation risks turning jurisprudential disagreement into a choice between stable rules and adaptive judgment. Read alongside Reiner Schürmann’s account of the tragic double bind and Jean-François Lyotard’s account of judging without criteria, however, Wise’s intervention opens a more radical question: how can law establish a common world while remaining answerable to singular claims that its categories cannot recognize? This essay argues that jurisprudence operates under two irreducible demands. Law must contextualize, classify, and stabilize; justice must also expose every context, classification, and settlement to interruption. Schürmann names this conflict an-archic because no higher principle can reconcile its terms. Lyotard names its practice just gaming: judgment case by case without a final metalanguage. To clarify what follows for jurisprudence, the essay develops three figures: wisdom inhabits the double bind, wisedumb converts it into a single rule, and the wiseacre interrupts every rule that mistakes its historical authority for the order of being. Law, on this account, does not merely regulate a world. It performs one. Keywords: jurisprudence; Heidegger; Doug Wise; Reiner Schürmann; Jean-François Lyotard; anarchy; double bind; textualism; pragmatism; performance The Jurisprudential Wiseacre Doug Wise does not approach Heidegger as the custodian of a finished doctrine. His essays carry Heideggerian concepts into evolutionary biology, thermodynamics, topology, semiotics, film, constitutional interpretation, and other domains that professional Heidegger scholarship often treats as external applications. This practice does not simply illustrate Heidegger. It places Heideggerian thinking at risk. A concept must work outside the protected climate of its commentary, or its failure must teach us something. The name for this practice is wiseacring. The wiseacre interrupts respectable intelligence with an impertinent question. He notices where a system’s official explanation ceases to describe what the system actually does. He treats disciplinary boundaries less as prohibitions than as invitations to trespass. The wiseacre may get the joke wrong, force an analogy, or press a concept past its warranted range. Yet these dangers belong to the experiment. Without such risk, wisdom congeals into the recitation of wisdom. Wise’s “Two Hermeneutical Motives in Reading the Constitution” offers an unusually concrete example.1 Its occasion is Stephen Breyer’s defense of pragmatic interpretation against textualism and 1 Doug C. Wise, “Two Hermeneutical Motives in Reading the Constitution ,” May 11, 2024. Wise’s piece is an online essay rather than a peer-reviewed law review article. This status matters: it should be read as an experimental Heideggerian intervention into jurisprudence, not as a comprehensive account of contemporary statutory or constitutional interpretation. MANUSCRIPT / 02 originalism in Reading the Constitution. 2 Its subject, however, is not simply which interpretive method judges should adopt. Wise asks what kind of world must already have disclosed itself for textualism or pragmatism to appear compelling. “Tell me what is your stance on interpreting,” his essay begins, “and I’ll tell you who you are.” The sentence relocates jurisprudence. A theory of interpretation becomes a way of existing in a legal world. Wise begins with John Manning’s influential account of the difference between textualism and purposivism. Textualists privilege semantic context: how a reasonable reader, conversant with the relevant conventions, would understand the enacted language. Purposivists allow evidence of policy purpose to overcome even strong semantic cues when a literal reading would frustrate the mischief the law addresses.3 Manning defends textualism as the method most respectful of legislative supremacy and compromise. The words that survive the legislative process mark the bargain that achieved enactment; judges who relax their semantic constraints may replace that bargain with their reconstruction of what the legislature ought to have wanted. Wise redescribes this controversy through David Bates’s polarity of automaticity and plasticity. Textualism values the reliable execution of instructions. It seeks rules judges can apply repeatedly, publicly, and with limited discretion. In Wise’s cybernetic language, the judge becomes a dependable component or servo-mechanism within a constitutional system. Pragmatism instead emphasizes adjustment. Because technologies, institutions, habits, and meanings change, interpretation must remain capable of modifying its operations. It must assess purposes and consequences, learn from feedback, and sometimes break with inherited instructions. The pairing is illuminating because it reveals the temporal commitments hidden inside interpretive methods. Textualism protects continuity against the retrospective invention of purposes. Pragmatism protects responsiveness against the mechanical survival of rules whose worlds have disappeared. But Wise’s pairing also raises a problem. If jurisprudence becomes a choice between automaticity and plasticity, then one might imagine an optimal setting somewhere between them: enough rule for predictability, enough flexibility for change. The conflict would become an engineering problem. The system would require calibration rather than judgment. Wise himself prevents that easy solution. He suggests that textualism and pragmatism express different Grundbefindlichkeiten, fundamental ways of finding oneself in and being affected by a world. The interpreter does not first encounter neutral legal materials and then select a method. What counts as fidelity, danger, arbitrariness, democratic legitimacy, or institutional survival has already acquired significance. Beneath the published rationale lie what Benjamin Cardozo called the forces that make the judge, and what Heidegger calls Befindlichkeit: the always already affective disclosure of a situation.4 This suggestion should not be mistaken for a causal psychology of judges. Wise has not established that textualists possess one temperament and pragmatists another, nor would such a diagnosis decide the legal merits of either position. His intervention works at another level. It asks jurisprudence to account for the world-disclosing commitments its technical arguments presuppose. That question leads from Wise to Schürmann. 2 Stephen Breyer, Reading the Constitution: Why I Chose Pragmatism, Not Textualism (New York: Simon & Schuster, 2024). 3 John F. Manning, “What Divides Textualists from Purposivists?” Columbia Law Review 106, no. 1 (2006): 70–111, especially 91–110. 4 Wise, “Two Hermeneutical Motives.” Wise cites Benjamin N. Cardozo, The Nature of the Judicial Process (New Haven, CT: Yale University Press, 1921), 127, and Katherine Withy’s account of Befinden and Grundbefinden. MANUSCRIPT / 03 From Automaticity and Plasticity to the Double Bind Reiner Schürmann’s work changes the structure of the dispute. In Heidegger on Being and Acting, he asks what becomes of action once thinking can no longer provide it with a secure first principle. Western practical philosophy repeatedly derives action from an archē: an origin that also commands, a foundation that also rules. A highest good, a divine order, rational nature, autonomous subjectivity, historical progress, or another hegemonic principle supplies both the intelligibility of a world and the norm by which conduct within it is measured. Schürmann calls action an-archic when the authority of such ultimate referents withers.5 An-archy does not mean that practices suddenly proceed "},{"url":"/essays/wiseacre-at-the-edge-of-nothing/","title":"Wiseacre at the Edge of Nothing","type":"Wiseacre essay","description":"A StudioLab/BeyngLab essay about Doug Wise’s Wiseacre, D0, queer science fiction, deconstraint, and AI worlding.","keywords":["Doug Wise","D0","gay sci-fi","Woesean humanities","AI worlding","poiesis","Wiseacre","deconstraint"],"text":"W2 / WISEACRE Hot House / Wiseacre essay Wiseacre at the Edge of Nothing D0, Gay Sci-Fi, Woesean Poiesis, and AI StudioLab / BeyngLab draft · Text edition from the working manuscript Return to Doug Wise and the Wiseacre ↗ IN THIS ESSAY 1. Thesis: D0 Turns Melancholy Into Gay Sci-Fi 2. From Representation to Ontological Genre Mechanics 3. Woesean Humanities: After the Tree, Before the World 4. D0: The Gap That Resists Completion 5. Wiseacre: Doug Wise's Comic-Ontological Station 6. Gay Melancholy, Lifedeath, and the Queer Gap 7. AI as Lateral-Transfer Machine and Capture System 8. CRISPR, Code, and Deconstraint 9. Language as Mitochondrion: Metabolism, Not Representation 10. Paranoid Comedy and the Anti-Doom Machine 11. StudioLab, BeyngLab, and the Practice of Cosmography 12. D-Stations as Gay Sci-Fi Apparatus 13. The Wiseacre Poster as Theory Machine 14. Toward a Woesean Gay AI Poetics 15. Implications for Writing, Teaching, and Making 16. Conclusion: From the Abyss, We Poet References Appendix table This is a text edition of a working draft. Paragraphs and page notes follow the source manuscript. Figures are omitted here. MANUSCRIPT / 03 Figure 1. Wiseacre info comic / theory machine, used here as the visual hinge for the essay. Abstract This essay argues that gay sci-fi can become one of the most powerful contemporary engines for transmediating nihilism into poiesis because it occupies a privileged crossing of four forces: D0, the zero-degree gap where world has not yet hardened into identity, genre, platform, or program; Woesean humanities, a post-arboreal account of life, inheritance, lateral transfer, and domain-shifting; genre mechanics, the practical knowledge by which forms mutate through tropes, plots, interfaces, and fandoms; and AI, the newest machinic medium of recombination, completion, hallucination, extraction, and world-making. Doug Wise's Wiseacre station in BeyngLab supplies the crucial comic-ontological hinge. Wise's writings on Angst, the thick sublime, CRISPR, deconstraint, language as the mitochondrion of Being, ontological recognition, entropitarianism, and paranoid comedy show how dread can disclose the nothing without surrendering to it. D0 turns gay melancholy into gay sci-fi by holding the void as a poietic gap: not absence but the deepest possibility. Wiseacre tends this gap with jokes, dread, scissors, dopamine, and world-maintenance. The result is not simply representation of queer characters in futurist settings. It is a theory-practice of queer worlding after the tree, after the closet, after the humanist subject, and after the prompt that thinks every silence should be filled. 1. Thesis: D0 Turns Melancholy Into Gay Sci-Fi D0 turns gay melancholy to gay sci-fi. It turns nihilism and void to poiesis. That formula is the kernel of this essay, and it must be protected from two tempting reductions. First, it is not an optimistic slogan saying that loss simply becomes creativity. Gay melancholy is not a mood to be cured by progress, recognition, representation, or technological novelty. It names the sedimented lifedeath of AIDS, the closet, institutional exclusion, public shame, erotic failure, family rupture, vanished subcultures, unbuilt futures, and archives whose traces are often fragmentary because the world did not care to preserve them. Second, the formula is not a productivity imperative. It does not mean: turn trauma into content; turn void into output; turn queerness into a marketable style; turn the prompt into a world. It means something slower and more dangerous: melancholy can enter D0, the gap or no-thing where meanings loosen, and there become available for poiesis. D0 is not another stage in a linear developmental sequence. It is the zero-degree condition that allows the other stations to appear, mutate, fail, and reappear. D1 gives ritual charge, soul, initiation, liminal intensity. D2 gives subject, identity, law, recognition, rights, and the coming-out narrative. D3 gives critique, deconstruction, Dasein, hermeneutic breakage, and the slantwise labor of refusing capture. D4 gives open-world cosmography, plural dwelling, transmedia platforms, and collective world-design. D0 is different. It is the clearing, gap, Dao, abyss, pause, unprogrammed interval, or not-yet through which these formations become possible. It is not the void as dead end. It is the void before coding, before recognition, before genre, before the machine completes the sentence. That is why D0 matters so much in the age of AI. Contemporary generative systems are extraordinary completion machines. They convert gaps into probable continuations. They make text where there was silence, image where there was blankness, answer where there was uncertainty. This capacity is wondrous and dangerous at once. If every ambiguity is completed, every archive scraped, every dream statistically regenerated, then the gap itself is colonized. D0 names the refusal MANUSCRIPT / 04 to let machine completion become metaphysics. It says that not every queer silence should be answered, not every lost scene should be reconstructed, not every desire should be classified, not every world should be scaled. Gay sci-fi, then, is not merely the representation of gay people in speculative settings. It is an ontological and genre-mechanical conversion process. It takes the melancholy produced by straight worlds, normative kinship, disciplinary reading machines, and performative optimization and runs it through the gap until it becomes an engine of alternative worlding. The result is neither pure consolation nor pure critique. It is poiesis: the making of worlds, forms, figures, interfaces, rituals, and stories that can carry queer life beyond the closed economy of loss. This move draws directly on StudioLab's long effort to shuttle between performance, transmedia knowledge, collaborative platforms, and cosmography. In the 2022 interview \"Mise en abyme,\" StudioLab is described as operating in an unsettling world of pandemic, climate change, mutation, spillover, digitality, and performativity; the general theory of performance is not simply an object to be studied but an atmosphere requiring intervention, critique, embodiment, and also \"nothing, letting be\" (Cheng, Hay, Willis, and McKenzie 2022). That small phrase is the hinge. The project is not only to perform differently, but also to let the gap remain open long enough for another performance, another medium, another world to arrive. Doug Wise's Wiseacre station gives this hinge a comic body. Wiseacre is the part of BeyngLab where the nothing does not become a solemn doom cult. It is tended with conceptual slapstick, ontological jokes, philosophical beasts, genetic scissors, dopamine loops, and thick-sublime dread. Wise's work shows how the gap can be disclosed without being worshiped, how nihilism can be recognized without being obeyed, and how melancholy can be metabolized without being denied. Wiseacre's motto might be: the void giggles and becomes world. 2. From Representation to Ontological Genre Mechanics The most familiar way to approach gay sci-fi is through representation: which characters appear, how they are named, whether desire is explicit, whether the story allows queer kinship, whether queer people survive. This remains necessary. Representation matters because invisibility and stereotype have real effects. Yet representation is not enough for the task at hand. The question is not only whether gay characters are present inside a sci-fi world. It is whether gay sci-fi can mutate the assumptions by which a world counts as a world. Genre is a machine for doing this. Science fiction works by selective mutation. It changes a premise, device, body, environment, law, technology, kinship system, or cosmic scale, then observes what else must change. Darko Suvin famously described science fiction through cognitive estrangement: a novum makes the ordinary appear strange and opens a systematic field of inquiry (Suvin 1979). Fredric Jameson later treated science fiction as a privileged mode for thinking historical possibility, not because it predicts the future but because it makes the present available to cognition through imagined difference (Jameson 2005). From a StudioLab perspective, these genre mechanics are not simply literary. They are design methods. They generate constraints, test alternatives, build worlds, and expose what a culture mistakes for nature. MANUSCRIPT / 05 Gay sci-fi radicalizes "},{"url":"/topoi/call/","title":"Call / Ruf","type":"Topos","description":"Begin with the call of conscience in Being and Time. Follow its unsettling relation to selfhood and care, then ask what kind of response it makes possible. Keep this analysis distinct from later accounts of the call of being.","keywords":["call","Ruf","Being and Time","conscience","possibility"],"text":"T5 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Who calls? Who can answer? How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say? Begin with the call of conscience in Being and Time. Follow its unsettling relation to selfhood and care, then ask what kind of response it makes possible. Keep this analysis distinct from later accounts of the call of being. Begin with the passages FOLLOW CALL 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail An address without an outside speaker. Two consecutive pages in §57 let us follow the argument without importing a theory of revelation or an inner moral adviser. Read the surrounding section as well as these entry points. 01 / Being and Time §57 Neither planned nor delivered by someone else The call interrupts expectation and volition. Heidegger refuses both an immediate appeal to a divine speaker and a reduction to an objectively present mechanism. The problem concerns the way Dasein is, rather than the identification of an unseen entity. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 320 02 / Being and Time §57 The caller in uncanniness Heidegger locates the caller in Dasein’s not-being-at-home: its thrown existence becomes exposed as familiar assurances recede. This helps explain why what addresses me need not feel like a familiar account of who I am. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 321 02 / Terms and distinctions Address, conscience, uncanniness. Term Reading distinction Ruf / call Track who calls, who is addressed, and what changes in the address. Do not assume a message transmitted between two separate people. Gewissen / conscience The existential analysis needs to be distinguished from a list of moral instructions. Ask how responsibility is opened rather than assuming the call supplies a rule. Unheimlichkeit / uncanniness Not-being-at-home names a disturbance of familiar self-understanding. It is not the name of a cultural identity. Call of being A later vocabulary with its own textual setting. A resemblance of words does not establish an unchanged argument. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Care, method, and a later crossing. WISEACRE / CARE AND NULLITY Doug Wise “The importance of the nothingness of what we care about” connects conscience, care, and nullity through Heidegger, Pippin, and Crowell. Wise’s evolutionary comparison and provocative description of the call are extensions to examine, not substitutes for §57. Read Wise at Ereignis METHOD AND EXPERIENCE Katherine Withy “The Methodological Role of Angst in Being and Time” questions the assumption that anxiety’s philosophical role is exhausted by an existential story about authenticity. Use this reading to test the movement from uncanniness to a claim about how to live. Read Withy on angst A DIFFERENT TEXTUAL SETTING Withy on Antigone “Authenticity and Heidegger’s Antigone” reads responsiveness and reticence in the 1942 Ister lectures. This is a route into a later call of being, not evidence that conscience in 1927 and Antigone in 1942 mean the same thing. Follow the later crossing 04 / Disagreement and risk What makes an address answerable? BeyngLab places these readings together as a question, not as a documented debate between their authors. Does the call disclose responsibility, and what would connect that disclosure to reasons others can contest? An intense feeling of being summoned does not establish that a proposed action is justified. Equally, interpreting the caller as Dasein does not make it a self-command that can be produced at will. Preserve both difficulties when moving from the text to an example. 05 / Reading move Separate the interruption from your explanation. Experimental A BeyngLab reading exercise. 01 Map the address. Read pp. 320–321. Note what the text says about the caller, the one addressed, and the absence of a planned message. 02 Describe a modest interruption. Choose a situation in which an accepted role became questionable. Separate what happened from the story you later gave it. This is a comparison to test, not proof that you experienced Heidegger’s call. 03 Make the response contestable. Write one possible response and one objection from someone affected. Keep the objection beside the response rather than treating the call as its authorization. 06 / Crossings From being addressed to taking up a possibility. T6 / AUTHENTICITY How might a response change a way of existing? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T1 / CARE What is already at stake? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T15 / NOTHINGNESS What happens when familiarity recedes? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, Being and Time (1927), §57, trans. John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (1962), pp. 320–321. These are English edition pages, not GA 2 page numbers. Interpretive Doug Wise, “The importance of the nothingness of what we care about” (2024); Katherine Withy, “The Methodological Role of Angst in Being and Time” and “Authenticity and Heidegger’s Antigone.” Links appear in the reader cards. Experimental The comparison, questions, and exercise are BeyngLab’s. Regional disclosure grammars remain revisable descriptions of operations, not fixed cultural essences."},{"url":"/topoi/call/#archive","title":"Call / Ruf / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/call/#terms","title":"Call / Ruf / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/call/#readers","title":"Call / Ruf / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/call/#disagreement","title":"Call / Ruf / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/call/#practice","title":"Call / Ruf / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/call/#relations","title":"Call / Ruf / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/call/#sources","title":"Call / Ruf / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?","keywords":["call","Ruf"],"text":"How can a call come from me without being something I choose to say?"},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit","type":"Topos","description":"Read authenticity through possibility, finitude, and the retrieval of a past. This path asks how a way of existing changes; it offers no personality test and no ranking of people or cultures.","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit","Being and Time","conscience","possibility"],"text":"T6 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Take up a possibility. Remain answerable. How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world? Read authenticity through possibility, finitude, and the retrieval of a past. This path asks how a way of existing changes; it offers no personality test and no ranking of people or cultures. Begin with the passages FOLLOW AUTHENTICITY 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail Possibility, finite time, and retrieval. These three stops connect the account of care with later analyses of temporality and historicality. Their differences matter: the early description of a structure is not yet the completed argument about authentic existence. 01 / Being and Time §41 Already ahead, already situated Dasein understands itself through possibilities while already thrown into a world. Being-ahead-of-itself is part of this whole, rather than a movement of a detached subject. Start here before treating self-ownership as independence. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 236 02 / Being and Time §65 Finitude changes the question of time Heidegger relates anticipatory resoluteness to a finite future. This is a claim about temporality, not the prediction that clock time will cease when a person dies. Ask how this argument changes what a possibility means. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 378 03 / Being and Time §74 Retrieve without restoring Repetition answers possibilities handed down from the past without simply reinstating their former actuality. The question of inheritance remains directed toward a future. Read this alongside the surrounding chapter’s difficult claims about shared history. Macquarrie–Robinson, p. 438 02 / Terms and distinctions Owning, opening, inheriting. Term Working question Eigentlichkeit / authenticity What changes in how a possibility is taken up? Avoid substituting sincerity, originality, or a supposedly hidden personality. Entschlossenheit / resoluteness How does resolution remain open to a situation? Read McNeill’s warning against hearing only stubborn willpower in the English word. Geworfenheit / thrownness Which conditions and inheritances precede the choice? An account of owning cannot erase these conditions. Wiederholung / repetition Which possibility is retrieved, and what is refused? Distinguish retrieval from nostalgia or compulsory return. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Openness and the limits of a single model. POSSIBILITY AND FINITUDE William McNeill “Rethinking the Possible” stresses the openness in Entschlossenheit that “resoluteness” can obscure. His reading makes finitude central while resisting the picture of a will closed off from possibilities. Consult especially his discussion and note on the translation. Read McNeill at Ereignis LATER AUTHENTICITY Katherine Withy “Authenticity and Heidegger’s Antigone” argues that familiar models of authenticity do not adequately explain Antigone in the Ister lectures. Responsiveness and reticence become central. Compare this later interpretation with the early analysis while preserving the difference of period and text. Read Withy at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk Who gets to name a life authentic? McNeill and Withy supply distinct approaches, not a single formula. BeyngLab asks whether ownership, openness, and responsiveness always support one another—or sometimes pull in different directions. Neither an inherited identity nor a decisive commitment justifies itself by being called authentic. A collective claim to its “true” essence can suppress internal differences. Keep the report’s regional grammars provisional: describe how something becomes intelligible in a situation, then record where that description fails. Authenticity also leaves an ethical question open: how does a chosen possibility affect others? Philosophical descriptions of owning a life do not by themselves settle its political or moral worth. 05 / Reading move Retrieve one possibility, preserve one objection. Experimental An exercise in reading and revision, not a score of personal authenticity. 01 Name the inheritance. Choose a practice you have inherited. List a possibility it enables and a constraint it imposes, including who made it available. 02 Distinguish retrieval from restoration. Read p. 438. Describe what you might take up differently and what you would decline to reproduce. Mark where your example exceeds the passage. 03 Leave the commitment revisable. Write what would make you reconsider it. Include an objection from someone affected. Return to McNeill’s account of openness and ask whether your resolution still permits an answer. 06 / Crossings Call, care, and the world already there. T5 / CALL What interrupts the familiar account of myself? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T1 / CARE How are possibilities already involved? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T2 / WORLD Which shared background makes a choice intelligible? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, Being and Time (1927), §§41, 65, 74, trans. John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (1962), pp. 236, 378, 438. These are English edition pages, not GA 2 pagination. Interpretive William McNeill, “Rethinking the Possible”; Katherine Withy, “Authenticity and Heidegger’s Antigone,” addressing the 1942 Ister lectures (GA 53). Direct links appear above. Experimental BeyngLab’s comparisons and exercise invite correction. They do not classify people, regions, or traditions by degrees of authenticity."},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/#archive","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/#terms","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/#readers","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/#disagreement","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/#practice","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/#relations","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/authenticity/#sources","title":"Authenticity / Eigentlichkeit / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?","keywords":["authenticity","Eigentlichkeit"],"text":"How can existence become one’s own while remaining thrown into a shared world?"},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen","type":"Topos","description":"Follow Building Dwelling Thinking from sparing to gathering and lived space. Keep the poetic account in conversation with questions of access, maintenance, and displacement. Those questions belong to our testing of the text, not to a ready-made housing policy in Heidegger.","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"T8 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Make room. Learn to dwell. What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain? Follow Building Dwelling Thinking from sparing to gathering and lived space. Keep the poetic account in conversation with questions of access, maintenance, and displacement. Those questions belong to our testing of the text, not to a ready-made housing policy in Heidegger. Begin with the passages FOLLOW DWELLING 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail Sparing, gathering, making room. Three entry points into the 1951 lecture, collected in GA 7. The links use Albert Hofstadter’s translation in the 1993 Basic Writings edition; its page numbers differ from Poetry, Language, Thought. 01 / Building Dwelling Thinking Sparing as a positive relation Heidegger treats sparing as more than avoiding damage. Dwelling lets something remain in its own character. The passage introduces earth, sky, divinities, and mortals as belonging together. Basic Writings, p. 351 02 / The bridge A thing gathers The bridge gathers the fourfold in its particular way. Heidegger resists treating this gathering as a symbolic meaning subsequently attached to a finished object. Ask what that changes in the account of a place. Basic Writings, p. 355 03 / Spaces and locales Already involved in space Our stay among things and locales opens spaces. Heidegger’s example of moving toward a door questions the picture of an encapsulated body first contained in a neutral room. Basic Writings, p. 359 02 / Terms and distinctions Keep the differences usable. Term Reading distinction Wohnen / dwelling A way of inhabiting and relating. Ask how the lecture’s argument reaches beyond residence without making actual shelter unimportant. Bauen / building Read building through the question of dwelling. Construction alone does not settle what a place allows its inhabitants to do. Schonen / sparing A positive letting, not merely leaving everything untouched. Test what preservation requires in a contested place. Geviert / fourfold A gathering of relations. Keep this account distinct from four boxes into which every culture must fit. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Relational things and a difficult divinity. RELATIONALITY Andrew J. Mitchell The introduction to The Fourfold describes gathering as also releasing a thing beyond a sealed identity. Its relations remain tenuous and finite. This reading complicates an image of dwelling as permanent enclosure or complete self-sufficiency. The Fourfold, p. 13 at Ereignis GODS AND POETICAL THINKING Lawrence J. Hatab “Logos, Muthos, Theos” asks how divinities function in the later Heidegger’s poetic thinking. Hatab cautions that a merely symbolic reading does not fit the tenor of Building Dwelling Thinking. Keep this difficulty visible instead of quietly replacing the gods with a convenient modern equivalent. Read Hatab at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk When does belonging become exclusion? Mitchell’s account of relation and Hatab’s question of divinity open different problems. Their juxtaposition here is BeyngLab’s. Neither reading supplies a license to romanticize a landscape or decide who properly belongs there. Ask who maintains the place, who can enter, and who has been displaced. These are our critical questions for the lecture. They prevent a beautiful description of dwelling from standing in for an account of its material conditions. Regional disclosure grammars describe revisable ways a place becomes intelligible. They do not identify a people’s permanent essence or an authentic homeland to which everyone must return. 05 / Reading move Read a threshold twice. Experimental A BeyngLab exercise, grounded in the passages above. 01 Describe what gathers. Choose a doorway, bridge, courtyard, or shared table. After reading p. 355, describe the relations it brings together. Mark where your description departs from Heidegger’s. 02 Trace the conditions. Record access, labor, repair, weather, ownership, and a conflicting use. Ask someone else how the place appears from their position. 03 Try one revision. Propose a small change that helps someone inhabit the place. State what it preserves, what it alters, and whose objection might undo it. Carry the record into Sixfold as an experiment, not a reconstruction of Heidegger’s doctrine. 06 / Crossings Gathering, letting, and experimentation. T3 / THING What does a thing gather? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T13 / RELEASE What does letting require? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T9 / EVENT How does belonging become a question? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. SIXFOLD / EXPERIMENTAL Sixfold your place. Test a cosmogram while keeping its additions distinct from the primary text. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, “Building Dwelling Thinking” (1951), collected in GA 7; trans. Albert Hofstadter, in Basic Writings, ed. David Farrell Krell, revised edition (1993), pp. 351, 355, 359. These are English edition pages, not GA pagination. Interpretive Andrew J. Mitchell, The Fourfold (2015), p. 13; Lawrence J. Hatab, “Logos, Muthos, Theos: Heidegger’s Phenomenology and the Sacralization of Being” (2024). Direct archive links appear in the reader cards. Experimental Questions of access and the threshold exercise are BeyngLab’s extensions. Sixfold adds a laboratory practice; it does not silently revise the fourfold in the source."},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/#archive","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/#terms","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/#readers","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/#disagreement","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/#practice","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/#relations","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/dwelling/#sources","title":"Dwelling / Wohnen / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?","keywords":["dwelling","Wohnen"],"text":"What does a place let happen—and whose dwelling does it sustain?"},{"url":"/topoi/event/","title":"Event / Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Read Ereignis across Contributions to Philosophy and Time and Being. Preserve the difference between the 1930s manuscripts and the 1962 lecture, and keep translation choices visible. “Event” is an entry into a problem, not a synonym for any dramatic occurrence.","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"T9 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path How does belonging happen? Ereignis. How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing? Read Ereignis across Contributions to Philosophy and Time and Being. Preserve the difference between the 1930s manuscripts and the 1962 lecture, and keep translation choices visible. “Event” is an entry into a problem, not a synonym for any dramatic occurrence. Begin with the passages FOLLOW EVENT 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail Two textual settings, one difficult word. Begin with the relation itself. Then ask whether the later lecture changes the question. The translations use different vocabularies and should not be silently harmonized. 01 / Contributions to Philosophy · GA 65 · §135 No independently existing sides The relation of Da-sein and beyng belongs to beyng’s own occurrence. Heidegger rejects a picture in which each first exists separately and then enters a relation. The subject–object model therefore misleads here. Rojcewicz–Vallega-Neu translation, p. 200 02 / Time and Being · GA 14 Question the question “What is it?” The lecture questions the demand to define Appropriation as something that is. It also explicitly distinguishes its use of “event” from an ordinary occurrence. Follow the difficulty before supplying an example. Stambaugh translation, p. 20 03 / TRANSLATOR’S NOTE · The Event · GA 71 See what English cannot carry at once The translator’s vocabulary note displays a family of terms built around eignen. It helps track the linguistic problem, but it is editorial guidance, not a stand-alone statement by Heidegger. Rojcewicz’s vocabulary note, p. 125 02 / Terms and distinctions Translate without flattening. Term Distinction to retain Ereignis Rendered here through the linked editions’ “event” and “Appropriation.” Neither English word settles the argument. Appropriation Do not assume possession by an already constituted subject. Read the relation described in Contributions before importing that model. Seyn / beyng The spelling signals a distinction in the Contributions translation. Preserve it when identifying the source’s vocabulary. Enteignis Ziarek brings withdrawal and disappropriation into the question of Ereignis. Treat his account as an interpretation to test in its textual setting. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Eventfulness, withdrawal, and language. A DOCUMENTED DISAGREEMENT Richard Polt “Meaning, Excess, and Event” directly engages Thomas Sheehan’s interpretation. Polt asks whether an account of conditions always already in effect gives sufficient weight to historical particularity in the 1930s writings. His claims remain explicitly sensitive to the period under discussion. Read Polt at Ereignis WITHDRAWAL Krzysztof Ziarek “Event and Nothingness” reads Ereignis through Enteignis and the nothing. Giving and withdrawal remain entangled. This challenges any account of the event as an entirely positive revelation available for us to possess. Read Ziarek at Ereignis BEING, TIME, AND SAYING Theodore Kisiel “The Language of the Event: The Event of Language” follows the linguistic problem of thinking being and time together. Read it alongside the question-and-answer difficulty on Stambaugh’s p. 20. Read Kisiel at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk A condition, a historical opening, or a changing question? Polt’s disagreement with Sheehan gives a concrete point of entry: how much eventfulness does Ereignis require? Ziarek and Kisiel add questions of withdrawal and saying. They should not be made into three interchangeable explanations. Compare passages by date and work before constructing a single definition. Equally, avoid treating every disruption, innovation, or moving experience as an instance of Ereignis. The term’s difficulty demands an argument, not a grander label. No region owns the event. Our regional grammars propose situated, revisable readings of disclosure; they do not turn histories or cultures into fixed metaphysical subjects. 05 / Reading move Make the disagreement legible. Experimental A comparative reading exercise. 01 Read two passages separately. Summarize what each primary passage rules out. Record work, date, translator, and page beside each summary. 02 Test a reader’s claim. Choose Polt’s question of historical particularity or Ziarek’s account of withdrawal. Identify what your two passages support and what requires further reading. 03 Keep the analogy provisional. If you propose a contemporary encounter as an example, explain exactly what it illuminates and where it fails. Do not let its drama substitute for textual evidence. 06 / Crossings Belonging, withdrawal, and historical turns. T8 / DWELLING How does a gathering make room? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T15 / NOTHINGNESS What does withdrawal disclose? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T16 / MINDFULNESS How does thinking question its own language? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. TURNS Keep the textual periods distinct. Situate the change of vocabulary before claiming continuity. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, Contributions to Philosophy (of the Event), written 1936–1938, GA 65, §135; trans. Richard Rojcewicz and Daniela Vallega-Neu (2012), p. 200. Primary Heidegger, “Time and Being” (1962), collected in GA 14; trans. Joan Stambaugh, On Time and Being (1972), p. 20. English page numbers are not GA page numbers. Editorial / translation Richard Rojcewicz’s translator’s note in The Event (2012), p. 125, translating Das Ereignis (1941–1942), GA 71. The note’s explanatory voice belongs to the translator. Interpretive Polt, “Meaning, Excess, and Event” (2011); Ziarek, “Event and Nothingness”; Kisiel, “The Language of the Event: The Event of Language.” Direct links appear above. Experimental BeyngLab’s comparisons and exercise remain open to correction. They do not establish an equivalence between Ereignis and a laboratory activity."},{"url":"/topoi/event/#archive","title":"Event / Ereignis / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/event/#terms","title":"Event / Ereignis / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/event/#readers","title":"Event / Ereignis / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/event/#disagreement","title":"Event / Ereignis / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/event/#practice","title":"Event / Ereignis / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/event/#relations","title":"Event / Ereignis / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/event/#sources","title":"Event / Ereignis / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?","keywords":["event","Ereignis"],"text":"How can we think what lets being and time belong together without turning it into another thing?"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/","title":"Difference","type":"Topos","description":"Begin with Identity and Difference: Heidegger’s thinking of belonging-together and his encounter with Hegel. Follow distinct crossings into Derrida’s différance and Deleuze’s difference in itself, while keeping the earlier ontological question and other sources of twentieth-century difference in view.","keywords":["difference","Difference","Heidegger","beyng.com","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"T7 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Identity and difference. What comes first? Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference? Begin with Identity and Difference: Heidegger’s thinking of belonging-together and his encounter with Hegel. Follow distinct crossings into Derrida’s différance and Deleuze’s difference in itself, while keeping the earlier ontological question and other sources of twentieth-century difference in view. Begin with the passages FOLLOW DIFFERENCE 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail Identity and Difference: the principal reading. The 1957 book brings together two lectures, not simply one commentary on Hegel. Read “The Principle of Identity” with “The Onto-theo-logical Constitution of Metaphysics.” The second makes the encounter with Hegel explicit. English page references below use Joan Stambaugh’s translation. 01 / Identity and Difference · GA 11 Identity as belonging-together The first lecture moves toward the belonging-together of thinking and being, and toward Ereignis, translated here as appropriation. Identity becomes a question about this relation rather than merely a formula of self-sameness. The Principle of Identity, p. 39 02 / Identity and Difference · Hegel What is the matter of thinking? Heidegger reads Hegel’s absolute Idea and asks how being becomes the matter of thought. Read this as Heidegger’s interpretation of Hegel, not a neutral summary that settles Hegel’s philosophy. The Onto-theo-logical Constitution of Metaphysics, p. 43 03 / Identity and Difference · historical encounter How should thinking meet its history? Heidegger considers Hegel’s criterion for dialogue with earlier thinkers. Compare taking up the force of what they thought with questioning what their thinking leaves unthought. Hegel and the conversation with history, p. 47 04 / Identity and Difference · the step back Being thought through difference The step back asks about the difference that metaphysics presupposes. It changes the question asked of metaphysics rather than simply adding another highest ground. Difference and the step back, p. 65 Earlier bearings: keep the distinction between being and beings separate from differences among ways of being. 01 / Introduction to Metaphysics · GA 40 The ambiguity of “being” Heidegger distinguishes a particular thing from what makes up its being. The passage moves through the ambiguity of the Greek expression to on. Read the translators’ notes as notes, alongside the argument. Fried–Polt translation, second edition, p. 34 02 / The Basic Problems of Phenomenology · GA 24 Many ways, one question? The lecture asks how radically different ways of being can belong to any unified concept of being. This concerns the relation among ways of being, rather than merely the separation of two classes of objects. Hofstadter translation, p. 176 02 / Terms and distinctions Related words, different operations. Term Distinction Identity / belonging-together In the 1957 lecture, ask what lets thinking and being belong together. Do not translate this immediately into personal or cultural identity. Hegel / dialectical difference Follow the roles of identity, negation, and mediation in the argument. Hegel is an interlocutor, not a shorthand for denying all difference. Derrida / différance Differing and deferring unsettle self-presence. This is not another name for the distinction between being and beings. Deleuze / difference in itself Ask how difference can be thought without first subordinating it to identity, resemblance, opposition, or analogy. Seiendes / beings The things, people, and other entities encountered. Distinguishing one entity from another does not yet pose the ontological question. Sein / being The question of the being of what appears. Avoid treating it as an additional entity behind the others. Ways of being Read differences among existence, readiness-to-hand, and presence-at-hand without turning them into a ranking of human cultures. Differenz / Unterschied Attend to the work and passage in which a term occurs. Later uses of difference need their own argument; English “difference” does not guarantee conceptual identity. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Derrida and Deleuze: engagement and departure. EXTERNAL PRIMARY TEXT / DERRIDA Différance Derrida explicitly engages Heidegger’s ontological difference while asking whether différance can remain within its terms. His essay’s encounter with Heidegger centers on “The Anaximander Fragment,” so the relation should not be reduced to influence from Identity and Difference alone. His discussion of Hegel through Koyré also complicates any simple anti-Hegelian label. Différance (1968), trans. Alan Bass, Margins of Philosophy, pp. 3–27 EXTERNAL PRIMARY TEXT / DELEUZE Difference and Repetition The original preface names Heidegger’s ontological difference alongside structuralism, literature, and other contemporary experiments. Deleuze seeks difference in itself rather than difference governed by identity and negation. His “Note on Heidegger’s Philosophy of Difference” (Patton translation, pp. 64–66) makes the engagement explicit; read it as an argument with Heidegger, not an adoption of his project. Read the prefaces to Difference and Repetition Further readers at Ereignis WAYS OF SAYING Lee Braver “How to say the same thing” distinguishes entities, their ways of being, and manifestation itself. His interpretive scheme helps expose why treating every distinction as one between things misses the problem. Keep the scheme marked as Braver’s reading. Read at Ereignis WISEACRE / A LIMIT QUESTION Doug Wise “Topology of being and its limit theorem” asks what would make a distinction’s two sides and their relation intelligible. Wise questions the possibility of stepping outside the horizon of sense-making to survey its setting. His topological vocabulary is an interpretive experiment. Read at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk Did Heidegger introduce difference to the twentieth century? A decisive intervention, not a single origin. Heidegger gives ontological difference a central role and later rethinks identity through belonging-together. But the field has multiple sources: Nietzsche, Bergson, Saussure and structuralism, Freud, and the inheritance of Hegel. Deleuze’s preface itself presents a convergence rather than one founder; Derrida’s essay works through several of these sources. Keep three claims separate: a direct citation establishes engagement; a shared problem supports comparison; a stronger claim of influence needs a history of reading and transformation. These pages establish explicit engagements with Heidegger, not that Identity and Difference alone generated either later project. Can a useful diagram become a misleading picture? Braver’s distinctions clarify the language of manifestation; Wise asks about the limits of any position from which we might survey such distinctions. Their juxtaposition here is BeyngLab’s question, not a claim that these essays directly debate each other. Two boxes labeled “being” and “beings” can make them look like two things. Ask what your diagram clarifies and what its spatial arrangement silently assumes. Likewise, differences among regional grammars concern situated ways of disclosure, not separate cultural substances with permanent properties. 05 / Reading move Read a crossing without erasing the difference. Experimental A BeyngLab exercise. Keep your extension distinct from the source. 01 Read Heidegger with Hegel. Begin with Identity and Difference, pp. 39, 43, 47, and 65. Write separately what Heidegger attributes to Hegel and what Heidegger proposes in response. 02 Follow one explicit engagement. Choose Derrida’s discussion of Heidegger in Différance or Deleuze’s preface and Note on Heidegger. Identify what the later text takes up, changes, and refuses. 03 Test the claim of influence. Mark your evidence as direct citation, interpretive comparison, or a historical hypothesis still needing support. Do not treat a shared word as proof of a shared concept. 04 Return to one situation. Choose a classification used in a classroom, workplace, or Atlas grammar. Ask whether its categories presuppose fixed identities or emerge through relations. This is a BeyngLab experiment; it does not make those philosophical projects interchangeable. 06 / Crossings World, nothingness"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/#archive","title":"Difference / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/#terms","title":"Difference / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/#readers","title":"Difference / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/#disagreement","title":"Difference / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/#practice","title":"Difference / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/#relations","title":"Difference / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/difference/#sources","title":"Difference / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?","keywords":["difference","Difference","Identity and Difference","Hegel","Derrida","Deleuze","différance","differance","Difference and Repetition","ontological difference","ontotheology","belonging together"],"text":"Must difference begin with identities—or can identity emerge through difference?"},{"url":"/topoi/techne/","title":"Technē","type":"Topos","description":"Follow Heidegger’s interpretation of technē through knowing, bringing-forth, and the question of modern technology. Read the Greek inheritance as an argument with a history, not as an uncontested dictionary definition.","keywords":["techne","Technē","Heidegger","beyng.com"],"text":"T12 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Know how. Ask what the knowing reveals. What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making? Follow Heidegger’s interpretation of technē through knowing, bringing-forth, and the question of modern technology. Read the Greek inheritance as an argument with a history, not as an uncontested dictionary definition. Begin with the passages FOLLOW TECHNĒ 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail Knowing and the reach of technics. Both stops come from The Question Concerning Technology. The first uses the archive’s parallel-pagination reading edition; the second links to a specific page in Basic Writings. 01 / The Question Concerning Technology · GA 7 Technē and knowing In the passage marked Basic Writings p. 319, Heidegger places technē with knowing and revealing. Making a house or a vessel involves a prior gathering of what is to appear. Its significance exceeds manipulation alone. Read the essay / locate the p. 319 marker 02 / The Question Concerning Technology · GA 7 Art reopens the question Near the essay’s end, Heidegger recalls art under the name technē while asking about the danger that ordering will exhaust revealing. The passage raises a possibility; it does not certify every artwork as a cure. Basic Writings, p. 339 02 / Terms and distinctions Knowing, making, ordering. Term Distinction Technē / τέχνη Heidegger’s reading emphasizes a knowing that reveals. “Craft,” “art,” and “technique” each illuminate only part of the problem. Poiēsis / ποίησις Bringing-forth: follow the neighboring Topos to distinguish this from sheer production volume. Technik / modern technology Do not simply substitute the modern category for the Greek word. The essay argues for a distinctive mode of revealing. Gestell / enframing The ordering mode explored in the standing-reserve Topos. A diagnosis of revealing requires more than an inventory of machines. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Two routes through the historical shift. EQUIPMENT AND HISTORY Hubert Dreyfus “Between Techne and Technology” provocatively reads Being and Time’s account of equipment as a decisive step toward the later technological understanding. This complicates an easy opposition between good craft and bad machines. Read at Ereignis WORLD AND ART William McNeill “Tracing Technē” follows production, world-formation, and the question of art across Heidegger’s changing engagement with Aristotle. His account draws attention to a disclosure of world that cannot simply be credited to a maker’s plan. Read at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk Does craft escape the problem? Dreyfus and McNeill trace different emphases within a changing corpus. Compare their arguments before treating “Heidegger on technē” as one stable position. Neither a handmade object nor a digital tool decides the question in advance. Ask what a practice lets its participants recognize, what counts as success, and what it hides. Regional grammars help compare these operations within particular settings; they do not assign one timeless technical mentality to an entire culture. 05 / Reading move Read one act of making through its knowing. Experimental A BeyngLab exercise. Keep your extension distinct from the source. 01 Name the work. Choose a repair or making task. Record the materials, anticipated outcome, and skills involved. 02 Notice the revealing. What becomes visible only to someone learning this practice? What does the task train them to disregard? Return to the p. 319 passage. 03 Compare without ranking in advance. Try a different tool or procedure. Describe what changes in attention and judgment, not just speed. Use Dreyfus or McNeill to challenge your initial account. 06 / Crossings Bringing-forth, ordering, and doing. T11 / POIESIS What comes into appearance? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T4 / GESTELL When does revealing become ordering? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T10 / PERFORMANCE How does a practice carry its standards? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, “The Question Concerning Technology” (1953), GA 7, trans. William Lovitt, revised by David Farrell Krell in Basic Writings (1993), pp. 319, 339. The archive’s reading edition also displays Lovitt’s 1977 pagination; its English text follows Basic Writings. Interpretive Hubert Dreyfus, “Between Techne and Technology”; William McNeill, “Tracing Technē: Heidegger, Aristotle, and the Legacy of Philosophy.” Experimental The comparison of tools and standards belongs to BeyngLab. It tests a reading rather than declaring a technology inherently liberating or oppressive. The English page numbers identify their editions; they are not interchangeable with GA pagination. Direct archive links appear above."},{"url":"/topoi/techne/#archive","title":"Technē / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/techne/#terms","title":"Technē / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/techne/#readers","title":"Technē / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/techne/#disagreement","title":"Technē / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/techne/#practice","title":"Technē / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/techne/#relations","title":"Technē / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/techne/#sources","title":"Technē / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?","keywords":["techne","Technē"],"text":"What changes when technē is read as a way of revealing rather than simply a means of making?"},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/","title":"Poiesis","type":"Topos","description":"Read bringing-forth alongside the work of art’s opening of truth. Keep poiēsis, poetry, manufacture, and creative novelty distinct. A new product does not automatically disclose a new world.","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis","Heidegger","beyng.com"],"text":"T11 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Bring forth. What comes into view? How does bringing something forth change what can appear? Read bringing-forth alongside the work of art’s opening of truth. Keep poiēsis, poetry, manufacture, and creative novelty distinct. A new product does not automatically disclose a new world. Begin with the passages FOLLOW POIESIS 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail From bringing-forth to the work of art. These readings cross two essays with different tasks. Do not make the later technology essay and the art essay say exactly the same thing. 01 / The Question Concerning Technology · GA 7 Appearance and occasioning Heidegger links bringing-forth with a movement from concealment into unconcealment. The four modes of occasioning participate in this bringing-forth. The page also carries Lovitt’s explanatory note on the German vocabulary. Lovitt translation, p. 11 02 / The Origin of the Work of Art · GA 5 A work changes unconcealment The essay calls art essentially poetic in a broad sense and locates its working in a change of unconcealment. It questions whether imagination alone adequately explains this opening. “Poetry” here cannot be confined to verse. Hofstadter translation / Basic Writings, p. 197 02 / Terms and distinctions Bringing-forth is not a creativity score. Term Distinction Poiēsis / bringing-forth Read the movement into appearance and its conditions. Do not reduce the term to the number of objects produced. Dichtung / poetry The art essay uses a broad account of poetic opening. Preserve the difference between that argument and literary poetry as a particular practice. Physis Ask how self-emergence and human making are related in each passage. Their relation needs textual attention rather than a permanent nature/culture partition. Technē The knowing involved in revealing. Follow its own Topos rather than treating it as interchangeable with poiēsis. 03 / Readers at Ereignis World-formation and the work’s measure. WORLD-FORMATION William McNeill “Tracing Technē” describes a poietic dimension in the happening of world and follows its significance for art. This provides a route from an account centered on production toward the question of what precedes and exceeds human making. Read at Ereignis THE WORK AND ITS APPEARANCE Gregory Schufreider “The Origin of the Work of Art” asks how a work can set a measure for its own appearance rather than merely satisfy a standard imposed upon it. Read this interpretation against the primary passage’s account of truth at work. Read at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk What warrants the claim that a work opens a world? A work may surprise its maker without establishing a philosophical claim about truth. Ask what the interpretation explains and what counterexamples resist it. A successful advertisement, an ingenious device, and an artwork may all produce effects; that fact alone does not settle their relation to poiēsis. McNeill and Schufreider offer different approaches to the work’s opening. BeyngLab’s comparison should keep labor, materials, reception, and disagreement visible, without pretending that these questions exhaust Heidegger’s account. 05 / Reading move Compare an intention with what appears. Experimental A BeyngLab exercise. Keep your extension distinct from the source. 01 Read before making. Summarize the difference between bringing-forth on Lovitt’s p. 11 and poetic opening on Basic Writings p. 197. 02 Make a modest alteration. Rewrite a label, rearrange an ordinary object, or compose a short passage. Record what you hoped it would make perceptible. 03 Read the reception. Ask another reader what appeared to them. Preserve the mismatch. Explain whether “bringing-forth” clarifies the encounter, and where the analogy becomes too ambitious. 06 / Crossings Knowing, gathering, and opening. T12 / TECHNĒ What knowing enters making? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T3 / THING What does the work gather? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T9 / EVENT When does “opening” require a different argument? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, “The Question Concerning Technology” (1953), GA 7, trans. William Lovitt, The Question Concerning Technology and Other Essays (1977), p. 11. Its long vocabulary note belongs to the translator. Primary Heidegger, “The Origin of the Work of Art,” developed in 1935–1936 and collected in GA 5; trans. Albert Hofstadter, Basic Writings, revised edition (1993), p. 197. The linked page’s footer labels GA 9, but the essay and inline reference identify GA 5; that distinction is retained here. Interpretive William McNeill, “Tracing Technē”; Gregory Schufreider, “The Origin of the Work of Art.” Experimental The making exercise belongs to BeyngLab. Neither novelty nor audience approval establishes a work’s philosophical significance. The English page numbers identify their editions; they are not interchangeable with GA pagination. Direct archive links appear above."},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/#archive","title":"Poiesis / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/#terms","title":"Poiesis / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/#readers","title":"Poiesis / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/#disagreement","title":"Poiesis / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/#practice","title":"Poiesis / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/#relations","title":"Poiesis / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/poiesis/#sources","title":"Poiesis / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?","keywords":["poiesis","Poiesis"],"text":"How does bringing something forth change what can appear?"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/","title":"Performance","type":"Topos","description":"Read practical involvement with Heidegger, labor and the performance principle with Marcuse, and performativity with Lyotard. The shared question is how ways of doing become demands that organize existence. Their concepts differ; this Topos makes the connections available for testing.","keywords":["performance","Performance","Heidegger","beyng.com","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"T10 / TOPOI An Ereignis reading path Work, desire, knowledge. Who sets the measure? How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts? Read practical involvement with Heidegger, labor and the performance principle with Marcuse, and performativity with Lyotard. The shared question is how ways of doing become demands that organize existence. Their concepts differ; this Topos makes the connections available for testing. Begin with the passages FOLLOW PERFORMANCE 01 / Archive trail 02 / Terms and distinctions 03 / Readers at Ereignis 04 / Disagreement and risk 05 / Reading move 06 / Crossings 07 / Source ledger 01 / Archive trail From practical involvement to the demand to perform. Begin at beyng.com, then follow clearly marked external readings. This is a comparative path, not a claim that each thinker simply inherits the same concept. 01 / Heidegger · Being and Time §15 · beyng.com Knowing in the doing Hammering lets equipment show itself through involvement. Such dealing has its own sight—circumspection—and belongs to an already encountered equipmental setting. It is neither mere blind activity nor a detached inspection of properties. Macquarrie–Robinson translation, p. 98 02 / Marcuse · 1933 · external editorial preview Labor as a mode of existence “On the Philosophical Foundations of the Concept of Labor in Economics” asks about labor’s ontological structure. Work binds the worker to the demands of the thing being worked on; its burden cannot simply be equated with physical strain or paid employment. The editors of Heideggerian Marxism trace the essay’s tension between labor and freedom through its contrast with play. The linked preview presents their introduction and quotations, not the complete essay. Heideggerian Marxism: editorial introduction, pp. xxii–xxiii 03 / Marcuse · Eros and Civilization · 1955 · external excerpts The performance principle Marcuse names a historically specific form of the reality principle: social life organized through competitive achievement and alienated labor. His distinction between basic and surplus repression asks which restrictions sustain domination beyond the requirements of collective life. This brings work into relation with desire, gratification, and freedom. The link is an archived selection of quotations, with edition-specific page references. Eros and Civilization excerpts: definition at p. 35 in the cited edition 04 / Lyotard · The Postmodern Condition · 1979 · external primary text Performativity and the authorization of knowledge Lyotard examines the pressure to legitimate research and education through improved system performance. The technical criterion is an advantageous relation of output to input; it differs from truth or justice. Ask what happens when funding and institutional recognition increasingly depend on this criterion. Read sections 11–12, especially printed pp. 44–47. Bennington–Massumi translation (1984), sections 11–12 05 / Heidegger · The Question Concerning Technology · beyng.com Does better performance answer the problem? Heidegger warns that human activity and achievement cannot directly banish the danger to revealing. Return here after Marcuse and Lyotard: could a proposed improvement strengthen the very ordering being questioned? This is our comparative question, not Heidegger’s reading of those books. Lovitt translation, p. 33 02 / Terms and distinctions Connected concepts, different questions. Term Distinction Ontology of labor / Marcuse, 1933 What kind of existence is involved in labor? Keep this ontological question distinct from the historical organization of employment. Performance principle / Marcuse, 1955 How are work and desire organized under a historically specific reality principle? This is a social and psychoanalytic diagnosis, not a timeless essence of humanity. Performativity / Lyotard, 1979 How does operational efficiency become a criterion of legitimation? Here performativity concerns knowledge and systems; it does not simply mean theatrical performance or a speech act that does what it says. Enactment / Vollzug How is a relation or understanding lived or carried out? Hatab provides a route through formal indication. Enactment does not by itself imply competitive achievement. Staged and measured performance Presentation to an audience and achievement against a standard may intersect. Neither is automatically equivalent to Marcuse’s principle or Lyotard’s diagnosis. 03 / Readers at Ereignis Read the connections—and their limits. ARISTOTELIAN CROSSING Franco Volpi “Dasein as Praxis” reads Heidegger through Aristotle’s practical philosophy. It helps distinguish action from production without reducing existence to productive output. Read at Ereignis FORMAL INDICATION Lawrence J. Hatab “The Point of Language in Heidegger’s Thinking” treats concepts as arising from factical life and pointing back toward tasks of enactment. This offers a way to teach concepts through practices of reading. Read at Ereignis EXTERNAL / PERFORMANCE STUDIES Jon McKenzie · Perform or Else McKenzie explicitly brings Marcuse’s performance principle and Lyotard’s performativity into a study of performance as power and knowledge. His introduction connects cultural, organizational, and technological performance while retaining the tension between experimentation and normative demands. This is a published interpretive bridge, not a genealogy invented for this page. Publisher preview: introduction, pp. 14–18 04 / Disagreement and risk Is labor the problem, or its organization? Comparative reading Marcuse’s early account of labor’s burden and his later critique of the performance principle should remain in tension. If constraint belongs to labor itself, what can changing institutions achieve? If domination is historically organized, which constraints can be transformed? Do not make the 1933 essay a finished version of the 1955 argument. Lyotard shifts the question toward the authorization of knowledge. Read the connection as a comparison of normative demands, not proof that efficiency, repression, and labor name one mechanism. McKenzie provides an explicit route for investigating their crossings. BeyngLab extension A practice may satisfy its metric while failing the people who sustain it. Its failure against that metric is not automatically liberation. Regional disclosure grammars name revisable arrangements of practices, expectations, and authority—not fixed cultural essences. Investigate disagreement within each setting. 05 / Reading move Read one task through three lenses. Experimental A BeyngLab exercise. Keep your extension distinct from the source. 01 Describe the labor. Choose teaching a lesson, maintaining a shared space, or completing a design. What does the task require of bodies, attention, and time? What demands arise from the task, and what demands come from its institution? 02 Trace the performance principle. Who assigns roles and rewards? Which needs must participants postpone, and who benefits? Treat Marcuse’s account as a question to investigate; do not infer domination from effort alone. 03 Inspect the criterion of knowledge. Which evidence counts as success: speed, throughput, a score, understanding? Use Lyotard to ask whether efficiency is replacing another justification. Identify who can challenge the measure. 04 Try a different arrangement. Change one rule or measure with participants. Record what becomes possible, what costs remain, and what the new standard misses. Return to Heidegger’s warning about answering ordering with more achievement. 06 / Crossings Care, making, and the demand to deliver. T1 / CARE What is at stake in doing? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T12 / TECHNĒ What does skilled practice reveal? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T4 / GESTELL Who becomes an available resource? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. T11 / POIESIS What exceeds a specified outcome? Continue through the archive passages and their readers. 07 / Source ledger Return to the archive. Primary Heidegger, Being and Time (1927), §15, trans. John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (1962), p. 98; “The Question Concerning Technology” (1953), GA 7, trans. William Lovitt (1977), p. 33. Both linked at beyng.com. Editorial access Marcuse’s 1933 labor essay is collected in Heideggerian Marxism, ed. Richard Wolin and John Abromeit (University of Nebraska Press, 2005). The linked publisher preview supplies e"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/#archive","title":"Performance / primary archive trail","type":"Topos","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/#terms","title":"Performance / terms and distinctions","type":"Topos","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/#readers","title":"Performance / readers at Ereignis","type":"Topos","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/#disagreement","title":"Performance / disagreement and risk","type":"Topos","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/#practice","title":"Performance / reading move","type":"Topos","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/#relations","title":"Performance / Topoi crossings","type":"Topos","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/topoi/performance/#sources","title":"Performance / source ledger","type":"Topos","description":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?","keywords":["performance","Performance","Marcuse","Herbert Marcuse","Lyotard","Jean-François Lyotard","labor","labour","ontology of labor","performance principle","Leistungsprinzip","performativity","performativité","Jon McKenzie","Perform or Else"],"text":"How does doing become a demand to perform—and who decides what counts?"},{"url":"/breakdown/","title":"Breakdown","type":"Section","description":"Heidegger’s crisis, Boss and Zollikon, Foucault and Binswanger, competing approaches to care, and Sixfold / Making Cures.","keywords":["breakdown","Gebsattel","Boss","Zollikon","Zollicon","Foucault","Binswanger","psychoanalysis","schizoanalysis","Romanyshyn","Stadlen","making cures","sixfold"],"text":"B / BREAKDOWN Crisis / care / making cures When a world stops holding. What happens when familiar ways of living, thinking, and caring no longer hold? Follow Heidegger’s crisis, the encounters between philosophy and psychotherapy, and experiments in making more livable worlds together. Breakdown is a field of inquiry across biography, institutions, and practices. A failed tool, an unsettled understanding, and a person’s suffering require different descriptions. None guarantees revelation or liberation. Begin with the historical encounters Try the Making Cures workshop Heidegger’s crisis Boss / Zollikon Foucault / Binswanger Competing approaches Wounded research Sixfold & Making Cures Sources Heidegger’s crisis: receiving help. Historical scholarship Andrew J. Mitchell’s “Heidegger’s Breakdown” reconstructs Heidegger’s postwar crisis and treatment by Viktor Emil von Gebsattel . The treatment belongs to 1946; retrospective accounts and the sequence of denazification proceedings need careful comparison. Gebsattel, rather than Medard Boss, occupies the central clinical role in this episode. Mitchell reads the encounter alongside changing accounts of health, help, and healing. Heidegger’s recollection emphasizes walking with Gebsattel and the help of another human being. Mitchell questions the claim that the doctor simply did nothing: he also documents continuing psychotherapy and glucose injections. The walking narrative is not a complete treatment history. Restraint and presence matter here alongside evidence of active treatment. Keep Heidegger’s account, the biographical evidence, and Mitchell’s philosophical interpretation distinct. His suffering does not cancel political responsibility for his National Socialist commitments; neither does it authorize a diagnosis from a distance. Mitchell, Research in Phenomenology 46 (2016), 70–97; especially 80–87 Boss / Zollikon: philosophy enters the consulting room. Primary collection Heidegger’s correspondence with the Swiss psychiatrist Medard Boss began in 1947. Their subsequent collaboration produced the Zollikon seminars, held across 1959–1969. The published collection combines seminar protocols, conversations, and letters; these genres do not provide interchangeable kinds of evidence. Read for questions of bodily existence, space, time, causality, and being with others. How does describing someone as an object of measurement differ from encountering a person in a world? Then turn the question back on the encounter: who decides what the patient’s world means? The ontological analysis of Dasein and clinical Daseinsanalysis operate at different levels. Boss and Ludwig Binswanger also developed different projects. “Existential psychotherapy” should not erase those disagreements. A challenge from within Anthony Stadlen’s The Madhouse of Being asks how language can darken and mystify a shared world. Read his scrutiny of Zollikon’s clinical discussions as a challenge to philosophical and therapeutic authority, not as an endorsement of every claim in that tradition. Zollikon Seminars: publisher record and account of the protocols Stadlen: The Madhouse of Being — author’s text Foucault: existential analysis and its changing stakes. Documented connection Foucault’s early route runs through Binswanger : a 1953 course and his introduction to the 1954 French translation of Dream and Existence . His early manuscript, now published as Binswanger and Existential Analysis , compares existential analysis with psychiatry, psychoanalysis, and phenomenology. This is a separate route from Boss’s Zollikon collaboration. Foucault’s later histories of madness and psychiatric power change the questions: how do institutions and forms of knowledge establish who may speak, what counts as truth, and what treatment authorizes? His preface to Anti-Oedipus gives another concrete crossing to Deleuze and Guattari. These encounters do not establish one continuous therapeutic school. Foucault: Binswanger and Existential Analysis — edition and manuscript context Anti-Oedipus — contents, including Foucault’s preface Different approaches. Questions to keep open. BeyngLab comparison Use these questions to enter the texts. They are neither a ranking of treatments nor complete definitions of internally diverse traditions. Approach Reading question Point of friction Psychoanalysis How do unconscious conflict, desire, interpretation, and transference enter suffering? Which theory of the unconscious and which practice of interpretation? Freud, Jung, and later schools cannot stand in for one another. Existential psychotherapy / Daseinsanalysis How are possibilities, relationships, embodiment, and a lived world altered? Does a philosophical account illuminate the encounter or speak over the person? Compare Boss and Binswanger rather than treating them as one voice. Foucault’s historical inquiries How do institutions organize care, authority, normality, and exclusion? A history of psychiatric power is not itself a treatment method. Schizoanalysis How does desire work across social arrangements beyond the family? Begin with Anti-Oedipus, chapters 2 and 4. Investigate institutions and collective arrangements as well as personal narratives. Do not turn clinical suffering into a metaphor for creativity. Imaginal research What does the researcher bring to, seek from, or fail to hear in the work? Romanyshyn’s Jungian method requires reflection on involvement, not automatic authority for personal experience. The comparison can return to Performance : when does care become an instruction to function, improve, or deliver? Read Marcuse’s organization of labor and desire beside the institution’s measures of success. The researcher enters the picture. Research method In The Wounded Researcher , Robert D. Romanyshyn asks how the researcher distinguishes personal investments in a topic from what the work asks of them. His account develops transference dialogues, active imagination, and alchemical hermeneutics. Begin with the preface, chapters 5–6, and chapters 10–11. For BeyngLab this opens a question about our own inquiry: what do we hope a story of breakdown will prove? Keep a record of attachments, uncertainties, and counter-readings. A wound may help explain why a question matters; it does not settle the answer. Cross to the Imaginal grammar while preserving the difference between Romanyshyn’s Jungian approach and other accounts of imagination. Regional disclosure grammars remain revisable descriptions of practices, never fixed cultural essences. Romanyshyn: The Wounded Researcher — book record Sixfold & Making Cures: make with. StudioLab / BeyngLab “Making cures” supplements talking cures and taking cures through creative doing-with oneself, others, and an environment. Media making, playform building, and cosmography become ways to work on shared conditions of life. The term’s perfumative register also lets us think of curing through time, materials, atmosphere, and relation. BREAKDOWN Notice what no longer holds. Whose account describes the trouble? Separate a person’s distress from a failed arrangement, a broken tool, or a disputed expectation. SIXFOLD Map the gathering. Read earth, sky, mortals, and divinities; make Thing and World explicit. What gathers here, and what wider conditions support or constrain it? MAKING CURES Change something together. Make a small, revisable intervention. Return it to those involved, attend to its effects, and keep open the possibility of stopping. These moves can recur in any order. Sixfold is a BeyngLab cosmographic exercise, not a diagnostic instrument. Making cures names a practice of collaborative inquiry and transformation, not a promise of clinical recovery. Begin with a shared table, meeting, classroom, or workplace. A story, rearranged space, changed schedule, or agreed pause may matter more than another product. Rest, refusal, and collaborative not-doing belong here: care must not become a fresh demand to perform. Run the Making Cures workshop Open the Sixfold worksheet Jon McKenzie: Traumaturgy, Making Cures, Non-Design Sources and return paths. Primary texts, editorial histories, critical interpretations, and BeyngLab exercises carry different kinds of authority. Read across them and preserve disagreement. Historical / interpretive: Andrew J. Mitchell, “Heidegger’s Breakdown: Health and Healing Under the Care of Dr. V.E. von Gebsattel,” Research in Phenomenology 46 (2016): 70–97. DOI 10.1163/15691640-12341"},{"url":"/breakdown/#history","title":"Breakdown / Heidegger’s crisis","type":"Section","description":"Mitchell on Heidegger’s postwar treatment by Gebsattel.","keywords":["Mitchell","Gebsattel","1946"],"text":"Mitchell on Heidegger’s postwar treatment by Gebsattel."},{"url":"/breakdown/#zollikon","title":"Breakdown / Boss and Zollikon","type":"Section","description":"Clinical Daseinsanalysis, protocols, and Stadlen’s critical reading.","keywords":["Boss","Zollikon","Stadlen","Daseinsanalysis"],"text":"Clinical Daseinsanalysis, protocols, and Stadlen’s critical reading."},{"url":"/breakdown/#foucault","title":"Breakdown / Foucault and Binswanger","type":"Section","description":"Existential analysis, institutional authority, and Anti-Oedipus.","keywords":["Foucault","Binswanger","madness"],"text":"Existential analysis, institutional authority, and Anti-Oedipus."},{"url":"/breakdown/#approaches","title":"Breakdown / Competing approaches","type":"Section","description":"Questions across psychoanalysis, existential psychotherapy, schizoanalysis, and imaginal research.","keywords":["Freud","Jung","Deleuze","Guattari","schizoanalysis"],"text":"Questions across psychoanalysis, existential psychotherapy, schizoanalysis, and imaginal research."},{"url":"/breakdown/#wounded-research","title":"Breakdown / Wounded research","type":"Section","description":"Romanyshyn on transference, active imagination, and the researcher’s involvement.","keywords":["Romanyshyn","wounded researcher","alchemical hermeneutics"],"text":"Romanyshyn on transference, active imagination, and the researcher’s involvement."},{"url":"/breakdown/#making-cures","title":"Breakdown / Sixfold and Making Cures","type":"Section","description":"Creative doing-with supplements talking cures and taking cures.","keywords":["sixfold","making cures","perfumative","non-design"],"text":"Creative doing-with supplements talking cures and taking cures."},{"url":"/breakdown/#sources","title":"Breakdown / Sources and archive","type":"Section","description":"Edition-specific sources and return paths to beyng.com.","keywords":["sources","editions","beyng.com"],"text":"Edition-specific sources and return paths to beyng.com."},{"url":"/workshops/making-cures/","title":"Making Cures","type":"Workshop","description":"Map an ordinary shared situation with Sixfold, try one change together, and return to revise or stop.","keywords":["making cures","sixfold","breakdown","workshop","PAR&D","StudioLab","non-design"],"text":"W / MAKE WITH Breakdown / Sixfold / Making Cures A gathering. A change. A return. Choose an ordinary shared situation that no longer works well. Map what sustains it, change one arrangement with the people involved, and return to ask what happened. Experimental A StudioLab/BeyngLab workshop for collaborative inquiry. Work with an everyday situation or a fictional case; personal disclosure is optional. This exercise does not diagnose or treat illness. Begin with something small. Allow 45–60 minutes for the first meeting and agree a later return. Bring paper or use the Sixfold worksheet . A shared table, a confusing meeting, or a room nobody feels able to use gives the group something concrete to examine. Example: a team’s weekly meeting produces fast decisions, but several members cannot contribute. Do not begin by diagnosing their confidence. Ask how timing, furniture, language, agendas, and expectations shape participation. Five moves, with room to pause. 01 Hear the trouble. Each person may offer an observation, a different account, or a pass. Write what happened separately from explanations of why. Identify who is missing and how their account might change the picture. 02 Sixfold the situation. Earth: materials, bodies, energy, upkeep. Sky: rhythms, light, seasons, time. Mortals: participants, limits, absences. Divinities / Other(s): obligations or hopes exceeding the plan. Thing: what gathers here? World: which institutions, histories, and infrastructures condition it? Thing and World work across the fourfold; they are not two extra boxes of the same kind. 03 Choose one change together. In the meeting example, try circulating questions beforehand, making space for a written response, or changing the time. Choose with participants. Name who will carry the extra work. An agreed pause or removal of an obligation can also be the intervention. 04 Make a shared form. Create a short invitation, annotated cosmogram, rearranged space, small performance, or revised practice. Agree who may see or reuse it. Let the form carry disagreements rather than forcing one story of success. 05 Return, revise, or stop. After trying it, ask what became possible, who carried a new cost, and what remained unresolved. Let the people involved decide whether to continue. A count or score can inform the discussion; it cannot replace their accounts. Keep a short return note. Record Prompt Situation What happened? Whose accounts differ? Gathering What did Sixfold reveal or miss? Change What did we agree to try, and who does the work? Return When will we meet again? What would lead us to revise or stop? Keep the note with your group. The workshop has no submission form. The Sixfold worksheet offers its existing download and print options. Read Wounded research to question your investment in the outcome, and Performance to question demands for improvement. Making cures supplements talking and taking cures through creative doing-with; its success cannot be assumed in advance."},{"url":"/workshops/making-cures/#begin","title":"Making Cures / begin","type":"Workshop","description":"Choose an ordinary shared situation and allow a pass.","keywords":["workshop","participation"],"text":"Choose an ordinary shared situation and allow a pass."},{"url":"/workshops/making-cures/#practice","title":"Making Cures / five moves","type":"Workshop","description":"Hear, sixfold, change, make, and return.","keywords":["sixfold","making cures"],"text":"Hear, sixfold, change, make, and return."},{"url":"/workshops/making-cures/#return","title":"Making Cures / return note","type":"Workshop","description":"Record disagreement, effects, responsibility, and the next return.","keywords":["reflection","non-design"],"text":"Record disagreement, effects, responsibility, and the next return."}];
